\textbf{The Neural Meta Evaluator.}
% In this section, we introduce our method to evaluate the performance of policies controlling humanoid robots.
In order to assess the quality of trajectories generated by learned policies, we introduce the % term 
\textit{Neural Meta Evaluator} (\acronym{}--see Figure \ref{fig:framework}), % to refer to 
a sequence modeling neural network designed to classify sequences of joints positions into behaviour categories. % In summary, we propose to  
We train \acronym{} on teleoperation data, with the goal of learning to classify behaviours from %expert-demonstrated trajectories.
%to learn the joint trajectories of the 
human demonstrations. 
Subsequently, we leverage \acronym{} to objectively evaluate trajectories produced by IL policies. Indeed, \acronym{} is employed 
%and then we use \acronym{}
as a \textit{meta-evaluator}, i.e. a neural network % trained independently and subsequently employed to
that evaluates trajectories generated by learned policies. {Learning a behaviour classifier such as \acronym{} allows to learn behaviour trajectories directly from data, rather than assessing only the success/failure of an action, fostering a more comprehensive IL policy evaluation.}    

Formally, let the proprioceptive joint trajectory extracted from the $i$-th demonstration be defined as $\tau_i^J = \{ j_t \}_{t=0}^{L}$, where $L$ is the time-span of the demonstration and $j_t \in \mathcal{J}$ denotes the vector of joint states at time $t$.
%
Each demonstration is associated with a behaviour label $b_i \in \mathcal{B}$ (e.g., \emph{handwave}, \emph{handshake}). \acronym{}, parameterized by $\phi$, is trained to map the proprioceptive trajectory to its corresponding behaviour label, i.e. $E_{\phi}(\tau_i^J) \rightarrow \hat{b}_i,$ where $\hat{b}_i$ is the predicted behaviour label for the trajectory.
% \mt{forse serve un smbolo per la lunghezza su cui e trainato il valutoore, ed un altro per la lunghezza di predizione del modello xbg}

%
Training is performed by minimizing the cross-entropy loss over the demonstration dataset $\mathcal{D}^J = \{\tau_1^J, \dots, \tau_N^J\}$:

\[
\hat{\phi} = \arg\min_{\phi} \, \mathbb{E}_{\tau_i^J \sim \mathcal{D}} \left[ \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{cls}} \Big( E_{\phi}(\tau_i^J), \, b_i \Big) \right],
\]

where $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{cls}}$ is the cross-entropy loss.
%%%
Once trained on teleoperation data, \acronym{} is employed as a \textit{meta-evaluator}—that is, a neural network trained independently that is subsequently used to assess the quality of trajectories generated by learned IL policies. Given a policy $\pi$ that produces a sequence of joint states $\hat{\tau}^a = \{ \hat{a}_t \}_{t=0}^{L},$ where $\hat a_t = \pi_\theta\bigl(s_{t-L+1:t}\bigr)$ represents the action predicted at time $t$,
\acronym{} predicts the behaviour associated with that trajectory as $\hat{b} = E_{\phi}(\hat{\tau}^a)$.

\textbf{Policy evaluation.} Since \acronym{} is trained to identify the behaviour in expert demonstrations at a trajectory level, % its prediction serves as an objective 
we use it as performance measure to evaluate the % of the quality of the behaviour learned 
joints trajectories predicted by the policies. 
The rationale behind \acronym{} is that the closer the trajectory produced by $\pi$ is to the characteristic patterns seen in the teleoperated data, the better its performance is evaluated.
%This trajectory-level evaluation is robust to local deviations and 
Additionally, \acronym{} allows the offline evaluation of partial sequences % by adjusting the length of the temporal window 
comparing the predicted behaviour $\hat{b}$ with the annotated behaviour label $b$, hence computing % --thus allowing to compute 
a \textit{meta}-accuracy. Moreover, the evaluation through \acronym{} is performed before deploying the model on the robot and measuring the performance with success rate, thus avoiding the need to perform model selection on the physical robot. %\mt{ thus eliminating the need for continuous deployment on the physical robot.}  
% In fact, the evaluation can be performed offline, by processing demonstrations that have not been seen by the IL method during training, and comparing the predicted behaviours class $\hat{b}$ with the ground truth behaviours label $\hat{b}$--thus allowing to compute a \textit{meta}-accuracy. 
The offline evaluation, loading the collected visual input and proprioception, implies the assumption that the policy will predict behaviours similar to the collected ones. % An assumption of \acronym{} is that the sensory input $s_t$ feeded to the robot policy is the one collected during data acquisition, and not the one corresponding to the predicted robot action \mt{Forse da spiegare meglio?: idea e' che stiamo processando un record offline, quindi l input visuale e propriocettivo non verra modificato in base ad azioni predette dal modello, ma saranno quelle del record}. 
Since the models are trained to align to the target trajectories, this does not represent a limiting assumption, as our experimental campaign shows in the following. % (please refer to Section \ref{sec:exp}). % \mt{Moreover, consider that the time span of the considered actions windows by state-of-the-art (SOTA) IL methods is limited to few tens \cite{fu2024humanplus}, preventing trajectory divergence}.
