\section{The phase portraits in the Poincar\'e disc } \label{sec4}

In this section, we study the global phase portraits for system \eqref{2d} in the Poincar\'e disc by using the theory described in Subsections \ref{ss:blowup(prel)} and \ref{ss:compactification(prel)}. 



\begin{thm}
\label{S5_th}
The global phase portraits of  \eqref{2d} in the Poincar\'e disc are topologically equivalent to one of the phase portraits of Figures \ref{fig1}, \ref{globalB} and \ref{globalC} for parameters values in \textbf{Cases A, B} and \textbf{C}, respectively.
\end{thm}

\begin{rem}
  The calculations used in the proof of Theorem \ref{S5_th} could be streamlined and simplified by using  weighted or quasi-homogeneous blow-ups. By a pedagogical point of view, we decided to do three blow-ups, which are illustrated in Figures \ref{fig1}, \ref{globalB} and \ref{globalC} for parameters values in \textbf{Cases A, B} and \textbf{C}.  Some computations in the following proof were carried out using the software \emph{Maple}. 


  \end{rem}

\begin{proof}
System \eqref{2d} in the local chart \(U_1= \{z_1>0\}\) may be written as:
\begin{align}\label{XXX}
\dot{u}=&-cu^2v^2-\frac{b}{c}uv^2-cuv^2+\frac{a}{c}v^3-\frac{1}{c}v^2+\frac{c}{3}u,\\\nonumber
\dot{v}=&-cuv^3-cv^3+\frac{c}{3}v.
\end{align}
The origin is the unique equilibrium on \(v=0\) (the points of \(\mathbb{S}^1\) in any chart have \(v= 0.\)) The eigenvalues of Jacobian matrix associated with the vector field \eqref{XXX}  are \(\dfrac{c}{3}, \dfrac{c}{3}.\) So, the origin is an unstable node (resp. stable) for \(c>0\) (resp. \(c<0\).) 

System \eqref{2d} in the local chart \(U_2\) may be written as:
\begin{align}\label{k8}
\dot{u}=&\frac{1}{c}u^2v^2-\frac{a}{c}uv^3-\frac{c}{3}u^3+\frac{b}{c}uv^2+cuv^2+cv^2,\\\nonumber
\dot{v}=&\frac{1}{c}uv^3-\frac{a}{c}v^4+\frac{b}{c}v^3.
\end{align}

The origin is an identically zero singular point. By doing the blow-up \((u,v)\rightarrow (u_1,w)\) 
where \(w=\dfrac{v_1}{u_1},\) we get
\begin{align}\label{k7}
\dot{u}_1&=-\frac{a}{c}{u_1}^4{v_1}^3
+\frac{1}{c}{u_1}^4{v_1}^2+c{u_1}^3{v_1}^2
+\frac{b}{c}{u_1}^3{v_1}^2+c{u_1}^2{v_1}^2
-\frac{c}{3}{u_1}^3,\\\nonumber
\dot{v}_1&=-c{u_1}^2{v_1}^3-c u_1 {v_1}^3+\frac{c}{3} {u_1}^2 {v_1}. 
\end{align}
By removing the common factor \(u_1,\) we have
\begin{align}\label{k6}
\dot{u}_1&=-\frac{a}{c}{u_1}^3{v_1}^3
+\frac{1}{c}{u_1}^3{v_1}^2+c{u_1}^2{v_1}^2
+\frac{b}{c}{u_1}^2{v_1}^2+c{u_1}{v_1}^2
-\frac{c}{3}{u_1}^2,\\\nonumber
\dot{v}_1&=-c{u_1}{v_1}^3-c {v_1}^3+\frac{c}{3} {u_1} {v_1}.
\end{align}
The origin is an identically zero singular point again. For studying the local phase portrait at the origin, we do another blow-up (see \S \ref{ss:blowup(prel)} of the present paper).  The characteristic direction at the origin is the real factors of \(\frac{2c}{3} {u_1}^2 v_1.\) Then the vertical axis \(u_1 = 0\) is a characteristic direction. So,
we translate the direction \(u_1 = 0\) to the
direction \(u_1 = v_1\) doing the change of variables 
\((u_1,v_1)=(u_2-v_2, v_2).\) Then, the new system is given by
\begin{align}\label{k5}
\dot{u_2}=&\dfrac{1}{3c}\Big(3 c^2 u_2 v_2- c^2 u_2^2  - 2 c^2 v_2^2 + 3 c^2 u2 v_2^2 + 3 b u_2^2 v_2^2+3 c^2 u_2^2 v_2^2 + 3 u_2^3 v_2^2\\\nonumber
&\qquad- 6 c^2 v_2^3-6 b u_2 v_2^3- 9 c^2 u_2 v_2^3 - 9 u_2^2 v_2^3-3 a u_2^3 v_2^3 + 3 b v_2^4
 + 6 c^2 v_2^4 \\\nonumber
&\qquad+ 9 u_2 v_2^4 + 9 a u_2^2 v_2^4 - 3 v_2^5 - 9 a u_2 v_2^5 + 3 a v_2^6\Big),\\\nonumber
\dot{v_2}=&\dfrac{c}{3}v_2 \left(u_2 - v_2 - 3 v_2^2 - 3 u_2 v_2^2+3 v_2^3\right).
\end{align}
The origin is the only singular point of system above  on the line \(u_2 = 0,\) and since the
linear part is identically zero, we do the vertical blow-up
\((u_2,v_2)\rightarrow (u_3,w)\)  where \(w=\dfrac{v_3}{u_3},\) and we have
\begin{align}\nonumber
\dot{u}_3=&\dfrac{1}{3c} u_3^2 \Big(3 c^2 v_3-c^2- 2 c^2 v_3^2 + 3 c^2 u_3 v_3^2 + 3 b u_3^2 v_3^2 + 3 c^2 u_3^2 v_3^2 + 3 u_3^3 v_3^2 -6 c^2 u_3 v_3^3 \\\nonumber
&\qquad\,- 6 b u_3^2 v_3^3 - 9 c^2 u_3^2 v_3^3 - 9 u_3^3 v_3^3 - 3 a u_3^4 v_3^3 + 3 b u_3^2 v_3^4+6 c^2 u_3^2 v_3^4 + 9 u_3^3 v_3^4\\\label{k4}
&\qquad\,+ 9 a u_3^4 v_3^4 - 3 u_3^3 v_3^5 
- 9 a u_3^4 v_3^5 + 3 a u_3^4 v_3^6\Big),\\\nonumber
\dot{v}_3=&\dfrac{1}{3c} u_3 v_3 (1-v_3) \Big(2 c^2 - 2 c^2 v_3 - 6 c^2 u_3 v_3^2 - 3 b u_3^2 v_3^2 - 6 c^2 u_3^2 v_3^2-3 u_3^3 v_3^2+3 b u_3^2 v_3^3\\\nonumber
&\hspace{2.8cm}+ 6 c^2 u_3^2 v_3^3 + 6 u_3^3 v_3^3
+ 3 a u_3^4 v_3^3 - 3 u_3^3 v_3^4 - 6 a u_3^4 v_3^4 + 3 a u_3^4 v_3^5\Big).
\end{align}

Eliminating the common factor \(u_3\) leads to
\begin{align}\nonumber
\dot{u}_3=&\dfrac{1}{3c} u_3 \Big(3 c^2 v_3-c^2- 2 c^2 v_3^2 + 3 c^2 u_3 v_3^2 + 3 b u_3^2 v_3^2 + 3 c^2 u_3^2 v_3^2 + 3 u_3^3 v_3^2 -6 c^2 u_3 v_3^3 \\\nonumber
&\qquad\,- 6 b u_3^2 v_3^3 - 9 c^2 u_3^2 v_3^3 - 9 u_3^3 v_3^3 - 3 a u_3^4 v_3^3 + 3 b u_3^2 v_3^4+6 c^2 u_3^2 v_3^4 + 9 u_3^3 v_3^4\\\label{eq3}
&\qquad\,+ 9 a u_3^4 v_3^4 - 3 u_3^3 v_3^5 
- 9 a u_3^4 v_3^5 + 3 a u_3^4 v_3^6\Big),\\\nonumber
\dot{v}_3=&\dfrac{1}{3c} v_3 (1-v_3) \Big(2 c^2 - 2 c^2 v_3 - 6 c^2 u_3 v_3^2 - 3 b u_3^2 v_3^2 - 6 c^2 u_3^2 v_3^2-3 u_3^3 v_3^2+3 b u_3^2 v_3^3\\\nonumber
&\hspace{2.8cm}+ 6 c^2 u_3^2 v_3^3 + 6 u_3^3 v_3^3
+ 3 a u_3^4 v_3^3 - 3 u_3^3 v_3^4 - 6 a u_3^4 v_3^4 + 3 a u_3^4 v_3^5\Big).
\end{align}
This system has two singular points on the line \(u_3 = 0,\) namely \(\tilde{E}_1: (0,0),\) and \(\tilde{E}_2: (0,1).\) 
The eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix associated with the vector field \eqref{eq3} at \((0,0)\) are \(-\dfrac{c}{3}, \dfrac{2c}{3}.\) Hence, \(\tilde{E}_1\) is
a saddle point.  
The linear part of the system at \(\tilde{E}_2\) is identically zero. Therefore, in order to know the local phase portrait around this point, we must do blow-up.
At first,  we translate the singular point \(\tilde{E}_2\) at the origin by doing the change of variables \((u_3,v_3)=(u_4, 1+v_4).\)
System \eqref{eq3} in the new variables  becomes
\begin{align}\nonumber
\dot{u}_4=&\dfrac{1}{3c} u_4 \Big(-3 c^2 u_4 - c^2 v_4 - 12 c^2 u_4 v_4 + 3 c^2 u_4^2 v_4
- 2 c^2 v_4^2-15 c^2 u_4 v_4^2 + 3 b u_4^2 v_4^2\\\nonumber
&\qquad\quad  +12 c^2 u_4^2 v_4^2-6 c^2 u_4 v_4^3+6 b u_4^2 v_4^3 + 15 c^2 u_4^2 v_4^3
 - 3 u_43 v_4^3 + 3 a u_4^4 v_4^3\\\nonumber
&\qquad\quad +3 b u_4^2 v_4^4 +6 c^2 u_4^2 v_4^4 - 6 u_4^3 v_4^4+ 9 a u_4^4 v_4^4 -3 u_4^3 v_4^5+ 9 a u_4^4 v_4^5 + 3 a u_4^4 v_4^6\Big),\\\nonumber
\dot{u}_4=&-\dfrac{1}{3c} v_4 (1+v_4)\Big(-6 c^2 u_4 - 2 c^2 v_4 - 12 c^2 u_4 v_4
+ 3 b u_4^2 v_4 + 6 c^2 u_4^2 v_4 -6 c^2 u_4 v_4^2 \\\nonumber
&\qquad\qquad\qquad\quad +6 b u_4^2 v_4^2 + 12 c^2 u_4^2 v_4^2 - 3 u_4^3 v_4^2 +3 a u_4^4 v_4^2+ 3 b u_4^2 v_4^3+6 c^2 u_4^2 v_4^3 \\\label{eq4}
&\hspace{3cm}- 6 u_4^3 v_4^3 +9 a u_4^4 v_4^3 - 3 u_4^3 v_4^4 
+9 a u_4^4 v_4^4 + 3 a u_4^4 v_4^5\Big).
\end{align}

The origin is the unique singular point of system above on the line \(u_4=0\) and furthermore, the characteristic direction at the origin of system above is \(u_4 = 0.\) Hence, by using the change of variables \((u_4,v_4)=(u_5-v_5,v_5),\) we translate the direction to \(u_5 = v_5,\) and we have
\begin{align}\nonumber
-3c\dot{u_5}=&3{u_5}^4{v_5}^5-15{u_5}^3{v_5}^6+27{u_5}^2{v_5}^7-21{u_5}{v_5}^8+6{v_5}^9
+6{u_5}^4{v_5}^4-33{u_5}^3{v_5}^5\\\nonumber
&+63{u_5}^2{v_5}^6-51{u_5}{v_5}^7
+15{v_5}^8+3{u_5}^4{v_5}^3-21{u_5}^3{v_5}^4-6c^2{u_5}^3{v_5}^4\\\nonumber
&-3b{u_5}^3{v_5}^4+12b{u_5}^2{v_5}^5
+45{u_5}^2{v_5}^5+24c^2{u_5}^2{v_5}^5-15b{u_5}{v_5}^6-30c^2{u_5}{v_5}^6\\\nonumber
&-39{u_5}{v_5}^6
+12c^2{v_5}^7+6b{v_5}^7+12{v_5}^7-3{u_5}^3{v_5}^3
-15c^2{u_5}^3{v_5}^3-6b{u_5}^3{v_5}^3\\\nonumber
&+9{u_5}^2{v_5}^4+27b{u_5}^2{v_5}^4
+63c^2{u_5}^2{v_5}^4-36b{u_5}{v_5}^5-9{u_5}{v_5}^5-81c^2{u_5}{v_5}^5\\\nonumber
&+33c^2{v_5}^6+15b{v_5}^6+3{v_5}^6-3b{u_5}^3{v_5}^2-12c^2{u_5}^3{v_5}^2
+18b{u_5}^2{v_5}^3-27b{u_5}{v_5}^4\\\nonumber
&+60c^2{u_5}^2{v_5}^3-90c^2{u_5}{v_5}^4
+42c^2{v_5}^5+12b{v_5}^5-3c^2{u_5}^3{v_5}+30c^2{u_5}^2{v_5}^2\\\nonumber
&+3b{u_5}^2{v_5}^2-69c^2{u_5}{v_5}^3-6b{u_5}{v_5}^3+42c^2{v_5}^4+3b{v_5}^4+12c^2{u_5}^2{v_5}\\\label{k2}
&-40c^2{u_5}{v_5}^2+26c^2{v_5}^3+3c^2{u_5}^2-11c^2{u_5}{v_5}+6c^2{v_5}^2, \\\nonumber
-3c\dot{v_5}=& {v_5}(1+{v_5})(-3{u_5}^3{v_5}^4+9{u_5}^2{v_5}^5-9{u_5}{v_5}^6+3{v_5}^7-6{u_5}^3{v_5}^3+18{u_5}^2{v_5}^4\\\nonumber
&-18{u_5}{v_5}^5+6{v_5}^6-3{u_5}^3{v_5}^2+3b{u_5}^2{v_5}^3
+6c^2{u_5}^2{v_5}^3+9{u_5}^2{v_5}^3-6b{u_5}{v_5}^4\\\nonumber
&-12c^2{u_5}{v_5}^4-9{u_5}{v_5}^4+3b{v_5}^5+6c^2{v_5}^5
+3{v_5}^5+6b{u_5}^2{v_5}^2+12c^2{u_5}^2{v_5}^2\\\nonumber
&-24c^2{u_5}{v_5}^3-12b{u_5}{v_5}^3+12c^2{v_5}^4+6b{v_5}^4
+6c^2{u_5}^2{v_5}+3b{u_5}^2{v_5}-18c^2{u_5}{v_5}^2\\\nonumber
&-6b{u_5}{v_5}^2+12c^2{v_5}^3+3b{v_5}^3-12c^2{u_5}{v_5}+12c^2{v_5}^2-6c^2{u_5}+4c^2{v_5}).
\end{align}

Since the linear part is identically zero, we do the vertical blow-up
\((u_5,v_5)=(u_6, u_6 v_6),\) for investigating the local phase portrait. The new system writes (applying the blow-up):
\begin{align}\nonumber
-3c\dot{u}_6=&{u_6}^2 (3 c^2 - 11 c^2 {v_6} + 12 c^2 {u_6} {v_6} - 3 c^2 {u_6}^2 {v_6} 
+ 6 c^2 {v_6}^2 - 40 c^2 {u_6} {v_6}^2 + 3 b {u_6}^2 {v_6}^2\\\nonumber
&+ 30 c^2 {u_6}^2 {v_6}^2 - 3 b {u_6}^3 {v_6}^2 - 12 c^2 {u_6}^3 {v_6}^2
+ 26 c^2 {u_6} {v_6}^3-6 b {u_6}^2 {v_6}^3 - 69 c^2 {u_6}^2 {v_6}^3 \\\nonumber
&+18 b {u_6}^3 {v_6}^3+60 c^2 {u_6}^3 {v_6}^3 - 3 {u_6}^4 {v_6}^3 
- 6 b {u_6}^4 {v_6}^3-15 c^2 {u_6}^4 {v_6}^3 + 3 {u_6}^5 {v_6}^3 \\\nonumber
&+3 b {u_6}^2 {v_6}^4 + 42 c^2 {u_6}^2 {v_6}^4 - 27 b {u_6}^3 {v_6}^4
- 90 c^2 {u_6}^3 {v_6}^4+9 {u_6}^4 {v_6}^4 + 27 b {u_6}^4 {v_6}^4\\\nonumber
&+ 63 c^2 {u_6}^4 {v_6}^4 - 21 {u_6}^5 {v_6}^4 - 3 b {u_6}^5 {v_6}^4
- 6 c^2 {u_6}^5 {v_6}^4 + 6 {u_6}^6 {v_6}^4 + 12 b {u_6}^3 {v_6}^5\\\nonumber
&+ 42 c^2 {u_6}^3 {v_6}^5 - 9 {u_6}^4 {v_6}^5 - 36 b {u_6}^4 {v_6}^5
- 81 c^2 {u_6}^4 {v_6}^5 + 45 {u_6}^5 {v_6}^5 + 12 b {u_6}^5 {v_6}^5\\\nonumber
&+24 c^2 {u_6}^5 {v_6}^5 - 33 {u_6}^6 {v_6}^5 + 3 {u_6}^7 {v_6}^5 
+ 3 {u_6}^4 {v_6}^6+15 b {u_6}^4 {v_6}^6 + 33 c^2 {u_6}^4 {v_6}^6\\\nonumber
&-39 {u_6}^5 {v_6}^6-15 b {u_6}^5 {v_6}^6 - 30 c^2 {u_6}^5 {v_6}^6
+ 63 {u_6}^6 {v_6}^6 - 15 {u_6}^7 {v_6}^6+12 {u_6}^5 {v_6}^7 \\\nonumber
&+ 6 b {u_6}^5 {v_6}^7 + 12 c^2 {u_6}^5 {v_6}^7 - 51 {u_6}^6 {v_6}^7
+27 {u_6}^7 {v_6}^7 + 15 {u_6}^6 {v_6}^8 - 21 {u_6}^7 {v_6}^8 \\\label{k1}
&+6 {u_6}^7 {v_6}^9),\\\nonumber
3c\dot{v}_6=&{u_6}  {v_6} ({v_6}-1) (-9 c^2 + 6 c^2 {v_6} - 30 c^2 {u_6} {v_6} 
+ 3 b {u_6}^2 {v_6} + 9 c^2 {u_6}^2 {v_6} + 26 c^2 {u_6} {v_6}^2\\\nonumber
&- 6 b {u_6}^2 {v_6}^2
-51 c^2 {u_6}^2 {v_6}^2 + 12 b {u_6}^3 {v_6}^2 + 30 c^2 {u_6}^3 {v_6}^2
-3 {u_6}^4 {v_6}^2 + 3 b {u_6}^2 {v_6}^3 \\\nonumber
&+ 42 c^2 {u_6}^2 {v_6}^3 - 24 b {u_6}^3 {v_6}^3
-72 c^2 {u_6}^3 {v_6}^3 + 9 {u_6}^4 {v_6}^3 + 15 b {u_6}^4 {v_6}^3
+ 33 c^2 {u_6}^4 {v_6}^3\\\nonumber
& - 12 {u_6}^5 {v_6}^3 + 12 b {u_6}^3 {v_6}^4
+42 c^2 {u_6}^3 {v_6}^4 - 9 {u_6}^4 {v_6}^4 - 30 b {u_6}^4 {v_6}^4
-66 c^2 {u_6}^4 {v_6}^4 \\\nonumber
&+ 36 {u_6}^5 {v_6}^4 + 6 b {u_6}^5 {v_6}^4
+12 c^2 {u_6}^5 {v_6}^4 - 15 {u_6}^6 {v_6}^4 + 3 {u_6}^4 {v_6}^5 + 15 b {u_6}^4 {v_6}^5\\\nonumber
&+33 c^2 {u_6}^4 {v_6}^5 - 36 {u_6}^5 {v_6}^5 - 12 b {u_6}^5 {v_6}^5
-24 c^2 {u_6}^5 {v_6}^5 + 45 {u_6}^6 {v_6}^5- 6 {u_6}^7 {v_6}^5\\\nonumber
&  + 12 {u_6}^5 {v_6}^6
+6 b {u_6}^5 {v_6}^6 + 12 c^2 {u_6}^5 {v_6}^6 - 45 {u_6}^6 {v_6}^6 + 18 {u_6}^7 {v_6}^6
+15 {u_6}^6 {v_6}^7\\\nonumber
& - 18 {u_6}^7 {v_6}^7 + 6 {u_6}^7 {v_6}^8).
\end{align}

 Now, by elimination of the common factor \(u_6,\) we obtain the truncated system
 \begin{align}\label{eq6}
\dot{u_6}=&-\dfrac{1}{3c} u_6\left(3 c^2 - 11 c^2 v_6+\mathcal{O}(|u_6,v_6|^2)\right),\\\nonumber
\dot{v_6}=&\dfrac{1}{3c} v_6 (v_6-1) \left( -9 c^2 + 6 c^2 v_6+\mathcal{O}(|u_6,v_6|^2)\right).
\end{align}
The equilibrium points for system \eqref{eq6} on the line \(u_6=0\) are given by 
\begin{align}
\tilde{E}_4=(0,0),\qquad \tilde{E}_5=(0,1), \qquad \tilde{E}_6=\left(0,\frac{3}{2}\right).
\end{align}
The eigenvalues of Jacobian matrix associated with vector field \eqref{eq6} at \(\tilde{E}_4\) and \(\tilde{E}_5\) are
$-c$, $ 3c $ and
 $\frac{2c}{3},-c$. 
 So, they are saddle points.
 On the other hand, the eigenvalues associated with Jacobian matrix at \(\tilde{E}_6\) are
$0$ and $\frac{3c}{2}$, and it is a semi-hyperbolic equilibrium. Given the center manifold \(\dot{u_6}=-\frac{b}{8c} {u_6}^3\),  
we conclude that the equilibrium point  \(\tilde{E}_6\)  is:
\begin{itemize}
\item[1.] a saddle point for
\begin{eqnarray}
\{(a,b,c) \big| b>0, \, c<0\} \cup \{(a,b,c) \big| b>0, \, c>0\}.
\end{eqnarray}
\item[2.] an unstable node for
\begin{eqnarray}
\{(a,b,c) \big| b<0, \, c<0\}.
\end{eqnarray}
\item[3.] a stable node for
\begin{eqnarray}
\{(a,b,c) \big| b<0, \, c>0\} \cup \{(a,b,c) \big| b=0\}.
\end{eqnarray}
\end{itemize}
 The final step of the proof follows from the description of Subsection \ref{ss:description}.
\end{proof} 

