\begin{abstract}
We give an abstract perspective on quadratic programming with an eye toward long portfolio theory geared toward explaining sparsity via maximum principles.
Specifically, in optimal allocation problems, we see that support of an optimal distribution
lies in a variety intersect a kind of distinguished boundary of a compact subspace to be allocated over.
We demonstrate some of its intelligence by using it to solve mazes and interpret such behavior as the underlying
space trying to understand some hypothetical platonic index for which the capital asset pricing model holds.
\end{abstract}

