\section{Functionally generated portfolios and model uncertainty}
\label{fgp-optimal-main}

Functionally Generated Portfolios (FGPs), introduced by Fernholz
\cite{fernholz1999FGP}, are central to Stochastic Portfolio Theory
(SPT), enabling the construction of self-financing stock-only
portfolios based solely on observable market weights. Their defining
feature is a pathwise decomposition of relative wealth (to be defined
precisely later), which does not require drift or volatility
estimation.

In a market with $n$ stocks, let $\mu(t)\in\Delta^{n}_{+}$ denote the
vector of strictly positive market weights at time $t\geq 0$, where
each component $\mu_{i}(t),\,i=1,\ldots, n$ represents the
capitalisation $X_{i}(t)$ of the $i^{\mathrm{th}}$ stock divided by
the total market capitalisation
$\sum_{j=1}^{n} X_{j}(t)$.\footnote{Without loss of generality, we
follow the usual convention in SPT that there is one share of each
company in the market with all stock positions infinitely divisible,
so $X_{i}(\cdot)$ also represents the $i^{\mathrm{th}}$ stock price
process.} Here, $\Delta^{n}_{+}$ denotes the interior of the positive
unit simplex:
\begin{equation*}
\Delta^{n}_{+} := \left\{a\in\mathbb{R}^{n}: \mbox{$\sum_{i=1}^{n}$, $a_{i}=1$
and $a_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,\ldots,n$}\right\}.  
\end{equation*}

Let $V^{w,\pi}(\cdot)$ be the wealth process starting from initial
capital $w>0$ when using a portfolio $\pi(t),\,t\geq 0$, where
$\pi(t)$ is a vector of proportions of wealth allocated to each stock
at time $t\geq 0$. For notational simplicity, we write
$V^{\pi}(\cdot):=V^{1,\pi}(\cdot)$, for initial capital $w=1$.  We
assume the \textit{\textbf{admissibility}} condition
\begin{equation*}
\mathbb{P}\left(V^{w,\pi}(t) \geq 0, \ \forall t \in [0,T]
\right) = 1.
\end{equation*}
With this notation, the \emph{market portfolio} corresponds to the
choice $\pi\equiv\mu$.

A portfolio $\pi$ is said to be \emph{functionally generated} if there
exists a $C^{2}$, strictly positive function $G:U\mapsto(0,\infty)$,
defined on an open neighbourhood $U\supseteq\Delta^{n}_{+}$, such that
the portfolio weights take the form
$\pi_{i}(\cdot)\equiv\pi_{i}(\mu(\cdot))$, given by
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:fgp-weights}
\pi_{i}(t) = \left(D_{i}\log(G(\mu(t))) + 1 -
\sum_{j=1}^{n}\mu_{j}(t)D_{j}\log G(\mu(t)) \right)\mu_i(t), \quad
i=1,\ldots,n, 
\end{equation}
where $D_{i}\equiv\partial/\partial x_{i}$. This prescription ensures
that the strategy is self-financing and remains within the
simplex. Some examples of FGPs are shown in Table
\ref{tab:fgp-examples}.

\begin{table*}[h]
\centering
\caption{Examples of Functionally Generated Portfolios}
\label{tab:fgp-examples}
\begin{tabular}{ccl}
\hline
\textbf{Portfolio} & \textbf{Generating Function $G(\mu)$} &
\textbf{Weights $\pi_{i}(\mu)$} \\
\hline
Equally Weighted Portfolio (EWP) & $\prod_{i=1}^{n}\mu_{i}^{1/n}$ &
$\pi_{i}=1/n$ \\
Diversity Weighted Portfolio (DWP) & $\sum_{i=1}^{n} \mu_{i}^{p}$,
$0<p<1$ & $\pi_{i}=\mu_{i}^{p}/\sum_{j}\mu_{j}^{p}$ \\
Entropy Weighted Portfolio &
$-\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mu_{i}\log\mu_{i}$ & $\propto -\log\mu_{i}$
\\ 
Market Portfolio & Constant: $G(\mu)=c>0$ & $\pi_{i}=\mu_{i}$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

In a continuous time setting, with the market capitalisations
following It\^o processes, it turns out that the \emph{relative
wealth} $V(\cdot)$ of an FGP $\pi$ relative to the market portfolio
$\mu$, defined (for the case of unit initial capital) by
\begin{equation}
V(t) := V^{\pi}(t)/V^{\mu}(t), \quad t\geq 0,  
\label{eq:rw}
\end{equation}
satisfies, over a time horizon $[0,T]$, the so-called \emph{master
equation}
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:master-fgp}
\log\left(
\frac{V^{\pi}(T)}{V^{\mu}(T)} \right) = \log\left(
\frac{G(\mu(T))}{G(\mu(0))} \right) + \int_{0}^{T}q(t)\ud t,
\end{equation}
where the drift term is given by
\begin{equation}
q(t) :=
\frac{-1}{2G(\mu(t))}\sum_{i,j=1}^{n}D^{2}_{ij}G(\mu(t))\mu_{i}(t)\mu_{j}(t)
\tau_{ij}^{\mu}(t), \quad t\geq 0,
\end{equation}
and with $\tau_{ij}^{\mu}(\cdot)$ denoting the covariance
structure of the market weights (the covariances of individual stocks
relative to the entire market), that is:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq-cov-1}
\tau_{ij}^{\mu}(t) := \langle\log\mu_{i},\log\mu_{j}\rangle(t), \quad
t\geq 0.
\end{equation}
Fernholz’s Master Formula \eqref{eq:master-fgp} (see Fernholz
\cite[Chapter~3]{fernholz2002sptbook} for a derivation), allows for
the explicit construction of portfolios with relative performance
guarantees.

The FGP weights \eqref{eq:fgp-weights} (or, indeed, the master
formula, given that \eqref{eq-cov-1} holds) shows that the wealth
process $V^{\pi}$ of an FGP relative to the market depends only on
observable quantities. This robustness makes FGPs potentially
attractive under high model uncertainty, where latent parameters such
as expected returns are difficult to estimate reliably.

\subsection{On Relative Arbitrage and Optimal Relative Arbitrage}
\label{subsec:raora}

As alluded to in the Introduction, a central objective of Stochastic
Portfolio Theory (SPT) is to construct portfolios that almost surely
outperform the market over finite horizons. This concept, known as
\emph{Relative Arbitrage (RA)}, is fundamental to the SPT framework.

\begin{definition}[Relative Arbitrage]\cite[Definition
6.1]{KARATZAS200989} \cite[(2.10) and (2.11)]{fkk2005diversity}

A portfolio $\pi_{1}(.)$ is a \textbf{\textit{relative arbitrage
(RA)}} with respect to portfolio $\pi_{2}$ over the time interval
$[0,T]$ if the associated wealth process (starting from the same
initial wealth $w$) satisfies:
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{P}(V^{w,\pi_{1}}(t) \geq V^{w,\pi_{2}}(t))=1, \text{ and }
\mathbb{P}(V^{w,\pi_{1}}(t) > V^{w,\pi_{2}}(t)) > 0.
\end{equation}
We call this a \textbf{\textit{strong relative arbitrage}} if it
satisfies:
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{P}(V^{w,\pi_{1}}(t) > V^{w,\pi_{2}}(t)) = 1.
\end{equation}

\end{definition}

Beyond constructing arbitrage, one can ask: what is the \emph{best}
way to beat the market? This leads to the notion of \emph{optimal
relative arbitrage}. As formulated by D. Fernholz and I. Karatzas in
\cite{danfernholzkaratza_optimalarbitrage_2010}, this refers to the
minimum initial capital required to outperform the market almost
surely over a finite time horizon:
\begin{equation}
u(T) := \inf\left\{w>0 : \mbox{$\exists\,\pi$ such that
$\mathbb{P}\left(V^{w,\pi}(T)\geq V^{w,\mu}(T)\right)=1$}
\right\}. 
\end{equation}
This can be reformulated as the problem of maximising, over the
portfolio choice $\pi$, the return on investment, relative to the
market, over the time horizon $[0,T]$:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:max-rel-ret}
\log \left( \frac{V^\pi(T)}{V^\mu(T)}  \right)
= \log \left({V(T)}  \right)  \to \max!
\end{equation}
where, in the case that $\pi$ is an FGP, and in a continuous-time
It\^o process setting for the market capitalisations, the relative
wealth process $V(\cdot)$ of \eqref{eq:rw} satisfies the master
formula \eqref{eq:master-fgp}.

In a complete Markovian diffusion setting, the problem of maximising
return relative to the market was studied by D. Fernholz and
I. Karatzas \cite{danfernholzkaratza_optimalarbitrage_2010} (see also
their associated book chapter
\cite{danfernholz_karatzas2010probabilistic_RA}). The best possible
relative arbitrage in this setting is characterised via the smallest
solution of a linear parabolic Cauchy problem. In particular, the
highest relative return that can be achieved over $[0,T]$ is given by
$1/U(T,\mathbf{X}(0))$, where $\mathbf{X}(0)$ is the initial
configuration of the market weight vector, and
$U(T-t,\mathbf{x}),\,t\in[0,T]$ (sometimes called the ``arbitrage
function'' by Fernholz and Karatzas) is the solution to the associated
Cauchy problem. There are also probabilistic characterisations
involving the probability, under the so-called exit measure of
F\"ollmer \cite{follmer72,follmer73} that the market weights remain in
the unit simplex.

The salient point from our perspective is that the portfolio which
realises the best return \emph{turns out to be functionally
generated} (see, for example, \cite[Remark
6]{danfernholzkaratza_optimalarbitrage_2010}) with generating function
directly related to the ``arbitrage function'' that arises as the
solution to a Cauchy problem.

The notion that FGPs emerge naturally as portfolios that can best beat
the market, and in particular when there is some form of model
uncertainty, has also emerged in other contexts, such as problems of
robust long-term growth, when the expected returns of assets are
unknown, as shown by Kardaras and Robertson \cite{kr12,kr21}.

These properties of FGPs, that they often arise as optimal solutions
to relative outperformance problems in the face of model uncertainty,
serve as motivation for the approach we adopt in this paper, namely a
neural network-based methodology to seek optimal relative return, when
the portfolio is constrained to be functionally generated. Our
approach is flexible and readily scalable to high-dimensional
settings, avoiding some of the implementation limitations of the
classical approach in \cite{danfernholzkaratza_optimalarbitrage_2010},
which would be computationally challenging in high dimensions, as it
involves solving an associated parabolic PDE system.

\subsection{From Classical to Neural FGPs} 
\label{subsec:fcnfgp}

Building on the theoretical foundation of classical FGPs, we propose a
\textbf{Neural FGP} framework that preserves the key structural
properties: self-financing evolution, pathwise decomposition (as
in~\eqref{eq:master-fgp}, with portfolio weights given
by~\eqref{eq:fgp-weights}), and direct dependence on observable
quantities, while allowing for greater flexibility in the design of
the generating function. As mentioned above, our methodology avoids the
need to solve a partial differential inequality, as in
\cite{danfernholzkaratza_optimalarbitrage_2010}.

In our framework, the generating function $G(\cdot)$ is parameterised
by a neural network trained on historical market data. This allows the
portfolio to adapt to complex, possibly nonlinear features in the
market weight dynamics, without sacrificing the interpretability and
robustness of the original FGP formulation. The Neural FGP framework
thus offers a hybrid approach that combines the theoretical rigour of
SPT with the data-adaptivity of modern machine learning.

\section{Neural Functionally Generated Portfolios (Neural FGPs)}
\label{neural-fgp-main}

We introduce a neural-network-based extension of Functionally
Generated Portfolios (FGPs), in which the generating function is
learned directly from data. This approach preserves the theoretical
structure of classical FGPs while enhancing flexibility and
adaptability to market dynamics.  To the best of our knowledge, this
extension has not appeared in the existing SPT or portfolio
optimisation literature. The following sections detail the methodology
and evaluate its performance against classical FGP benchmarks.

\subsection{Neural FGP Architecture and Training Framework}
\label{subsec:nfgpatf}

\subsubsection{Neural Network Approximation of the Generating Function.}
\label{subsubsec:nnagf}

To construct a neural FGP, we approximate the generating function
$G(\cdot)$ using a parametrised version $G_{\theta}(\cdot)$,
where $\theta$ is the learning vector in a neural network. From this
point onwards, we use $x$ in place of $\mu$ to preserve notational
consistency with standard neural network frameworks. We compute the
portfolio weights according to the classical prescription
(\ref{eq:fgp-weights}). In the current notation, we have a portfolio
weight for the $i^{\mathrm{th}}$ stock similarly parametrised by the
learning vector, and given by
\begin{equation}
\pi_{\theta,i}(x) = \left(\frac{\partial \log G_{\theta}(x)}{\partial
x_{i}} + 1 - \sum_{j}x_{j} \frac{\partial \log
G_{\theta}(x)}{\partial x_{j}} \right) x_{i}, \quad i=1,\ldots,n.
\label{eq:pithetai}
\end{equation}
This construction ensures that the resulting portfolio remains
long-only and self-financing. We employ automatic differentiation on
$\log G_{\theta}(\cdot)$ to compute the derivatives in
\eqref{eq:pithetai}, in a manner that thus mirrors the classical FGP
structure.

\subsubsection{Enforcing Concavity via Input Convex Neural Networks
  (ICNN)}
\label{subsubsec:ecicnn}

Traditional neural networks may not enforce the concavity required for
generating functions in FGPs. To resolve this, we adopt
\textit{\textbf{Input Convex Neural Networks (ICNN)}}, first
introduced in \cite{icnn_amos_2017}, and specifically designed to
generate convex outputs based on their inputs.

We ensure the concavity of the learned generating function
$G_\theta(\cdot)$ by defining it as the negative of an Input Convex
Neural Network (ICNN) output:
\begin{equation*}
G_{\theta}(x) := -f(x),  
\end{equation*}
where $f(\cdot)$ is a convex function constructed using an ICNN
architecture, defined below.

Let the network input be $x\in\mathbb{R}^{n}$, where $n$ is
the number of stocks. The ICNN defines a sequence of intermediate
variables $z_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{m_{k}}$ recursively, as follows:
\begin{align*}
z_{0} & = 0, \\
z_{1} & = \phi(W_{0}x + b_{0}), \\
z_{2} & = \phi(W_{1}z_{1} + U_{1}x + b_{1}), \\
& \vdots \notag \\
z_{K} & = \phi(W_{K-1}z_{K-1} + U_{K-1}x + b_{K-1}),
\end{align*}
with final output
\begin{equation*}
f(x) = w^\top z_K + u^\top x + c.
\end{equation*}

The full set of learnable parameters of the ICNN is denoted by
$\theta$, defined by:
\begin{equation*}
\theta := \left\{\{W_{k},U_{k},b_{k}\}_{k=0}^{K-1},\,w,u,c \right\}.  
\end{equation*}
These parameters are subject to the following architectural constraints:

\begin{itemize}

\item $W_{k} \in \mathbb{R}^{m_{k+1}\times m_{k}}$: unconstrained
weight matrices,

\item $U_{k} \in \mathbb{R}^{m_{k+1}\times n}$: weight matrices
constrained to have non-negative entries (that is, $U_{k} \geq 0$,
elementwise), 

\item $b_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{m_{k+1}}$: bias vectors for $k= 0,\ldots,K-1$, 

\item $w\in\mathbb{R}^{m_{K}}$, $u\in\mathbb{R}^{n}$,
$c\in\mathbb{R}$: output layer parameters.  

\end{itemize}

Here, $m_{k}\in\mathbb{N}$ denotes the width of the $k^{\mathrm{th}}$
hidden layer, and $\phi(\cdot)$ is a convex and non-decreasing activation
function (for example, \texttt{Softplus} or ReLU). These structural
constraints ensure that $f(x)$ is convex in \( x \), and thus that
$G_{\theta}(x)$ is concave.

\subsubsection{Training Objective.}
\label{subsubsec:to}

We train the model to maximise the logarithmic return on investment
relative to the market, that is, the objective in
~\eqref{eq:max-rel-ret}. Let $x_{i}(s)$ denote the \emph{relative}
capitalisation of the $i^{\mathrm{th}}$ stock at time step $s$, that
is, the capitalisation of stock $i$ stock divided by the total market
capitalisation. For notational convenience, we denote this as
$x_{s,i}$, and similarly denote the relative wealth process by
$V_{t}\equiv V(t)$. The discrete-time evolution of the
relative wealth process $V$ is given by
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:rel-wealth-discrete}
V_{t} =\prod_{s=1}^{t}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\pi_{i}(x_{s-1})\frac{x_{s,i}}{x_{s-1,i}},
\end{equation}
with $\pi_{i}(\cdot)$
defined in~\eqref{eq:fgp-weights} (with its corresponding
neural-vector parametrised form as in \eqref{eq:pithetai}).

We optimise the \emph{logarithmic return relative to the market},
given by $\log V_{T}$, the logarithm of relative wealth at the
terminal time $T$. This objective is consistent with the
continuous-time formulation~\eqref{eq:max-rel-ret}.

The objective is recast as a loss function, incorporating an
$\ell_{2}$ regularisation term to discourage overly concentrated
portfolios and promote numerical stability during training:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:loss-func1}
\mathcal{L}(\theta) = -\frac{1}{T}\log(V_T) + \lambda \|\pi(x)\|_{2}^{}.
\end{equation}
This discrete-time objective mirrors the continuous-time formulation
associated with optimal relative arbitrage discussed in Section
\ref{fgp-optimal-main}, ensuring theoretical consistency in our
learning framework.

% \paragraph*{All Stocks, No Cash.}

% We assume a fully invested portfolio with no allocation to cash or any
% risk-free asset. That is, the entire wealth is distributed across the
% available risky assets (in our case, stocks) at all times. This is
% consistent with the definition of FGPs as stock-only portfolios.

%Cannot use environment remark

\begin{remark}[ICNN versus a Hessian Penalty]
\label{rem:icnnvshp}

To encourage concavity in the function $G(\cdot)=-f(\cdot)$, another
possible approach would involve adding a \textbf{Hessian penalty} to the
loss function, which discourages regions of positive curvature by
penalising the positive eigenvalues of the Hessian of $G_{\theta}(\cdot)$.
This would yield a total loss of the form
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{\text{total}}(\theta) = \mathcal{L}(\theta) + \lambda
\cdot \sum_{i=1}^{n} \max\left(\lambda_{i}^{(x)},0\right)^{2}, 
\end{equation}
where $\lambda_{i}^{(x)}$ are the eigenvalues of the Hessian at
input $x$.\footnote{Recall that a function is concave if the
Hessian is negative semidefinite, that is, all eigenvalues are
non-positive.}

While this method has a sound theoretical basis, it is numerically
unstable and computationally inefficient. Moreover, it only encourages
concavity rather than enforcing it and requires careful tuning of the
regularisation parameter $\lambda$. In contrast, Input Convex
Neural Networks (ICNNs) impose convexity by architectural design,
thereby ensuring that $G(\cdot)$ is concave by construction.

\end{remark}

\subsection{Implementation and Experiments}
\label{subsec:ie}

\subsubsection{Simulation Workflow and Implementation Details}
\label{subsubsec:swid}

To evaluate the performance of the Neural FGP with ICNN, we conduct
simulations on both synthetic and real-world datasets. For synthetic
data, we generate geometric Brownian motion (GBM) paths for asset
prices and normalise them to compute market weights. For real data, we
fetch historical prices from Yahoo Finance. The neural network is
trained via back-propagation using these inputs, and we derive the
resulting portfolio weights through the gradient of the learned
generating function. The procedure involves the following steps:

\begin{enumerate}

\item \textbf{Data type and input configuration.}  

The user specifies whether to use real equity market data or synthetic
geometric Brownian motion (GBM) paths. The following input parameters
are defined:

\begin{itemize}

\item \texttt{use\_real}: Boolean flag indicating real vs synthetic data

\item \texttt{n}: Number of assets in the portfolio

\item \texttt{y}: Time window (in years) of historical data (if using
real data) 

\item \texttt{p\_vals}: Exponents used for DWP benchmark comparisons

\end{itemize}

\item \textbf{Data loading and normalisation.}  

For synthetic data, we simulate GBM price paths with randomly sampled
drifts and volatilities over $1000$ days. For real data, we fetch
daily adjusted closing prices for the selected tickers (e.g., AAPL,
MSFT, GOOG, AMZN, META) over the past $y= 5$ years via Yahoo Finance,
ending on Friday, 11 April 2025. In both cases, the price series is
normalised at each time step to compute the market weight vector
$x_{t}\in\Delta^{n}_{+}$. This normalisation implicitly sets the
market portfolio as the num\'eraire; that is, each asset's
capitalisation is divided by total market capitalisation at each time.

\item \textbf{Model architecture and portfolio rule.}  

An Input Convex Neural Network (ICNN) is initialised with input
dimension equal to the number of assets. The model outputs a convex
function $G(x)$, and portfolio weights are computed via the gradient
$\nabla\log G(x)$, following the structure of functionally generated
portfolios (FGPs). The gradient is projected onto the simplex and
scaled for numerical stability.

\item \textbf{Training and optimisation.}  

The ICNN is trained via back-propagation to maximise logarithmic
portfolio returns relative to the market over a window of $200$
trading days. A regularisation term penalising extreme weight
concentration is included to encourage robustness. The training
objective is given by the loss function in~\eqref{eq:loss-func1}, and
the optimisation is carried out using the Adam optimiser.

\item \textbf{Walk-forward evaluation.}  

A sliding window approach is used: for each $200$-day training
segment, we evaluate the trained model on the subsequent 20-day
out-of-sample period. This walk-forward setup mimics realistic
deployment by retraining on rolling windows and helps mitigate
over-fitting.

\end{enumerate}

\subsubsection{Benchmark Strategies and Evaluation}
\label{subsubsec:bse}

To evaluate the performance of the neural FGP portfolio, we compare it
against three classical FGP benchmark strategies:

\begin{itemize}

\item \textbf{Equally-Weighted Portfolio (EWP):} assigns equal weights
to each asset, that is, $\pi^{\text{equal}}_{i}=1/n$

\item \textbf{Market Portfolio (MP):} invests proportionally to market
weights, that is, $\pi^{\text{market}}(x)= x$

\item \textbf{Diversity-Weighted Portfolio (DWP):} defined as
$\pi^{\text{DWP}}_{i}(x)=x_{i}^{p}/\sum_{j}x_{j}^{p}$, with
$p=0.3,0.5,0.8$  

\end{itemize}

We track the relative wealth evolution $V$ as defined in
\eqref{eq:rel-wealth-discrete} for each portfolio strategy using the
walk-forward setup described earlier. Specifically, the model is
retrained every $200$ trading days and evaluated on the subsequent
$20$-day out-of-sample window. For each test window, $V_{T}$ denotes
the terminal relative wealth at the end of that $20$-day period, which
we denote by $V_{T_{k}}$ for the $k^{\mathrm{th}}$ evaluation.

The resulting trajectories of $V_{T_{k}}$ across all walk-forward
windows are shown in Figure~\ref{fig:cum-wealth-synthetic-log-icnn}
(synthetic data) and Figure~\ref{fig:cum-wealth-real-log-icnn} (real
data). Since we explicitly normalise by the market portfolio,
$V_{T_{k}}=V^{\pi}(T_{k})/V^{\mu}(T_{k})$ captures portfolio
performance \emph{relative to the market} at time $T_{k}$.

In both settings, the Neural FGP consistently outperforms all
benchmark strategies across nearly all evaluation periods. We report
the average of the logarithmic relative return across all test windows
in Table~\ref{tab:perf-icnn-simulated-log} (synthetic data) and
Table~\ref{tab:perf-icnn-real-log} (real data). \textit{Note that this
is not a time-average over each $20$-day testing window, but rather an
average of terminal relative log-returns
$\log(V_{T_{1}}),\log(V_{T_{2}}),\dots,\log(V_{T_{K}})$, where each
$T_{k}$ denotes the end of the $k^{\mathrm{th}}$ $20$-day test
period. The quantity reported is the average terminal log-relative
return $(1/K)\sum_{k=1}^{K}\log(V_{T_{k}})$. For the simulated
dataset, we have $K=(1000-220)/20=39$, and for the real dataset,
$K=(252\times 5 - 220)/20= 52$.}

