Before reaching the pebble isolation mass (horizontal dashed line), the protoplanet's mass increases mainly via pebble accretion ({\it core growth phase}). Since the rapid pebble accretion brings energy large enough to support the hydrostatic condition, the protoplanet's envelope doesn't contract. The gas accretion rate is much lower than the solid accretion rate and is controlled by the expansion of the accretion radius $R_\mathrm{gas,acc}$. 
%, namely by the pebble accretion rate. 
Once the protoplanet reaches pebble isolation mass, the pebble accretion stops, and the envelope begins to contract. The cooling timescale controls the gas accretion rate ({\it contraction phase}). Since the cooling timescale depends on the atmospheric opacity, the gas accretion rate is shorter for the lower grain opacity factors. 

