	\title{Optimal Ratcheting of Dividends with Irreversible Reinsurance}
	\author{Tim J. Boonen
	\thanks{Department of Statistics and Actuarial Science, The University of Hong Kong, Hong Kong, China. Email: tjboonen@hku.hk}
	\and
	Engel John C. Dela Vega
	\thanks{Department of Statistics and Actuarial Science, The University of Hong Kong, Hong Kong, China. Email: ejdv@hku.hk}}
	\date{}
	\maketitle

	\begin{abstract}
		This paper considers an insurance company that faces two key constraints: a ratcheting dividend constraint and an irreversible reinsurance constraint. The company allocates part of its reserve to pay dividends to its shareholders while strategically purchasing reinsurance for its claims. The ratcheting dividend constraint ensures that dividend cuts are prohibited at any time. The irreversible reinsurance constraint ensures that reinsurance contracts cannot be prematurely terminated or sold to external entities. The dividend rate level and the reinsurance level are modelled as nondecreasing processes, thereby satisfying the constraints. The incurred claims are modelled via a Brownian risk model. The main objective is to maximize the cumulative expected discounted dividend payouts until the time of ruin. The reinsurance and dividend levels belong to either a finite set or a closed interval. The optimal value functions for the finite set case and the closed interval case are proved to be the unique viscosity solutions of the corresponding Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman equations, and the convergence between these optimal value functions is established. For the finite set case, a threshold strategy is proved to be optimal, while for the closed interval case, an $\epsilon$-optimal strategy is constructed. Finally, numerical examples are presented to illustrate the optimality conditions and optimal strategies.
	\end{abstract}

        \noindent\textbf{Keywords}: optimal dividends, ratcheting dividends, irreversible reinsurance, viscosity solutions, HJB equations, stochastic optimal control.

\bigskip
\noindent\textbf{AMS MSC2020}: 91G05, 49L25, 35F21, 93E20

	\section{Introduction}

	The optimal dividend payout problem is a fundamental topic in the field of actuarial science. The foundations of this topic were laid in the seminal paper by \cite{definetti1957}, which explored the optimal way to distribute dividends to a company's shareholders. The key insight is that the optimal dividend payout strategy should be the one that maximizes the expected discounted sum of all dividends paid to shareholders up until the company's time of bankruptcy, also called the ruin time. 

	This optimization problem is essential and critical for financial institutions, such as insurance companies and banks, that need to establish balance between the interests of shareholders through dividend income and the level of reserves to cover potential future liabilities. As such, the work in \cite{definetti1957} has produced several variations and extensions of the optimal dividend payout problem. An overview of techniques and strategies for solving optimal dividend payout problems under the framework of collective risk theory is presented in \cite{albrecher2009} and  \cite{avanzi2009}.

	The application of control techniques, such as the concept of viscosity solutions and the Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman (HJB) approach, in solving optimal dividend payout problems is discussed in \cite{schmidli:controlinsurance} and \cite{azcuebook2014}. The case where the dividend rate can be unbounded is also discussed in \cite{schmidli:controlinsurance}. The reserve process is modelled commonly via the classical risk model, sometimes referred to as the Cram\'er-Lundberg model, or the diffusion approximation model. Jump-diffusion processes have also been used to model the reserve process such as in \cite{belhaj2010}.

	The term \emph{ratcheting} is first used in the context of optimal dynamic consumption and investment problems \citep{duesenberry:consumption,dybvig1995}, wherein consumption is not allowed to fall over time and increases proportionately as the wealth reaches a new threshold or maximum. The dividend ratcheting constraint is motivated by practitioners' observations and the psychological behaviour that shareholders are likely to be unhappy about a reduction in the dividend payments, as discussed in \cite{avanzi2016} for the case of insurance companies. The positive and significant share price response of insurers to dividend increases is discussed in \cite{akhigbe1993}.

	The first time that the dividend ratcheting constraint was introduced in the context of optimal dividend payout problems is in \cite{albrecher2018}, where the dividend rate can be increased only once and must not exceed the drift rate of the original surplus process under the L\'evy risk model and the Brownian risk model. The general case of the ratcheting constraint, where the dividend is allowed to increase more than once during the lifetime of the process, is discussed in \cite{albrecher2020} under the compound Poisson process and in \cite{albrecher2022} under the Brownian risk model. There is more regularity present in the value function under the Brownian risk model; hence, an explicit differential equation was derived and used to identify the candidates for the optimal strategies in the latter work. Some extensions and variations include a habit-formation (or drawdown) constraint with ratcheting dividends as in \cite{angoshtari2019}, spectrally negative Lévy risk models (SNLRMs) until drawdown time as in \cite{zhang2020}, and periodic dividend payout with ratcheting as in \cite{song2023} and \cite{sun2024}. 

%	The inclusion of the ratcheting constraint produces a two-dimensional stochastic control problem. One-dimensional control problems include \cite{hojgaard1999} that solves an optimal dividend payout problem under the Brownian risk model. Two-dimensional stochastic control problems in the context of optimal dividend payout include \cite{albrecher2017}, \cite{gu2018}, \cite{grandits2019eaj}, \cite{grandits2019saj}, and \cite{azcue2019}, which study collaboration between two insurance companies. 
%	
%	A multi-dimensional stochastic control problem is even more difficult to handle. Some multi-dimensional stochastic control problem include \cite{tian2020} wherein the time variable is considered as a separate dimension through a process defined for the time elapsed since the last jump time.

	\cite{chang1996} argue that reinsurance negotiations are expensive, time-consuming, and irreversible unlike insurance futures and call spreads, which are standardized and exchange-traded. The irreversible reinsurance contracts is motivated by problems such as the finite-fuel problem (e.g. \cite{bank2005}), the irreversible investment problem (e.g. \cite{bertola1994}), and the irreversible liquidation problem (e.g. \cite{ferrari2021}), all of which employ techniques for singular control problems. \cite{yan2022} formulate the irreversible reinsurance problem using a singular control problem under a mean-reverting risk exposure process. Their objective function consists of a running cost function generated by the risk exposure and the cost of purchasing new reinsurance contracts. \cite{brachetta2021} propose an optimal reinsurance problem, wherein a reinsurance contract with some fixed cost is purchased and simultaneously the risk retention level is decided at some random time before maturity. \cite{federico2024} study the minimization of the flow of capital injections while considering purchasing perpetual reinsurance contracts at certain random times.

	The optimal dividend payout with proportional reinsurance problem under the diffusion approximation without the ratcheting dividend constraint and the irreversible reinsurance constraint was first studied in \cite{hojgaard1999}. Dynamic reinsurance with dividend payout problems have also been discussed in \cite{asmussen2000}, wherein excess-of-loss reinsurance is considered. Impulse control has been used in solving optimal dividend payout with proportional reinsurance policies, such as in \cite{wei2010}, where regime-switching was also introduced.

	In this work, we consider the problem of a company that wants to optimize dividend payouts subject to a ratcheting dividend constraint and an irreversible reinsurance constraint. This extends the work of \cite{albrecher2022} by introducing the irreversible constraint, leading to a multi-dimensional stochastic control problem. The rate at which the company pays dividends is modelled as a nondecreasing process. The level of risk retained by the company is modelled as a nonincreasing process. The level of reinsurance is then a nondecreasing process. We use proportional reinsurance for this work.

	The discussion begins with the reserve process modelled via the classical Cram\'er-Lundberg model. We assume that the company's premium rate is computed using the expected value principle and that the company's incoming claims are reinsured using proportional reinsurance. We use the result of \cite{grandell1977} to obtain a diffusion approximation of the reserve process with proportional reinsurance. The dynamics of the controlled reserve process is then introduced, which is essentially the diffusion approximation of the reserve process deducted by the dividend  rate process. The goal is then to maximize the expected discounted dividends paid until ruin time.   

	The dynamic programming approach is then used to obtain the HJB equations. We start with the case of constant dividend and reinsurance levels, which yields a characteristic equation that motivates the form of the candidate value function. This is followed by the analysis of the case where the dividend and reinsurance levels are allowed to vary in a closed interval. Here we introduce viscosity solutions since the candidate value function may not be twice continuously differentiable. We then discuss the HJB equation for the case where the dividend and reinsurance levels belong to some finite sets. The convergence of optimal value functions from the finite set case to the closed interval case is then proved. 

	The optimal strategy for the finite set case is constructed by determining the appropriate threshold levels for the dividend and reinsurance variables. The order in which these levels are changed must be carefully analyzed, which is one of the main differences with the work of \cite{albrecher2022}. To determine the optimal strategy for the closed interval case, we first discuss the curve strategies. The strategies are constructed using the optimal threshold levels obtained in the finite set case. These strategies lead to two differential equations depending on whether the reinsurance level or the dividend level is increased first. We then obtain a threshold strategy for the closed interval case and prove that it is $\epsilon$-optimal under specific conditions.

	The paper is organized as follows. Section \ref{section:model} discusses the model for the reserve process and the properties of the value function. Section \ref{section:hjb} discusses the HJB equations and the corresponding viscosity solutions. The convergence of the optimal value functions is proved in Section \ref{section:convergence}. The optimal strategies are discussed in Section \ref{section:optimalstrat}. Some numerical examples are presented in Section \ref{section:examples}. Section \ref{section:conc} concludes. Appendix \ref{section:appendix} provides the proofs of our results.



	\section{Model}\label{section:model}

	Consider a complete probability space $(\Omega,\mathcal{F},\mathbb{F},\mathbb{P})$, where $\mathbb{F}:=\{\mathcal{F}_t\}_{t\geq 0}$ is the $\mathbb{P}$-augmentation of the natural filtration $\{\mathcal{F}_t^W\}_{t\geq 0}$ generated by a standard Brownian motion $\{W_t\}_{t\geq 0}$. We consider an insurance company that simultaneously uses part of the reserve to pay dividends to its shareholders and develop a strategic plan to reinsure some of its claims.

	Suppose that the company's reserve process follows the classical Cramér-Lundberg (CL) model given by
	\begin{align*}
		R_t=x+pt-\sum_{i=1}^{N_t}Z_i,
	\end{align*}
	where $x>0$ is the initial reserve, $p>0$ is the premium rate on the risk that the company is insuring, $\{N_t\}_{t\geq 0}$ is a Poisson process modelling claim frequency with intensity parameter $\lambda>0$, and $\{Z_i\}_{i=1,2,\ldots}$ is a series of positive-valued, independent and identically distributed (i.i.d.) random variables with $Z_i$ denoting the size of the $i$th loss. We assume that $\{N_t\}_{t\geq 0}$ and $\{Z_i\}_{i=1,2,\ldots}$ are independent under $\mathbb{P}$. We further suppose that the i.i.d random variables $Z_1,Z_2,\ldots$ have a common distribution with finite mean $\mu_0$ and finite second moment $\sigma_0^2$.

	We assume that the company's premium rate $p$ is calculated using the expected value principle with relative safety loading $\gamma>0$, that is, $p=(1+\gamma)\lambda\mu_0$. We further assume that the claims are reinsured by proportional reinsurance with retention level $A\in[0,1]$. For a claim $Z_i$, the company pays $AZ_i$ and the reinsurer pays $(1-A)Z_i$. Suppose that the reinsurance premium $p^A$ is calculated using the expected value principle with relative safety loading $\theta>0$, that is, $p^A=(1+\theta)(1-A)\lambda\mu_0$. The reserve process with proportional reinsurance, denoted by $R^A$, can then be written as
	\begin{align*}
		R^A_t&=x+(p-p^A)t-A\sum_{i=1}^{N_t}Z_i=x+\left[A(1+\theta)-(\theta-\gamma)\right]\lambda\mu_0t-A\sum_{i=1}^{N_t}Z_i.
	\end{align*}
	The company can then choose a reinsurance strategy $A=\{A(t)\}_{t\geq 0}$.
	Following \cite{grandell1977}, the diffusion approximation of the reserve process, denoted by $X=\{X_t\}_{t\geq 0}$ is given by
	\begin{align*}
		X_t=x+\int_0^t\left[\mu A(s)-b\right]ds+\int_0^t\sigma A(s)dW_s,
	\end{align*}
	where $\mu:=\theta\lambda\mu_0>0$, $b:=(\theta-\gamma)\lambda\mu_0$, and $\sigma:=\sqrt{\lambda\sigma_0^2}>0$.

	\begin{myrem}
		The above form for the diffusion approximation of the reserve process also works for the following premium principles:
		\begin{itemize}
			\item Standard Deviation Premium Principle: $p=\lambda\mu_0+\gamma\sqrt{\lambda\sigma_0^2}$ and $p^A=\lambda(1-A)\mu_0+\theta(1-A)\sqrt{\lambda\sigma_0^2}$ implies $\mu=\theta\sqrt{\lambda\sigma^2_0}$, $b=(\theta-\gamma)\sqrt{\lambda\sigma_0^2}$, and $\sigma=\sqrt{\lambda\sigma_0^2}$.
			\item Modified Variance Principle: $p=\lambda\mu_0+\gamma\frac{\sigma_0^2}{\mu_0}$ and $p^A=\lambda(1-A)\mu_0+\theta(1-A)\frac{\sigma_0^2}{\mu_0}$ implies $\mu=\theta\frac{\sigma_0^2}{\mu_0}$, $b=(\theta-\gamma)\frac{\sigma_0^2}{\mu_0}$, and $\sigma=\sqrt{\lambda\sigma_0^2}$.
		\end{itemize}
	\end{myrem}

	Let $C(t)$ be the rate at which the company pays dividends at time $t$. When applying the policy $\pi:=(A,C)$, let $\{X_t^{\pi}\}_{t\geq 0}$ be the controlled reserve process. The dynamics for $X_t^{\pi}$ can then be written as
	\begin{align*}
		\begin{cases}
			dX_t^{\pi}=\left[\mu A(t)-b\right]dt+\sigma A(t)dW_t-C(t)dt,\\
			X_0^{\pi}=x.
		\end{cases}
	\end{align*}

	Suppose that the proportion of the risk retained by the insurer belongs in a set $\mathcal{A}\subseteq [0,1]$ while the dividend rates belong in a set $\mc{C}\subseteq[0,\ol{c}]$, where $0\leq \ol{c}\in\mc{C}$ is the maximum dividend rate possible. Given an initial surplus $x\geq 0$, a maximum initial proportion $A(0)=a\in \mathcal{A}$, and a minimum initial divided rate $C(0)=c\in\mathcal{C}$, the policy $\pi$ is said to be admissible if
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item[(i)] the processes $A:=\{A(t)\}_{t\geq 0}$ and $C:=\{C(t)\}_{t\geq 0}$ are adapted to the filtration $\mathbb{F}$,
		\item[(ii)] $A$ is nonincreasing, right-continuous, and $A(t)\in\mathcal{A}$ for all $t\geq 0$,
		\item[(ii)] $C$ is nondecreasing, right-continuous, and $C(t)\in\mathcal{C}$ for all $t\geq 0$.
	\end{enumerate}
	 Denote by $\Pi^{\mathcal{A},\mathcal{C}}_{x,a,c}$ as the set of all admissible policies. 

	 Given $(A,C)\in\Pi^{\mathcal{A},\mathcal{C}}_{x,a,c}$, the value function is given by
	 \begin{align*}
	 	J(x;A,C)=\mathbb{E}\left[\int_0^{\tau}e^{-qs}C(s)ds\right],
	 \end{align*}
	 where $q>0$ is the discount factor and $\tau:=\inf\{t\geq0:X_t^{\pi}<0\}$ is the ruin time. For any initial surplus $x\geq 0$, initial proportion rate $a$, and initial dividend rate $c$, the goal is to find the optimal value function defined as
	 \begin{align}\label{value function orig}
	 	V(x,a,c)=\sup_{(A,C)\in\Pi^{\mathcal{A},\mathcal{C}}_{x,a,c}}J(x;A,C).
	 \end{align}

	 \begin{myrem}\label{remark on no constraint case}
	 	The optimal dividend payout with proportional reinsurance problem under the diffusion approximation without the ratcheting dividend constraint and the irreversible reinsurance constraint was first studied in \cite{hojgaard1999}. Their model setup is similar to this work without the constraints on the dividends and the reinsurance and without the constant term in the drift coefficient of the reserve process. Their problem is considered to be one-dimensional without these constraints. The additional constant term is a trivial extension of their work and the same results should still hold. Hence, if we denote by $V_{NC}(x)$ the optimal value function without constraints, then it is clear that $V_{NC}(x)\geq V(x,a,c)$ for all $x\geq 0$, $a\in\mc{A}$, and $c\in\mc{C}$. By Theorem 2.3 of \cite{hojgaard1999}, $V_{NC}$ is increasing, concave, twice continuously differentiable with $V_{NC}(0)=0$ and $\lim_{x\to\infty}V_{NC}(x)=\frac{\ol c}{q}$. Thus, for any $0\leq x_1\leq x_2$, $V_{NC}$ is Lipschitz, that is, it satisfies
	 	\begin{align}\label{no constraint Lipschitz}
	 		0\leq V_{NC}(x_2)-V_{NC}(x_1)\leq L_0(x_2-x_1),
	 	\end{align}
	 	where $L_0=V'_{NC}(0)$.
	 \end{myrem}

	 The above remark on the relationship between the optimal value functions with constraints and no constraints is useful in proving some properties of the optimal value function $V$ considered in this paper. For the rest of this section, we state some properties of $V$. The next proposition states that $V$ satisfies boundedness and monotonicity. 

	 \begin{mypr}\label{bound and monotone}
	 	The optimal value function $V(x,a,c)$ is bounded above by $\frac{\ol{c}}{q}$, nondecreasing in $x$ and $a$, and nonincreasing in $c$.
	 \end{mypr}

	 The optimal value function $V$ cannot exceed the upper limit $\frac{\ol c}{q}$ given any reserve level and it approaches $\frac{\ol c}{q}$ as the reserve levels become high enough. Moreover, higher reserve and retention levels imply that $V$ reaches the upper limit faster. On the other hand, higher dividend levels imply that $V$ reaches the upper limit slower.

	 The next proposition states that $V$ satisfies a Lipschitz property with respect to all of its variables.

	 \begin{mypr}\label{Lipschitz property}
	 	There exists a constant $L>0$ such that
	 	\begin{align*}
	 		0\leq V(x_2,a_1,c_1)-V(x_1,a_2,c_2)\leq L\left[(x_2-x_1)+(a_1-a_2)+(c_2-c_1)\right]
	 	\end{align*}
	 	for all $0\leq x_1\leq x_2$, $a_1,a_2\in\mc{A}$ with $a_2\leq a_1$, and $c_1,c_2\in\mc{C}$ with $c_1\leq c_2$. 
	 \end{mypr}

	 One way to interpret this property is that small or large perturbations in reserve level, retention rate, or dividend rate do not lead to disproportionately large changes in the value of $V$. More precisely, the Lipschitz result implies that the change in $V$ is bounded by a term that is proportional to the change in input values. Moreover, if $V$ has bounded partial derivatives in $x$, $a$, and $c$, then the Lipschitz property is immediately satisfied. We will see in the next sections that $V$ may not be smooth, that is, the partial derivatives may not exist.

	 The following lemma states that the optimal value function $V$ satisfies the dynamic programming principle, which is essential in proving that $V$ is a viscosity solution of the Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman (HJB) equation, which is the subject of the next section. 

	 \begin{mylm}\label{dpp}
	 	Given any stopping time $\tilde{\tau}$, we have
	 	\begin{align*}
	 		V(x,a,c)=\sup_{(A,C)\in\Pi^{\mathcal{A},\mathcal{C}}_{x,a,c}}\mathbb{E}\left[\int_0^{\tau\wedge \tilde{\tau}}e^{-qs}C(s)ds+e^{-q(\tau\wedge \tilde{\tau})}V(X^{\pi}_{\tau\wedge \tilde{\tau}},A(\tau\wedge \tilde{\tau}),C(\tau\wedge \tilde{\tau}))\right].
	 	\end{align*}
	 \end{mylm}

	 \section{Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman Equations}\label{section:hjb}

	 In this section, we present the HJB equations for three different cases, each characterized by the form of the reinsurance set $\mc{A}$ and the dividend set $\mc{C}$: (1) \textbf{singleton sets}, where the reinsurance rate and the dividend rate are fixed; (2) \textbf{finite sets}, where the reinsurance rate and the dividend rate can take on any value from a discrete set of choices; and (3) \textbf{closed intervals}, where the reinsurance rate and the dividend rate can take on any value within a continuous range. For the finite set case and the closed interval case, we will prove that the respective optimal value functions are viscosity solutions to their corresponding HJB equations.

	 \subsection{HJB for Singleton Sets}

	 Consider the case $\mc{A}=\{a\}$ and $\mc{C}=\{c\}$. In this case, the unique admissible strategy consists of having a constant proportion rate $a$ retained by the insurer and paying a constant dividend rate $c$ up to the ruin time. The value function, denoted by $V^{\{a\},\{c\}}(x,a,c)$, is the unique solution of the second-order linear ordinary differential equation:
	 \begin{align}\label{HJB base}
	 	\mathcal{L}^{a,c}(W):=\frac{1}{2}\sigma^2a^2W_{xx}+(\mu a-b-c)W_x-qW+c=0
	 \end{align}
	 with boundary conditions $V^{\{a\},\{c\}}(0,a,c)=0$ and $\lim_{x\to\infty}V^{\{a\},\{c\}}(x,a,c)=\frac{c}{q}$. The solutions of (\ref{HJB base}) are of the form
	 \begin{align*}
	 	K_1e^{\theta_1(a,c)x}+K_2e^{\theta_2(a,c)x}+\frac{c}{q},
	 \end{align*}
	 where $K_1,K_2\in\mathbb{R}$, and $\theta_1(a,c)$ and $\theta_2(a,c)$ are defined as
	 \begin{align*}
	 	\theta_1(a,c)=\frac{b+c-\mu a+\sqrt{(b+c-\mu a)^2+2q\sigma^2 a^2}}{\sigma^2a^2}\quad\mbox{and}\quad \theta_2(a,c)=\frac{b+c-\mu a-\sqrt{(b+c-\mu a)^2+2q\sigma^2 a^2}}{\sigma^2a^2}.
	 \end{align*}

	 We state the properties of the functions $\theta_1(a,c)$ and $\theta_2(a,c)$ and their partial derivatives, focusing on the their signs.

	 \begin{mypr}\label{claim on theta 1 and 2}
	 	We have that (i) $\theta_2(a,c)<0<\theta_1(a,c)$, (ii) $\frac{\partial}{\partial c}\theta_1(a,c),\frac{\partial}{\partial c}\theta_2(a,c)>0$, (iii) $\frac{\partial}{\partial a}\theta_1(a,c)<0$, (iv) $\frac{\partial}{\partial a}\theta_2(a,c)< 0$ if $\mu a-2(b+c)< 0$ and $\sigma<\sqrt{\frac{-\mu(\mu a-2(b+c))}{2qa}}$, and (v) $\frac{\partial}{\partial a}\theta_2(a,c)\geq 0$ if $\mu a-2(b+c)\geq 0$ or if $\mu a-2(b+c)< 0$ and $\sigma\geq \sqrt{\frac{-\mu(\mu a-2(b+c))}{2qa}}$, where equality holds if and only if $\mu a-2(b+c)< 0$ and $\sigma=\sqrt{\frac{-\mu(\mu a-2(b+c))}{2qa}}$.
	 \end{mypr}


	 \begin{myrem}\label{remark on sums of thetas}
	 	We have that
	 	\begin{align*}
	 		\mbox{sgn}\left[\theta_1(a,c)+\theta_2(a,c)\right]=\mbox{sgn}\left[\frac{2(b+c-\mu a)}{\sigma^2a^2}\right]=\mbox{sgn}\left[b+c-\mu a\right],
	 	\end{align*}
	 	\begin{align*}
	 		\mbox{sgn}\left[\frac{\partial}{\partial a}\theta_1(a,c)+\frac{\partial}{\partial a}\theta_2(a,c)\right]=\mbox{sgn}\left[\frac{2(\mu a-2(b+c))}{\sigma^2a^3}\right]=\mbox{sgn}\left[\mu a-2(b+c)\right],
	 	\end{align*}
	 	and
	 	\begin{align*}
	 		\mbox{sgn}\left[\frac{\partial}{\partial c}\theta_1(a,c)-\frac{\partial}{\partial c}\theta_2(a,c)\right]=\mbox{sgn}\left[\frac{2(b+c-\mu a)}{\sigma^2a^2}\right]=\mbox{sgn}\left[b+c-\mu a\right].
	 	\end{align*}
	 \end{myrem}
	 The sign of the drift term with the dividend rate affects the sign of the sum and/or difference of $\theta_1$ and $\theta_2$ and their derivatives with respect to the dividend and reinsurance variables.

	 The solutions of (\ref{HJB base}) with boundary condition $W(0)=0$ are of the form
	 \begin{align}\label{ode solution base}
	 	K\left[e^{\theta_1(a,c)x}-e^{\theta_2(a,c)x}\right]+\frac{c}{q}\left[1-e^{\theta_2(a,c)x}\right],
	 \end{align}
	 where $K\in\mathbb{R}$.

	 The unique solution of (\ref{HJB base}) with boundary conditions $W(0)=0$ and $\lim_{x\to\infty}W(x)=\frac{c}{q}$ corresponds to $K=0$. Hence, $V^{\{a\},\{c\}}$ is given by
	 \begin{align*}
	 	V^{\{a\},\{c\}}(x,a,c)=\frac{c}{q}\left[1-e^{\theta_2(a,c)x}\right].
	 \end{align*}
	 It is clear that $V^{\{a\},\{c\}}(x,a,c)$ is nondecreasing and concave in $x$.

	 \begin{myrem}\label{remark on asymptote V}
	 	Given $\mc{A}$ and $\mc{C}$, we have
	 	\begin{align*}
	 		V(x,a,c)\geq V^{\{a\},\{ c\}}(x,a,{c})=\frac{{c}}{q}\left[1-e^{\theta_2(a,{c})x}\right].
	 	\end{align*}
	 	Together with Proposition \ref{bound and monotone}, we obtain
	 	\begin{align*}
	 		\lim_{x\to\infty}V(x,a,c)=\frac{{c}}{q}.
	 	\end{align*}
	 \end{myrem}

	 \subsection{HJB for Closed Intervals}
	 Consider the case $\mc{A}:=[\ul{a},\ol{a}]$ and $\mc{C}:=[\ul{c},\ol{c}]$ with $0\leq\ul{a}<\ol{a}\leq 1$ and $0\leq\ul{c}<\ol{c}$. We define the value function in this case to be $V^{\mc{A},\mc{C}}$. The HJB equation associated to \eqref{value function orig} is given by
	 \begin{align}\label{hjb closed interval}
	 	\max\left\{\mc{L}^{a,c}(V^{\mc{A},\mc{C}})(x,a,c),-V^{\mc{A},\mc{C}}_a(x,a,c),V^{\mc{A},\mc{C}}_c(x,a,c)\right\}=0.
	 \end{align}
	 for $x\in[0,\infty)$, $a\in[\ul{a},\ol{a}]$, and $c\in[\ul{c},\ol{c}]$.

	 We say that a function $f:[0,\infty)\times[\ul{a},\ol{a}]\times[\ul{c},\ol{c})\to\mathbb{R}$ is $\mathbf{(2,1,1)}$\textbf{-differentiable} if $f$ is continuously differentiable and $f_x(\cdot,a,c)$ is continuously differentiable. We also define viscosity subsolutions and supersolutions for locally Lipschitz functions. This allows solutions to the HJB equation \eqref{hjb closed interval} that are not classical (or differentiable).

	 \begin{mydef}
	 	\begin{enumerate}
	 		\item[(a)] A (locally Lipschitz) function $\overline{u}:[0,\infty)\times[\ul{a},\ol{a}]\times[\ul{c},\ol{c}]\to\mathbb{R}$ is a \textbf{viscosity supersolution} of \eqref{hjb closed interval} at $(x,a,c)\in(0,\infty)\times[\ul{a},\ol{a}]\times[\ul{c},\ol{c})$ if any $(2,1,1)$-differentiable function $\varphi:(0,\infty)\times[\ul{a},\ol{a}]\times[\ul{c},\ol{c})\to\mathbb{R}$%, called a test function for supersolution at $(x,a,c)$,
            with $\varphi(x,a,c)=\overline{u}(x,a,c)$ such that $\overline{u}-\varphi$ reaches the minimum at $(x,a,c)$ satisfies
	 		\begin{align*}
	 			\max\{\mathcal{L}^{a,c}(\varphi)(x,a,c),-\varphi_a(x,a,c),\varphi_c(x,a,c)\}\leq 0.
	 		\end{align*}
	 		\item[(b)] A (locally Lipschitz) function $\underline{u}:[0,\infty)\times[\ul{a},\ol{a}]\times[\ul{c},\ol{c}]\to\mathbb{R}$ is a \textbf{viscosity subsolution} of (\ref{hjb closed interval}) at $(x,a,c)\in(0,\infty)\times[\ul{a},\ol{a}]\times[\ul{c},\ol{c})$ if any $(2,1,1)$-differentiable function $\psi:(0,\infty)\times[\ul{a},\ol{a}]\times[\ul{c},\ol{c})\to\mathbb{R}$%, called a test function for subsolution at $(x,a,c)$, 
            with $\psi(x,a,c)=\underline{u}(x,a,c)$ such that $\underline{u}-\psi$ reaches the maximum at $(x,a,c)$ satisfies
	 		\begin{align*}
	 			\max\{\mathcal{L}^{a,c}(\varphi)(x,a,c),-\varphi_a(x,a,c),\varphi_c(x,a,c)\}\geq 0.
	 		\end{align*}
	 		\item[(c)] A (locally Lipschitz) function ${u}:[0,\infty)\times[\ul{a},\ol{a}]\times[\ul{c},\ol{c}]\to\mathbb{R}$ which is both a viscosity supersolution and a viscosity subsolution at $(x,a,c)\in(0,\infty)\times[\ul{a},\ol{a}]\times[\ul{c},\ol{c})$ is called a \textbf{viscosity solution} of (\ref{hjb closed interval}) at $(x,a,c)$.
	 	\end{enumerate}
	 \end{mydef}

	 We now state the result on $V^{\mc{A},\mc{C}}$ as a viscosity solution of \eqref{hjb closed interval}. 

	 \begin{mypr}\label{V is viscosity closed set}
	 	$V^{\mc{A},\mc{C}}$ is a viscosity solution of \eqref{hjb closed interval} in $(0,\infty)\times[\ul{a},\ol{a}]\times[\ul{c},\ol{c})$.
	 \end{mypr}

	 The next result is a comparison principle for viscosity solutions, which will allows us to prove the uniqueness of the viscosity solution to the HJB equation \eqref{hjb closed interval}.

	 \begin{mypr}\label{Comparison closed interval}
	 	Suppose the following conditions hold:
	 	\begin{itemize}
	 		\item[(i)] $\ul{u}$ is a viscosity subsolution and $\ol{u}$ is a viscosity supersolution of the HJB equation \eqref{hjb closed interval} for all $x\in(0,\infty)$, $a\in[\ul{a},\ol{a}]$, and $c\in[\ul{c},\ol{c})$,
	 		\item[(ii)] $\ul{u}$ and $\ol{u}$ are nondecreasing the variables $x$ and $a$, nonincreasing in the variable $c$ and Lipschitz in $[0,\infty)\times[\ul{a},\ol{a}]\times[\ul{c},\ol{c}]$, and
	 		\item[(iii)] $\ul{u}(0,a,c)=\ol{u}(0,a,c)=0$ and $\lim_{x\to\infty}\ul{u}(x,a,c)\leq\frac{\ol{c}}{q}\leq \lim_{x\to\infty}\ol{u}(x,a,c)$.
	 	\end{itemize}
	 	Then, $\ul{u}\leq\ol{u}$ in $[0,\infty)\times[\ul{a},\ol{a}]\times[\ul{c},\ol{c}]$.
	 \end{mypr}

	 We now state the main result for the closed interval case. It shows that the viscosity solution $V^{\mc{A},\mc{C}}$ is unique and satisfies the boundary conditions of the optimal value function $V$ defined in \eqref{value function orig}.

	 \begin{myth}\label{verification for limit function}
	 	The optimal value function $V^{\mc{A},\mc{C}}$ is the unique viscosity solution of \eqref{hjb closed interval} in $(0,\infty)\times[\ul{a},\ol{a}]\times[\ul{c},\ol{c})$ with $V^{\mc{A},\mc{C}}(0,a,c)=0$ and $\lim_{x\to\infty}V^{\mc{A},\mc{C}}(x,a,c)=\frac{\ol{c}}{q}$ for $(a,c)\in[\ul{a},\ol{a}]\times[\ul{c},\ol{c})$.
	 \end{myth}
	 \begin{proof}
	 	The result is a direct consequence of Remark \ref{remark on asymptote V} and Propositions \ref{V is viscosity closed set} and \ref{Comparison closed interval}.
	 \end{proof}

	 The following result is a verification theorem which states the optimality conditions for the optimal value function. More precisely, it gives some sufficient conditions that a candidate value function must satisfy to be considered the optimal value function.

	 \begin{myth}
	 	Consider a family of strategies
	 	\begin{align*}
	 		\left\{(A,C)\in\Pi^{\mc{A},\mc{C}}_{x,a,c}:(x,a,c)\in[0,\infty)\times[\ul{a},\ol{a}]\times[\ul{c},\ol{c}]\right\}.
	 	\end{align*}
	 	If the function $W(x,a,c):=J(x;A,C)$ is nondecreasing in the variable $x$ and a viscosity supersolution of the HJB equation \eqref{hjb closed interval} in $(0,\infty)\times[\ul{a},\ol{a}]\times[\ul{c},\ol{c})$ with $W(0,a,c)=0$ and $\lim_{x\to\infty}W(x,a,c)=\frac{\ol{c}}{q}$, then $W$ is the optimal value function $V^{[\ul{a},\ol{a}],[\ul{c},\ol{c}]}$. Moreover, if for each $m,n\geq 1$ there exists a family of strategies
	 	\begin{align*}
	 		\left\{(A^m,C^n)\in\Pi^{\mc{A},\mc{C}}_{x,a,c}:(x,a,c)\in[0,\infty)\times[\ul{a},\ol{a}]\times[\ul{c},\ol{c}]\right\}
	 	\end{align*}
	 	such that $W(x,a,c):=\lim_{m\to\infty}\lim_{n\to\infty}J(x;A^m,C^n)$ is a viscosity supersolution of the HJB equation \eqref{hjb closed interval} in $(0,\infty)\times[\ul{a},\ol{a}]\times[\ul{c},\ol{c})$ with $W(0,a,c)=0$ and $\lim_{x\to\infty}W(x,a,c)=\frac{\ol{c}}{q}$, then $W$ is the optimal value function $V^{\mc{A},\mc{C}}$.
	 \end{myth}
	 \begin{proof}
	 	The result is a direct consequence of Remark \ref{remark on asymptote V} and Propositions \ref{Lipschitz property}, \ref{V is viscosity closed set}, and \ref{Comparison closed interval}.
	 \end{proof}

	 \subsection{HJB for Finite Sets}

	 Consider the case $\mathcal{A}:=\{a_1,a_2,\ldots,a_m\}$ with $0\leq a_m<a_{m-1}<\cdots <a_1\leq 1$ and $\mathcal{C}:=\{c_1,c_2,\ldots,c_n\}$ with $0\leq c_1<c_2<\cdots<c_n=\ol{c}$. By definition, $V^{\mc{A},\mc{C}}(x,a,c)=V^{\mc{A},\mc{C}}(x,a_i,c_j)$, where $a_i:=\max\{\tilde a\in\mc{A}:\tilde{a}\leq a\}$ and $c_j:=\min\{\tilde c\in\mc{C}:\tilde c\geq c\}$ and $V^{\mc{A},\mc{C}}(x,a,c)\leq V(x,a,c)$.

	 For $1\leq i\leq m$ and $1\leq j\leq n$, we simplify the notation as follows
	 \begin{align}\label{value discrete}
	 	V^{a_i,c_j}(x):=V^{\mathcal{A},\mathcal{C}}(x,a_i,c_j).
	 \end{align}
	 Using Proposition \ref{bound and monotone}, we have the following inequalities
	 \begin{align*}
	 	V^{a_i,c_j}(x)\geq V^{a_{i+1},c_j}(x)\geq \cdots\geq V^{a_m,c_j}(x),
	 \end{align*}
	 for a fixed $1\leq j\leq n$, and
	 \begin{align*}
	 	V^{a_i,c_j}(x)\geq V^{a_{i},c_{j+1}}(x)\geq \cdots \geq V^{a_i,c_n}(x),
	 \end{align*} 
	for a fixed $1\leq i\leq m$.

	The HJB equation associated to (\ref{value discrete}) is given by
	\begin{align}\label{hjb main}
		\max\{\mathcal{L}^{a_i,c_j}(V^{a_i,c_j})(x),V^{a_{i+1},c_{j}}(x)-V^{a_i,c_j}(x),V^{a_i,c_{j+1}}(x)-V^{a_i,c_j}(x)\}=0,
	\end{align}
	for $x\geq 0$, $1\leq i\leq m$, and $1\leq j\leq n$.

	We now define viscosity subsolutions and supersolutions for the HJB equation \eqref{hjb main}.

	\begin{mydef}
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item[(a)] A (locally Lipschitz) function $\overline{u}:[0,\infty)\to\mathbb{R}$ is a viscosity supersolution of (\ref{hjb main}) at $x\in(0,\infty)$ if any twice continuously differentiable function $\varphi:[0,\infty)\to\mathbb{R}$%, called a test function for supersolution at $x$, 
            with $\varphi(x)=\overline{u}(x)$ such that $\overline{u}-\varphi$ reaches the minimum at $x$ satisfies
			\begin{align}\label{hjb supersolution}
				\max\{\mathcal{L}^{a_i,c_j}(\varphi)(x),V^{a_{i+1},c_{j}}(x)-\varphi(x),V^{a_i,c_{j+1}}(x)-\varphi(x)\}\leq 0.
			\end{align}
			\item[(b)] A (locally Lipschitz) function $\underline{u}:[0,\infty)\to\mathbb{R}$ is a viscosity subsolution of (\ref{hjb main}) at $x\in(0,\infty)$ if any twice continuously differentiable function $\psi:[0,\infty)\to\mathbb{R}$%, called a test function for subsolution at $x$, 
            with $\psi(x)=\underline{u}(x)$ such that $\underline{u}-\psi$ reaches the maximum at $x$ satisfies
			\begin{align*}
				\max\{\mathcal{L}^{a_i,c_j}(\psi)(x),V^{a_{i+1},c_{j}}(x)-\psi(x),V^{a_i,c_{j+1}}(x)-\psi(x)\}\geq 0.
			\end{align*}
			\item[(c)] A (locally Lipschitz) function ${u}:[0,\infty)\to\mathbb{R}$ which is both a viscosity supersolution and a viscosity subsolution at $x\in[0,\infty)$ is called a viscosity solution of (\ref{hjb main}) at $x$.
		\end{enumerate}
	\end{mydef}

	The following proposition states that $V^{a_i,c_j}$ is also a viscosity solution to its corresponding HJB equation \eqref{hjb main}.

	\begin{mypr}\label{V is a viscosity solution}
		For $1\leq i<m$ and $1\leq j<n$, $V^{a_i,c_j}$ is a viscosity solution of \eqref{hjb main} in $(0,\infty)$.
	\end{mypr}

	The next result is a comparison principle for the finite case and is used to prove uniqueness of the viscosity solution to the HJB equation \eqref{hjb main}

	\begin{mypr}\label{comparison viscosity}
		Suppose the following conditions hold:
		\begin{itemize}
			\item[(i)] $\underline{u}$ is a viscosity subsolution and $\overline{u}$ is a viscosity supersolution of the HJB equation \eqref{hjb main} for all $x>0$,
			\item[(ii)] $\ul{u}$ and $\ol{u}$ are nondecreasing in the variable $x$ and Lipschitz in $[0,\infty)$, and
			\item[(iii)] $\ul{u}(0)=\ol{u}(0)$ and $\lim_{x\to\infty}\ul{u}(x)\leq\frac{c_n}{q}\leq \lim_{x\to\infty}\ol{u}(x)$.
		\end{itemize}
		Then, $\ul{u}\leq\ol{u}$ in $[0,\infty)$.
	\end{mypr}

	The following theorem proves that $V^{a_i,c_j}$ is the unique viscosity solution of \eqref{hjb main} and satisfies the boundary conditions.

	\begin{myth}\label{verification discrete}
		The optimal value function $V^{a_i,c_j}(x)$ for $1\leq i<m$ and $1\leq j<n$ is the unique viscosity solution of the associated HJB equation (\ref{hjb main}) with boundary condition $V^{a_i,c_j}(0)=0$ and $\lim_{x\to\infty}V^{a_i,c_j}(x)=\frac{c_n}{q}$.
	\end{myth}
	\begin{proof}
		The result is a direct consequence of Remark \ref{remark on asymptote V} and Propositions \ref{Lipschitz property} and \ref{V is a viscosity solution}, and \ref{comparison viscosity}.
	\end{proof}

	\section{Convergence of Optimal Value Functions}\label{section:convergence}

	In the previous section, we have shown that the optimal value functions of the finite set case and the closed interval case are the unique viscosity solutions to their respective HJB equations. We are now interested in the convergence of the optimal value function of the finite set case to the optimal value function of the closed interval case. In this section, we will establish the convergence of the optimal value functions as the finite sets become more refined.

	Consider for $m\geq0$ a sequence of sets $\mc{A}^m$ with $k_m$ elements of the form
	\begin{align*}
		\mc{A}^m:=\left\{\ul{a}:=a_{k_m}^m<\cdots<a^m_2<a^m_{1}=\ol{a}\right\}
	\end{align*}
	satisfying $\mc{A}^0=\{\ul{a},\ol{a}\}$, $\mc{A}^m\subset\mc{A}^{m+1}$ and mesh size $\delta(\mc{A}^m):=\max_{k=2,\ldots,k_m}(a^m_{k-1}-a^m_{k})\downarrow 0$ as $m\to\infty$. Similarly, consider for $n\geq0$ a sequence of sets $\mc{C}^n$ with $l_n$ elements of the form
	\begin{align*}
		\mc{C}^n:=\left\{\ul{c}:=c_1^n<c_2^n<\cdots<c^n_{l_n}=\ol{c}\right\}
	\end{align*}
	satisfying $\mc{C}^0=\{\ul{c},\ol{c}\}$, $\mc{C}^n\subset\mc{C}^{n+1}$ and mesh size $\delta(\mc{C}^n):=\max_{l=2,\ldots,l_n}(c^n_l-c^n_{l-1})\downarrow 0$ as $n\to\infty$. We extend the definition of $V^{\mc{A}^m,\mc{C}^n}$ to the function $V^{m,n}:[0,\infty)\times[\ul{a},\ol{a}]\times[\ul{c},\ol{c}]\to\mathbb{R}$ as
	\begin{align*}
		V^{m,n}(x,a,c):=V^{\mc{A}^m,\mc{C}^n}(x,\tilde{a}^m,\tilde{c}^n),
	\end{align*}
	where
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\tilde{a}^m:=\max\{a_i^m:a_i^m\leq a\}\quad\mbox{and}\quad \tilde{c}^n:=\min\{c_j^n:c_j^n\geq c\}.
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	We want to show that $\lim_{m\to\infty}\lim_{n\to\infty}V^{m,n}(x,a,c)=V^{[\ul{a},\ol{a}],[\ul{c},\ol{c}]}(x,a,c)$ for any $(x,a,c)\in[0,\infty)\times[\ul{a},\ol{a}]\times[\ul{c},\ol{c}]$. In particular, we want to show that the limits converge uniformly.

	Since $\mc{A}^m\subset\mc{A}^{m+1}$ and $\mc{C}^n\subset\mc{C}^{n+1}$, it then follows that $\tilde a^{m+1}\geq \tilde a^m\in\mc{A}^m$ for every $a\in[\ul{a},\ol{a}]$ and $\tilde c^{n+1}\leq\tilde c^n\in\mc{C}^n$ for every $c\in[\ul{c},\ol{c}]$. Consequently,
	\begin{align*}
		V^{[\ul{a},\ol{a}],[\ul{c},\ol{c}]}(x,a,c)
		&\geq V^{m+1,n+1}(x,a,c)\\
		&=V^{\mc{A}^{m+1},\mc{C}^{n+1}}(x,\tilde{a}^{m+1},\tilde{c}^{n+1})\\
		&\geq V^{\mc{A}^{m+1},\mc{C}^{n+1}}(x,\tilde{a}^{m},\tilde{c}^{n})\\
		&\geq V^{\mc{A}^{m},\mc{C}^{n}}(x,\tilde{a}^{m},\tilde{c}^{n})\\
		&=V^{m,n}(x,a,c).
	\end{align*}
	This implies that $V^{m,n}$ is bounded above by $V^{[\ul{a},\ol{a}],[\ul{c},\ol{c}]}$. Thus, the limit function, denoted by $\ol{V}$ exists, that is,
	\begin{align*}
		\ol{V}(x,a,c):=\lim_{m\to\infty}\ol{V}^m(x,a,c),
	\end{align*}
	where $\ol{V}^m(x,a,c):=\lim_{n\to\infty}V^{m,n}(x,a,c)$ is also a limit function.
	We want to show that the limit function $\ol{V}$ is indeed the optimal value function $V^{[\ul{a},\ol{a}],[\ul{c},\ol{c}]}$.

	\begin{mypr}\label{V discrete converge to V cont}
		The sequence $V^{m,n}$ converges uniformly to $\ol{V}^m$ and the sequence $\ol{V}^m$ converges uniformly to $\ol{V}$.
	\end{mypr}


	\begin{myrem}\label{remark on limit value functions}
		For any $(x,a,c)\in[0,\infty)\times[\ul{a},\ol{a}]\times[\ul{c},\ol{c}]$, $V^{m,n}(x,a,c)=V^{\mc{A}^m,\mc{C}^n}(x,\tilde{a}^m,\tilde{c}^n)$ is a limit of value functions of admissible strategies $\Pi^{\mc{A}^m,\mc{C}^n}_{x,\tilde{a}^m,\tilde{c}^n}\subset\Pi^{[\ul{a},\ol{a}],[\ul{c},\ol{c}]}_{x,a,c}$. Hence, $\ol{V}(x,a,c)$ and $\ol{V}^m(x,a,c)$ are also limits of value functions of admissible strategies in $\Pi^{[\ul{a},\ol{a}],[\ul{c},\ol{c}]}_{x,a,c}$. Moreover, for all $x_1\leq x_2$, $a_k\leq a_i$, $c_l\leq c_j$, by Proposition \ref{Lipschitz property},
		\begin{align*}
			0\leq V^{m,n}(x_2,a_i,c_l)-V^{m,n}(x_1,a_k,c_j)\leq L\left[(x_2-x_1)+(a_i-a_k)+(c_j-c_l)\right]
		\end{align*}
		with the same Lipschitz constant $L$ for all $n$. Since $V^{m,n}$ converges uniformly to $\ol V^{m}$, then
		\begin{align*}
			0\leq \ol V^{m}(x_2,a_i,c_l)-\ol V^{m}(x_1,a_k,c_j)\leq L\left[(x_2-x_1)+(a_i-a_k)+(c_j-c_l)\right]
		\end{align*}
		with, still, the same Lipschitz constant $L$ for all $m$. Since $\ol{V}^m$ converges uniformly to $\ol{V}$, then the function $\ol{V}$ is Lipschitz with constant $L$.
	\end{myrem}

	The following result proves the main result which states that the limit function $\ol V$ of the finite set case is indeed the optimal value function of the closed interval case.

	\begin{myth}\label{theorem on convergence}
		The function $\ol{V}$ defined as
		\begin{align*}
			\ol V(x,a,c):=\lim_{m\to\infty}\ol{V}^m(x,a,c)=\lim_{m\to\infty}\lim_{n\to\infty}V^{m,n}(x,a,c)
		\end{align*}
		is the optimal value function $V^{[\ul{a},\ol{a}],[\ul{c},\ol{c}]}$.
	\end{myth}


	\section{Optimal Strategies}\label{section:optimalstrat}

	In the previous sections, we characterized and identified the optimal value functions as the unique viscosity solutions to their respective HJB equations and established the convergence of the optimal value functions from the finite set case to the closed interval case. To fully solve the control problems, we construct the corresponding optimal strategies. In this section, we first construct the optimal strategies for the finite set case. We then discuss the curve strategies as preparation for the construction of the optimal strategies for the closed interval case. Finally, we prove that the candidate strategies for the finite set case is optimal and that the candidate strategies for the closed interval case is $\epsilon$-optimal under specific conditions.

	\subsection{Optimal Strategy for Finite Sets}

	By Theorem \ref{verification discrete}, we can partition $(0,\infty)$ in two ways. The first way is fixing $j$. For $i<m$, $(0,\infty)$ can be partitioned in such a way that the optimal strategy is to retain $a_i$ of the incoming claims when the current surplus is in the open set $\{x:V^{a_i,c_j}(x)>V^{a_{i+1},c_j}(x)\}$ and to decrease the risk exposure to $a_{i+1}$ when the current surplus is in the closed set $\{x:V^{a_i,c_j}(x)=V^{a_{i+1},c_j}(x)\}$. The second way is fixing $i$. Similarly, for $j<n$, the optimal strategy is to pay dividends at rate $c_j$ of the incoming claims when the current surplus is in the open set $\{x:V^{a_i,c_j}(x)>V^{a_{i},c_{j+1}}(x)\}$ and to increase the dividend rate to $c_{j+1}$ when the current surplus is in the closed set $\{x:V^{a_i,c_j}(x)=V^{a_{i},c_{j+1}}(x)\}$.

	Define the functions $y:\mathcal{A}\times\mathcal{C}\to[0,\infty]$ and $z:\mathcal{A}\times\mathcal{C}\to[0,\infty]$ such that $y(a_m,c_j)=+\infty$ for $1\leq j\leq n$ and $z(a_i,c_n)=+\infty$ for $1\leq i\leq m$.

	For $1\leq i\leq m$ and $1\leq j\leq n$, we simplify the notation as follows
	\begin{align*}
		y_{i,j}:=y(a_i,c_j)\quad\mbox{and}\quad z_{i,j}:=z(a_i,c_j).
	\end{align*}

	Consider the simplest reinsurance-dividend strategies wherein the corresponding value function $\upsilon^{a_i,c_j}(x)$ satisfies the following:
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item[(i)] $\upsilon^{a_m,c_j}(x)=V^{a_m,c_j}(x)$ for $1\leq j\leq n$ and  $\upsilon^{a_i,c_n}(x)=V^{a_i,c_n}(x)$ for $1\leq i\leq m$.
		\item[(ii)] For $i<m$, $\mathcal{L}^{a_i,c_n}(\upsilon^{a_i,c_n}(x))=0$ if $x\in[0,y_{i,n})$ and $\upsilon^{a_i,c_j}(x)=\upsilon^{a_{i+1},c_j}(x)$ if $x\in[y_{i,n},\infty)$.
		\item[(iii)] For $j<n$, $\mathcal{L}^{a_m,c_j}(\upsilon^{a_m,c_j}(x))=0$ if $x\in[0,z_{m,j})$ and $\upsilon^{a_i,c_j}(x)=\upsilon^{a_{i},c_{j+1}}(x)$ if $x\in[z_{m,j},\infty)$.
		\item[(iv)] For $i<m$ and $j<n$, $\mathcal{L}^{a_i,c_j}(\upsilon^{a_i,c_j}(x))=0$ if $x\in[0,y_{i,j}\wedge z_{i,j})$, $\upsilon^{a_i,c_j}(x)=\upsilon^{a_{i+1},c_j}(x)$ if $x\in[y_{i,j},\infty)$, and $\upsilon^{a_i,c_j}(x)=\upsilon^{a_i,c_{j+1}}(x)$ if $x\in[z_{i,j},\infty)$.
	\end{enumerate}	

%	\begin{myass}
%		For all $1\leq i<m$ and $1\leq j<n$, $y(a_i)\neq z(c_j)$.
%	\end{myass}
%	

	Denote the threshold strategy as
	\begin{align}\label{threshold strategy}
		\pi^{y,z}:=\{(A_{x,a_i,c_j},C_{x,a_i,c_j})\}_{(x,a_i,c_j)\in[0,\infty)\times\mathcal{A}\times\mathcal{C}},
	\end{align}
	where $(A_{x,a_i,c_j},C_{x,a_i,c_j})\in\Pi^{\mathcal{A},\mathcal{C}}_{x,a_i,c_j}$. The threshold strategy $\pi^{y,z}$ is defined as follows:
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item[(i)] If $i=m$ and $j=n$, retain $a_m$ of the incoming claims and pay dividends with rate $c_n$ until ruin time, that is, $(A_{x,a_m,c_n},C_{x,a_m,c_n})(t)=(a_m,c_n)$.
		\item[(ii)] If $i=m$, $j<n$, and $x\in[z_{m,j},\infty)$, follow $(A_{x,a_m,c_l},C_{x,a_m,c_l})\in\Pi^{\mathcal{A},\mathcal{C}}_{x,a_m,c_l}$, where 
		\begin{align*}
			l=\begin{cases}
				n,&\mbox{if $x\geq z_{m,l}$ for $l=j+1,\ldots,n-1$},\\
				\min\{l\in\{j+1,\ldots,n-1\}:x<z_{m,l}\},&\mbox{otherwise}.
			\end{cases}
		\end{align*}
		\item[(iii)] If $j=n$, $i<m$, and $x\in[y_{i,n},\infty)$, follow $(A_{x,a_k,c_n},C_{x,a_k,c_n})\in\Pi^{\mathcal{A},\mathcal{C}}_{x,a_k,c_n}$, where 
		\begin{align*}
			k=\begin{cases}
				m,&\mbox{if $x\geq y_{k,n}$ for $k=i+1,\ldots,m-1$},\\
				\min\{k\in\{i+1,\ldots,m-1\}:x<y_{k,n}\},&\mbox{otherwise}.
			\end{cases}
		\end{align*}
		\item[(iv)] If $i<m$, $j<n$, and $x\in [y_{i,j},z_{i,j})$, follow $(A_{x,a_k,c_j},C_{x,a_k,c_j})\in\Pi^{\mathcal{A},\mathcal{C}}_{x,a_k,c_j}$, where 
		\begin{align*}
			k=\begin{cases}
				m,&\mbox{if $z_{k,j}>x\geq y_{k,j}$ for $k=i+1,\ldots,m-1$},\\
				\min\{k\in\{i+1,\ldots,m-1\}:z_{k,j}<y_{k,j}\},&\mbox{otherwise}.
			\end{cases}
		\end{align*}
		\item[(v)] If $i<m$, $j<n$, and $x\in [z_{i,j},y_{i,j})$, follow $(A_{x,a_i,c_l},C_{x,a_i,c_l})\in\Pi^{\mathcal{A},\mathcal{C}}_{x,a_i,c_l}$, where 
		\begin{align*}
			l=\begin{cases}
				n,&\mbox{if $y_{i,l}>x\geq z_{i,l}$ for $l=i+1,\ldots,m-1$},\\
				\min\{l\in\{j+1,\ldots,n-1\}:y_{i,l}<z_{i,l}\},&\mbox{otherwise}.
			\end{cases}
		\end{align*}
		\item[(vi)] If $i<m$, $j<n$, and $x\in [y_{i,j}\vee z_{i,j},\infty)$, follow $(A_{x,a_k,c_l},C_{x,a_k,c_l})\in\Pi^{\mathcal{A},\mathcal{C}}_{x,a_k,c_l}$, where 
		\begin{align*}
			k=\begin{cases}
				m,&\mbox{if $x\geq y_{k,j}$ for $k=i+1,\ldots,m-1$},\\
				\min\{k\in\{i+1,\ldots,m-1\}:x<y_{k,j}\},&\mbox{otherwise},
			\end{cases}
		\end{align*}
		and
		\begin{align*}
			l=\begin{cases}
				n,&\mbox{if $x\geq z_{i,l}$ for $l=j+1,\ldots,m-1$},\\
				\min\{l\in\{j+1,\ldots,n-1\}:x<z_{i,l}\},&\mbox{otherwise}.
			\end{cases}
		\end{align*}
		\item[(vii)] If $i<m$, $j<n$, and $x\in [0,y_{i,j}\wedge z_{i,j})$,  retain $a_i$ of the incoming claims and pay dividends with rate $c_j$ until ruin time or until before the current surplus reaches $y_{i,j}\wedge z_{i,j}$, whichever comes first.
	\end{enumerate}
	We call the values $y_{i,j}$ and $z_{i,j}$ the proportion threshold and dividend threshold, respectively, at proportion rate level $a_i$ and dividend rate level $c_j$. The functions $y$ and $z$ are called the proportion threshold function and the dividend threshold function, respectively. The expected payoff of the strategy $\pi^{y,z}$ is defined as
	\begin{align}\label{expected payoff}
		W^{y,z}(x,a_i,c_j):=J(x;A_{x,a_i,c_j},C_{x,a_i,c_j}).
	\end{align}

	\begin{mypr}\label{Recursive formula W}
		The recursive formula for $W^{y,z}$ is given by
		\begin{align*}
			W^{y,z}(x,a_m,c_n)&=\frac{c_n}{q}\left[1-e^{\theta_2(a_m,c_n)x}\right],\\
			W^{y,z}(x,a_i,c_j)&=\begin{cases}
				\frac{c_j}{q}\left[1-e^{\theta_2(a_i,c_j)x}\right]+k^{y,z}(a_i,c_j)\left[e^{\theta_1(a_i,c_j)x}-e^{\theta_2(a_i,c_j)x}\right],&\mbox{if $x<y_{i,j}\wedge z_{i,j}$},\\
				W^{y,z}(x,a_{i+1},c_j)\mathds{1}_{\{y_{i,j}<z_{i,j}\}}+W^{y,z}(x,a_i,c_{j+1})\mathds{1}_{\{y_{i,j}>z_{i,j}\}}\\
				\quad+W^{y,z}(x,a_{i+1},c_{j+1})\mathds{1}_{\{y_{i,j}=z_{i,j}\}},&\mbox{otherwise},
			\end{cases}
		\end{align*}
		for $i<n$ or $j<m$, where
		\begin{align*}
			k^{y,z}(a_i,c_j):=\begin{cases}
				\frac{W^{y,z}(y_{i,j},a_{i+1},c_j)-\frac{c_j}{q}\left[1-e^{\theta_2(a_i,c_j)y_{i,j}}\right]}{e^{\theta_1(a_i,c_j)y_{i,j}}-e^{\theta_2(a_i,c_j)y_{i,j}}},&\mbox{if $y_{i,j}<z_{i,j}$},\\
				\frac{W^{y,z}(z_{i,j},a_{i},c_{j+1})-\frac{c_j}{q}\left[1-e^{\theta_2(a_i,c_j)z_{i,j}}\right]}{e^{\theta_1(a_i,c_j)z_{i,j}}-e^{\theta_2(a_i,c_j)z_{i,j}}},&\mbox{if $y_{i,j}>z_{i,j}$},\\
				\frac{W^{y,z}(y_{i,j},a_{i+1},c_{j+1})-\frac{c_j}{q}\left[1-e^{\theta_2(a_i,c_j)y_{i,j}}\right]}{e^{\theta_1(a_i,c_j)y_{i,j}}-e^{\theta_2(a_i,c_j)y_{i,j}}},&\mbox{if $y_{i,j}=z_{i,j}$}.
			\end{cases}
		\end{align*}
	\end{mypr}
	\begin{proof}
		Since the strategy is to retain $a_i$ of the incoming claims and pay dividends with rate $c_j$ whenever $x\in[0,y_{i,j}\wedge z_{i,j})$, then $\mc{L}^{a_i,c_j}(W^{y,z})(x,a_i,c_j)=0$ for $x\in[0,y_{i,j}\wedge z_{i,j})$. Secondly, $W^{y,z}(0,a_i,c_j)=0$ since ruin is immediate as soon as the reserve level hits $0$. Lastly, by definition, $W^{y,z}(x,a_i,c_j)=W^{y,z}(x,a_{i+1},c_j)$ if $x\geq y_{i,j}$ and $W^{y,z}(x,a_i,c_j)=W^{y,z}(x,a_{i},c_{j+1})$ if $x\geq z_{i,j}$. The formula for $W^{y,z}$ then follows from \eqref{ode solution base}.
	\end{proof}

	We now look for the maximum of the expected payoff $W^{y,z}(x,a_i,c_j)$ among all the possible threshold functions $y$ and $z$. Denote by $y^*$ and $z^*$ the optimal threshold functions in the sense that $k^{y^*_{i,j},z^*_{i,j}}(a_i,c_j)$ is the maximum for any $i<m$ or $j<n$. Since the function $W^{y,z}(x,a_m,c_n)$ is known, we can solve this optimization problem using backward recursion.

	We look for the optimal threshold strategy, which can be seen as a sequence of $m-1$ and $n-1$ one-dimensional optimization problems. The optimization problems pertain to maximizing $k^{y,z}(a_i,c_j)$ for $i=m-1,\ldots,1$ and $j=n-1,\ldots,1$. If $W^{y^*,z^*}(x,a_k,c_l)$, $y^*_{k,l}$, and $z^*_{k,l}$ are known for $k=i+1,\ldots,m-1$ and $l=j+1,\ldots,n-1$, then using the recursive formula for $W^{y,z}$, we can solve for $W^{y^*,z^*}(x,a_{i+1},c_j)$ and $W^{y^*,z^*}(x,a_i,c_{j+1})$. Consequently, we can then solve for $W^{y^*,z^*}(x,a_i,c_j)$, $y^*_{i,j}$, and $z^*_{i,j}$.

	Define the continuous functions $G^{\mathcal{A}}_{i,j}:[0,\infty)\to\mathbb{R}$, $G^{\mathcal{C}}_{i,j}:[0,\infty)\to\mathbb{R}$, and $G^{\mathcal{E}}_{i,j}:[0,\infty)\to\mathbb{R}$ as
	\begin{align}\label{G^A function}
		G^{\mathcal{A}}_{i,j}(x):=\begin{cases}
			\frac{W^{y^*,z^*}(x,a_{i+1},c_j)-\frac{c_j}{q}\left[1-e^{\theta_2(a_i,c_j)x}\right]}{e^{\theta_1(a_i,c_j)x}-e^{\theta_2(a_i,c_j)x}},&\mbox{if $x>0$}\\
			\frac{\partial_xW^{y^*,z^*}(0,a_{i+1},c_j)+\frac{c_j}{q}\theta_2(a_i,c_j)}{\theta_1(a_i,c_j)-\theta_2(a_i,c_j)},&\mbox{if $x=0$},
		\end{cases}
	\end{align}
	\begin{align*}
		G^{\mathcal{C}}_{i,j}(x):=\begin{cases}
			\frac{W^{y^*,z^*}(x,a_{i},c_{j+1})-\frac{c_j}{q}\left[1-e^{\theta_2(a_i,c_j)x}\right]}{e^{\theta_1(a_i,c_j)x}-e^{\theta_2(a_i,c_j)x}},&\mbox{if $x>0$}\\
			\frac{\partial_xW^{y^*,z^*}(0,a_{i},c_{j+1})+\frac{c_j}{q}\theta_2(a_i,c_j)}{\theta_1(a_i,c_j)-\theta_2(a_i,c_j)},&\mbox{if $x=0$},
		\end{cases}
	\end{align*}
	and
	\begin{align*}
		G^{\mathcal{E}}_{i,j}(x):=\begin{cases}
			\frac{W^{y^*,z^*}(x,a_{i+1},c_{j+1})-\frac{c_j}{q}\left[1-e^{\theta_2(a_i,c_j)x}\right]}{e^{\theta_1(a_i,c_j)x}-e^{\theta_2(a_i,c_j)x}},&\mbox{if $x>0$}\\
			\frac{\partial_xW^{y^*,z^*}(0,a_{i+1},c_{j+1})+\frac{c_j}{q}\theta_2(a_i,c_j)}{\theta_1(a_i,c_j)-\theta_2(a_i,c_j)},&\mbox{if $x=0$}.
		\end{cases}
	\end{align*}


	\begin{mylm}\label{G has maxima and min exists}
		The functions $G^{\mathcal{A}}_{i,j}$, $G^{\mathcal{C}}_{i,j}$, and $G^{\mathcal{E}}_{i,j}$ attain their respective maxima in $[0,\infty)$. Moreover, $\min\left[\argmax_{x\in[0,\infty)}G^{\mathcal{A}}_{i,j}(x)\right]$, $\min\left[\argmax_{x\in[0,\infty)}G^{\mathcal{C}}_{i,j}(x)\right]$, and $\min\left[\argmax_{x\in[0,\infty)}G^{\mathcal{E}}_{i,j}(x)\right]$ exist.
	\end{mylm}


	We introduce the following notations:
	\begin{align*}
		g^{\mathcal{A}}_{i,j}&:=
		\begin{cases}
			\min\left[\argmax_{x\in[0,\infty)}G^{\mathcal{A}}_{i,j}(x)\right],&\mbox{if $\max_{x\in [0,\infty)}G^{\mathcal{A}}_{i,j}(x)\neq 0$},\\
			h^{\mc{A}}_{i,j},&\mbox{otherwise},
		\end{cases}\\
		g^{\mathcal{C}}_{i,j}&:=
		\begin{cases}
			\min\left[\argmax_{x\in[0,\infty)}G^{\mathcal{C}}_{i,j}(x)\right],&\mbox{if $\max_{x\in [0,\infty)}G^{\mathcal{C}}_{i,j}(x)\neq 0$},\\
			h^{\mc{C}}_{i,j},&\mbox{otherwise},
		\end{cases}\\
		g^{\mathcal{E}}_{i,j}&:=
		\begin{cases}
			\min\left[\argmax_{x\in[0,\infty)}G^{\mathcal{E}}_{i,j}(x)\right],&\mbox{if $\max_{x\in [0,\infty)}G^{\mathcal{E}}_{i,j}(x)\neq 0$},\\
			h^{\mc{E}}_{i,j},&\mbox{otherwise},
		\end{cases}
	\end{align*}
	where $h^{\mc{A}}_{i,j}$, $h^{\mc{C}}_{i,j}$, and $h^{\mc{A}}_{i,j}$ are to be determined. It is crucial to discuss the case $\max_{x\in [0,\infty)}G^{k}_{i,j}(x)=0$, for $k\in\{\mathcal{A},\mathcal{C},\mathcal{E}\}$. Consider, for instance, the case $\max_{x\in [0,\infty)}G^{\mc A}_{i,j}(x)=0$. This implies that $k^{y^*,z^*}(a_i,c_j)=0$ and
	\begin{align*}
		W^{y^*,z^*}(x,a_i,c_j)=\frac{c_j}{q}\left[1-e^{\theta_2(a_i,c_j)x}\right].
	\end{align*}
	To ensure that the continuity of $W^{y^*,z^*}(\cdot,a_i,c_j)$ is preserved at the threshold level $y^*_{i,j}$, it must hold that
	\begin{align*}
		0=W^{y^*,z^*}(y^*_{i,j},a_i,c_j)-W^{y^*,z^*}(y^*_{i,j},a_{i+1},c_j)=\frac{c_j}{q}\left[1-e^{\theta_2(a_i,c_j)y^*_{i,j}}\right]-W^{y^*,z^*}(y^*_{i,j},a_{i+1},c_j).
	\end{align*}
    Suppose $\frac{c_j}{q}\left[1-e^{\theta_2(a_i,c_j) x}\right]<W^{y^*,z^*}( x,a_{i+1},c_j)$ for all $x\in(0,\infty)$. This implies that decreasing the retention level to $a_{i+1}$ is more optimal for any $x>0$. Hence, we choose $y^*_{i,j}=0$. Otherwise, we set a tolerance level $\delta$ close to 0. We then choose the minimum $x\in(0,\infty)$ such that $\frac{c_j}{q}\left[1-e^{\theta_2(a_i,c_j) x}\right]-W^{y^*,z^*}( x,a_{i+1},c_j)<\delta$. A similar method can be made for the other cases. Write $p_{i,j}(x)=\frac{c_j}{q}\left[1-e^{\theta_2(a_i,c_j) x}\right]$. We then have the following:
	\begin{align*}
		h^{\mc{A}}_{i,j}&=
		\begin{cases}
			\min_{ x\in(0,\infty)}\left[p_{i,j}(x)-W^{y^*,z^*}( x,a_{i+1},c_j)<\delta\right],&\mbox{if $p_{i,j}(x)\geq W^{y^*,z^*}( x,a_{i+1},c_j)$,}\\
			0,&\mbox{otherwise},
		\end{cases}\\
		h^{\mc{C}}_{i,j}&=
		\begin{cases}
			\min_{ x\in(0,\infty)}\left[p_{i,j}(x)-W^{y^*,z^*}( x,a_{i},c_{j+1})<\delta\right],&\mbox{if $p_{i,j}(x)\geq W^{y^*,z^*}( x,a_{i},c_{j+1})$,}\\
			0,&\mbox{otherwise},
		\end{cases}\\
            h^{\mc{E}}_{i,j}&=
		\begin{cases}
		\min_{x\in(0,\infty)}\left[p_{i,j}(x)-W^{y^*,z^*}( x,a_{i+1},c_{j+1})<\delta\right],&\mbox{if $p_{i,j}(x)\geq W^{y^*,z^*}( x,a_{i+1},c_{j+1})$,}\\
		0,&\mbox{otherwise}.
		\end{cases}
	\end{align*}

	The optimal threshold functions $y^*_{i,j}$ and $z^*_{i,j}$ are given by:\\
		\textbf{Case 1:} If $\max_{x\in[0,\infty)}G^{\mathcal{A}}_{i,j}(x)>\max_{x\in[0,\infty)}G^{\mathcal{C}}_{i,j}(x)\vee\max_{x\in[0,\infty)}G^{\mathcal{E}}_{i,j}(x)$, then
		\begin{align*}
			y^*_{i,j}=g^{\mathcal{A}}_{i,j}\quad\mbox{and}\quad z^*_{i,j}=+\infty.
		\end{align*}
		\textbf{Case 2:} If $\max_{x\in[0,\infty)}G^{\mathcal{C}}_{i,j}(x)>\max_{x\in[0,\infty)}G^{\mathcal{A}}_{i,j}(x)\vee\max_{x\in[0,\infty)}G^{\mathcal{E}}_{i,j}(x)$, then
		\begin{align*}
			y^*_{i,j}=+\infty\quad\mbox{and}\quad z^*_{i,j}=g^{\mathcal{C}}_{i,j}.
		\end{align*}
		\textbf{Case 3:} If $\max_{x\in[0,\infty)}G^{\mathcal{E}}_{i,j}(x)>\max_{x\in[0,\infty)}G^{\mathcal{A}}_{i,j}(x)\vee\max_{x\in[0,\infty)}G^{\mathcal{C}}_{i,j}(x)$, then
		\begin{align*}
			y^*_{i,j}=z^*_{i,j}=g^{\mathcal{E}}_{i,j}.
		\end{align*}
		\textbf{Case 4:} If $\max_{x\in[0,\infty)}G^{\mathcal{A}}_{i,j}(x)=\max_{x\in[0,\infty)}G^{\mathcal{C}}_{i,j}(x)>\max_{x\in[0,\infty)}G^{\mathcal{E}}_{i,j}(x)$, then either
		\begin{align*}
			y^*_{i,j}=\begin{cases}
				g^{\mathcal{A}}_{i,j},&\mbox{if $g^{\mathcal{A}}_{i,j}\leq g^{\mathcal{C}}_{i,j}$}\\
				+\infty,&\mbox{otherwise}
			\end{cases}
			\quad\mbox{and}\quad
			z^*_{i,j}=\begin{cases}
				g^{\mathcal{C}}_{i,j},&\mbox{if $g^{\mathcal{C}}_{i,j}< g^{\mathcal{A}}_{i,j}$}\\
				+\infty,&\mbox{otherwise},
			\end{cases}
		\end{align*}
		or
		\begin{align*}
			y^*_{i,j}=\begin{cases}
				g^{\mathcal{A}}_{i,j},&\mbox{if $g^{\mathcal{A}}_{i,j}< g^{\mathcal{C}}_{i,j}$}\\
				+\infty,&\mbox{otherwise}
			\end{cases}
			\quad\mbox{and}\quad
			z^*_{i,j}=\begin{cases}
				g^{\mathcal{C}}_{i,j},&\mbox{if $g^{\mathcal{C}}_{i,j}\leq g^{\mathcal{A}}_{i,j}$}\\
				+\infty,&\mbox{otherwise},
			\end{cases}
		\end{align*}
		depending on whichever between decrease in proportion level rate and increase in dividend level rate is prioritized.\\
		\textbf{Case 5:} If $\max_{x\in[0,\infty)}G^{\mathcal{E}}_{i,j}(x)=\max_{x\in[0,\infty)}G^{\mathcal{A}}_{i,j}(x)>\max_{x\in[0,\infty)}G^{\mathcal{C}}_{i,j}(x)$, then
		\begin{align*}
			y^*_{i,j}=\begin{cases}
				g^{\mathcal{E}}_{i,j},&\mbox{if $g^{\mathcal{E}}_{i,j}\leq g^{\mathcal{A}}_{i,j}$}\\
				g^{\mathcal{A}}_{i,j},&\mbox{otherwise}
			\end{cases}
			\quad\mbox{and}\quad
			z^*_{i,j}=\begin{cases}
				g^{\mathcal{E}}_{i,j},&\mbox{if $g^{\mathcal{E}}_{i,j}\leq g^{\mathcal{A}}_{i,j}$}\\
				+\infty,&\mbox{otherwise}.
			\end{cases}
		\end{align*}
		\textbf{Case 6:} If $\max_{x\in[0,\infty)}G^{\mathcal{E}}_{i,j}(x)=\max_{x\in[0,\infty)}G^{\mathcal{C}}_{i,j}(x)>\max_{x\in[0,\infty)}G^{\mathcal{A}}_{i,j}(x)$, then
		\begin{align*}
			y^*_{i,j}=\begin{cases}
				g^{\mathcal{E}}_{i,j},&\mbox{if $g^{\mathcal{E}}_{i,j}\leq g^{\mathcal{C}}_{i,j}$}\\
				+\infty,&\mbox{otherwise}
			\end{cases}
			\quad\mbox{and}\quad
			z^*_{i,j}=\begin{cases}
				g^{\mathcal{E}}_{i,j},&\mbox{if $g^{\mathcal{E}}_{i,j}\leq g^{\mathcal{C}}_{i,j}$}\\
				g^{\mathcal{C}}_{i,j},&\mbox{otherwise}.
			\end{cases}
		\end{align*}
		\textbf{Case 7:} If $\max_{x\in[0,\infty)}G^{\mathcal{E}}_{i,j}(x)=\max_{x\in[0,\infty)}G^{\mathcal{A}}_{i,j}(x)=\max_{x\in[0,\infty)}G^{\mathcal{C}}_{i,j}(x)$, then
		\begin{align*}
			y^*_{i,j}=\begin{cases}
				g^{\mathcal{E}}_{i,j},&\mbox{if $g^{\mathcal{E}}_{i,j}\leq g^{\mathcal{A}}_{i,j}\wedge g^{\mathcal{C}}_{i,j}$}\\
				g^{\mathcal{A}}_{i,j},&\mbox{if $g^{\mathcal{A}}_{i,j}< g^{\mathcal{E}}_{i,j}\wedge g^{\mathcal{C}}_{i,j}$ or $g^{\mathcal{A}}_{i,j}= g^{\mathcal{C}}_{i,j}< g^{\mathcal{E}}_{i,j}$}\\
				+\infty,&\mbox{otherwise,}
			\end{cases}\quad\mbox{and}\quad
			z^*_{i,j}=\begin{cases}
				g^{\mathcal{E}}_{i,j},&\mbox{if $g^{\mathcal{E}}_{i,j}\leq g^{\mathcal{A}}_{i,j}\wedge g^{\mathcal{C}}_{i,j}$}\\
				g^{\mathcal{C}}_{i,j},&\mbox{if $g^{\mathcal{C}}_{i,j}< g^{\mathcal{E}}_{i,j}\wedge g^{\mathcal{A}}_{i,j}$}\\
				+\infty,&\mbox{otherwise,}
			\end{cases}
		\end{align*}
		or
		\begin{align*}
			y^*_{i,j}=\begin{cases}
				g^{\mathcal{E}}_{i,j},&\mbox{if $g^{\mathcal{E}}_{i,j}\leq g^{\mathcal{A}}_{i,j}\wedge g^{\mathcal{C}}_{i,j}$}\\
				g^{\mathcal{A}}_{i,j},&\mbox{if $g^{\mathcal{A}}_{i,j}< g^{\mathcal{E}}_{i,j}\wedge g^{\mathcal{C}}_{i,j}$}\\
				+\infty,&\mbox{otherwise,}
			\end{cases}\quad\mbox{and}\quad
			z^*_{i,j}=\begin{cases}
				g^{\mathcal{E}}_{i,j},&\mbox{if $g^{\mathcal{E}}_{i,j}\leq g^{\mathcal{A}}_{i,j}\wedge g^{\mathcal{C}}_{i,j}$}\\
				g^{\mathcal{C}}_{i,j},&\mbox{if $g^{\mathcal{C}}_{i,j}< g^{\mathcal{E}}_{i,j}\wedge g^{\mathcal{A}}_{i,j}$ or $g^{\mathcal{C}}_{i,j}= g^{\mathcal{A}}_{i,j}< g^{\mathcal{E}}_{i,j}$}\\
				+\infty,&\mbox{otherwise,}
			\end{cases}
		\end{align*}
		depending on whichever between decrease in proportion level rate and increase in dividend level rate is prioritized.


	Consider three auxiliary problems of looking for the smallest solution $U^*_{\mc{A}}$, $U^*_{\mc{C}}$, and $U^*_{\mc{E}}$ of the equation $\mc{L}^{a_i,c_j}(U)=0$ in $[0,\infty)$ with boundary condition $U(0)=0$ above $\wopt(\cdot,a_{i+1},c_j)$, $\wopt(\cdot,a_{i},c_{j+1})$, and $\wopt(\cdot,a_{i+1},c_{j+1})$, respectively. Once we find $\mc{U}^*_{\mc{A}}$, $\mc{U}^*_{\mc{C}}$, and $\mc{U}^*_{\mc{E}}$, we get, respectively,
	\begin{align*}
		y^*_{i,j}=\begin{cases}
			0,&\mbox{if $U^*_{\mc{A}}(\cdot)>\wopt(\cdot,a_{i+1},c_j)$ in $(0,\infty)$}\\
			\inf\{y>0:U^*_{\mc{A}}(\cdot)=\wopt(\cdot,a_{i+1},c_j)\},&\mbox{otherwise},
		\end{cases}
	\end{align*}
	\begin{align*}
		z^*_{i,j}=\begin{cases}
			0,&\mbox{if $U^*_{\mc{C}}(\cdot)>\wopt(\cdot,a_{i},c_{j+1})$ in $(0,\infty)$}\\
			\inf\{y>0:U^*_{\mc{C}}(\cdot)=\wopt(\cdot,a_{i},c_{j+1})\},&\mbox{otherwise},
		\end{cases}
	\end{align*}
	and
	\begin{align*}
		y^*_{i,j}=z^*_{ij}=\begin{cases}
			0,&\mbox{if $U^*_{\mc{E}}(\cdot)>\wopt(\cdot,a_{i+1},c_{j+1})$ in $(0,\infty)$}\\
			\inf\{y>0:U^*_{\mc{E}}(\cdot)=\wopt(\cdot,a_{i+1},c_{j+1})\},&\mbox{otherwise}.
		\end{cases}
	\end{align*}
	We then have 
	\begin{align*}
		\begin{cases}
			\wopt(x,a_i,c_j)=U^*_{\mc{A}}(x),&\mbox{for $x<y^*_{i,j}$}\\
			\wopt(x,a_i,c_j)=\wopt(x,a_{i+1},c_j),&\mbox{for $x\geq y^*_{i,j}$},
		\end{cases}
	\end{align*}
	\begin{align*}
		\begin{cases}
			\wopt(x,a_i,c_j)=U^*_{\mc{C}}(x),&\mbox{for $x<z^*_{i,j}$}\\
			\wopt(x,a_i,c_j)=\wopt(x,a_{i},c_{j+1}),&\mbox{for $x\geq z^*_{i,j}$},
		\end{cases}
	\end{align*}
	and
	\begin{align*}
		\begin{cases}
			\wopt(x,a_i,c_j)=U^*_{\mc{E}}(x),&\mbox{for $x<y^*_{i,j}=z^*_{i,j}$}\\
			\wopt(x,a_i,c_j)=\wopt(x,a_{i+1},c_{j+1}),&\mbox{for $x\geq y^*_{i,j}=z^*_{i,j}$}.
		\end{cases}
	\end{align*}

%	We now prove the existence of $U^*_{\mc{A}}$, $U^*_{\mc{C}}$, and $U^*_{\mc{E}}$.
	\begin{mylm}\label{lemma on existence of U}
		$U^*_{\mc{A}}$, $U^*_{\mc{C}}$, and $U^*_{\mc{E}}$ exist.
	\end{mylm}

	The following result states the differentiability and continuity of $W^{y^*,z^*}$. 

	\begin{mylm}\label{W is cont diff}
		$\wopt(x,a_i,c_j)$ is infinitely continuously differentiable at all $x\in[0,\infty)\setminus\{y^*_{k,j}:k=i,\ldots,m-1\}\cup\{z^*(c_{i,l}):l=j,\ldots,n-1\}$ and is continuously differentiable at the points $y^*_{k,j}$ and $z^*_{i,l}$ for $k=i,\ldots,m-1$ and $l=j,\ldots,n-1$.
	\end{mylm}
        \begin{proof}
		It is clear that $W^{y^*,z^*}(x,a_m,c_n)$ is infinitely continuously differentiable at all $x$. By construction and a recursive argument, the result follows.
	\end{proof}

	The next lemma proves an inequality for $\wopt_{xx}$ at a neighborhood of threshold values. The result can be used to prove that $\wopt$ is a viscosity solution since $\wopt_{xx}$ may not exist as stated in Lemma \ref{W is cont diff}.

	\begin{mylm}\label{Wxx inequality}
		For $1\leq i\leq m-1$ and $1\leq j\leq n-1$,
		\begin{align*}
			\wopt_{xx}(y^{*-}_{i,j},a_i,c_j)-\wopt_{xx}(y^{*+}_{i,j},a_i,c_j)\geq 0,
		\end{align*}
		and
		\begin{align*}
			\wopt_{xx}(z^{*-}_{i,j},a_i,c_j)-\wopt_{xx}(z^{*+}_{i,j},a_i,c_j)\geq 0.
		\end{align*}
	\end{mylm}

	We can now state the main result for the finite set case. It shows that the constructed candidate strategy yields the optimal value function.

	\begin{myth}\label{y and z optimal implies verification}
		If $y^*$ and $z^*$ are the optimal threshold functions, then $W^{y^*,z^*}(x,a_i,c_j)$ is the optimal function $V^{a_i,c_j}(x)$ in (\ref{value function orig}) for $i=1,\ldots,m$ and $j=1,\ldots, n$.
	\end{myth}


\begin{myrem}\label{remark on extended threshold strategy}
	Given $y_{i,j},z_{i,j}:\mc{A}\times\mc{C}\to[0,\infty]$, we have defined in \eqref{threshold strategy} a threshold strategy $\pi^{y,z}:=\{(A_{x,a_i,c_j},C_{x,a_i,c_j})\}_{(x,a_i,c_j)\in[0,\infty)\times\mathcal{A}\times\mathcal{C}}$, where $(A_{x,a_i},C_{x,c_j})\in\Pi^{\mathcal{A},\mathcal{C}}_{x,a_i,c_j}$ for $1\leq i\leq m$ and $1\leq j\leq n$. This strategy can be extended to
	\begin{align*}
		\tilde{\pi}^{y,z}:=\{(A_{x,a,c},C_{x,a,c})\}_{(x,a,c)\in[0,\infty)\times[a_m,a_1]\times[c_1,c_n]}
	\end{align*}
	as follows:
	\begin{itemize}
		\item[(i)] If $a\in(a_{i+1},a_{i})$, $c\in(c_j,c_{j+1})$, and $x<y_{i,j}\wedge z_{i,j}$, retain $a$ of the incoming claims and pay dividends with rate $c$ until ruin time. If the current surplus reaches $y_{i,j}$ first before ruin time, follow $\left(A_{y_{i,j},a_{i+1},c},C_{y_{i,j},a_{i+1},c}\right)\in\Pi^{\mc{A},\mc{C}}_{x,a_{i+1},c}$. If the current surplus reaches $z_{i,j}$ first before ruin time, follow $\left(A_{z_{i,j},a,c_{j+1}},C_{z_{i,j},a,c_{j+1}}\right)\in\Pi^{\mc{A},\mc{C}}_{x,a,c_{j+1}}$. If the current surplus reaches $y_{i,j}$ and $z_{i,j}$ simultaneously before ruin time, follow $\left(A_{y_{i,j},a_{i+1},c_{j+1}},C_{y_{i,j},a_{i+1},c_{j+1}}\right)\in\Pi^{\mc{A},\mc{C}}_{x,a_{i+1},c_{j+1}}$.

		\item[(ii)] If $a\in(a_{i+1},a_{i})$, $c\in(c_j,c_{j+1})$, and $x\geq y_{i,j}$, follow $\left(A_{x,a_{i+1},c_j},C_{x,a_{i+1},c_j}\right)\in\Pi^{\mc{A},\mc{C}}_{x,a_{i+1},c_j}$. More precisely, if $(x,a,c)\in[0,y_{i,j})\times(a_{i+1},a_i)\times(c_j,c_{j+1})$ with $y_{i,j}<z_{i,j}$, then $(A_{x,a,c},C_{x,a,c})\in\Pi^{\mc{A},\mc{C}}_{x,a,c}$ is defined as $(A_{x,a,c},C_{x,a,c})_t=(a,c)$ and so $X^{(A_{x,a,c},C_{x,a,c})}_t=X_t-ct$ for 
		\begin{align*}
			t<\tau\wedge\tau^{\mc{A}}_i\quad\mbox{where $\tau^{\mc{A}}_i:=\min\{s:X^{(A_{x,a,c},C_{x,a,c})}_t=y_{i,j}\}$},
		\end{align*}
		and $(A_{x,a,c},C_{x,a,c})_t=(A_{y_{i,j},a_{i+1},c_j},C_{y_{i,j},a_{i+1},c_j})_{t-\tau^{\mc{A}}_i}\in\Pi^{\mc{A},\mc{C}}_{y_{i,j},a_{i+1},c_j}$ for $t\geq\tau_i$. Finally, $(A_{x,a,c},C_{x,a,c})=(A_{x,a_{i+1},c_j},C_{x,a_{i+1},c_j})\in\Pi^{\mc{A},\mc{C}}_{x,a_{i+1},c_j}$ for $(x,a,c)\in[y_{i,j},\infty)\times(a_{i+1},a_i)\times(c_j,c_{j+1})$.

		\item[(iii)] If $a\in(a_{i+1},a_{i})$, $c\in(c_{j},c_{j+1})$ and $x\geq z_{i,j}$, follow $\left(A_{x,a_i,c_{j+1}},C_{x,a_i,c_{j+1}}\right)\in\Pi^{\mc{A},\mc{C}}_{x,a_i,c_{j+1}}$. More precisely, if $(x,a,c)\in[0,z_{i,j})\times(a_{i+1},a_i)\times(c_j,c_{j+1})$ with $z_{i,j}<y_{i,j}$, then $(A_{x,a,c},C_{x,a,c})\in\Pi^{\mc{A},\mc{C}}_{x,a,c}$ is defined as $(A_{x,a,c},C_{x,,a,c})_t=(a,c)$ and so $X^{(A_{x,a,c},C_{x,a,c})}_t=X_t-ct$ for 
		\begin{align*}
			t<\tau\wedge\tau^{\mc{C}}_j\quad\mbox{where $\tau^{\mc{C}}_j:=\min\{s:X^{(A_{x,a,c},C_{x,a,c})}_t=z_{i,j}\}$},
		\end{align*}
		and $(A_{x,a,c},C_{x,a,c})_t=(A_{z_{i,j},a_{i},c_{j+1}},C_{z_{i,j},a_i,c_{j+1}})_{t-\tau^{\mc{C}}_j}\in\Pi^{\mc{A},\mc{C}}_{y_{i,j},a_{i},c_{j+1}}$ for $t\geq\tau^{\mc{C}}_j$. Finally, $(A_{x,a,c},C_{x,a,c})=(A_{x,a_{i},c_{j+1}},C_{x,a_i,c_{j+1}})\in\Pi^{\mc{A},\mc{C}}_{x,a_{i},c_{j+1}}$ for $(x,a,c)\in[z_{i,j},\infty)\times(a_{i+1},a_i)\times(c_j,c_{j+1})$.

		\item[(iv)] If $a\in(a_{i+1},a_{i})$, $c\in(c_{j},c_{j+1})$ and $x\geq y_{i,j}=z_{i,j}$, follow $\left(A_{x,a_{i+1},c_{j+1}},C_{x,a_{i+1},c_{j+1}}\right)\in\Pi^{\mc{A},\mc{C}}_{x,a_{i+1},c_{j+1}}$. More precisely, if $(x,a,c)\in[0,z_{i,j})\times(a_{i+1},a_i)\times(c_j,c_{j+1})$ with $z_{i,j}=y_{i,j}$, then $(A_{x,a,c},C_{x,a,c})\in\Pi^{\mc{A},\mc{C}}_{x,a,c}$ is defined as $(A_{x,a,c},C_{x,a,c})_t=(a,c)$ and so $X^{(A_{x,a,c},C_{x,a,c})}_t=X_t-ct$ for 
		\begin{align*}
			t<\tau\wedge\tau^{\mc{E}}_{i,j}\quad\mbox{where $\tau^{\mc{E}}_{i,j}:=\min\{s:X^{(A_{x,a,c},C_{x,a,c})}_t=z_{i,j}\}$},
		\end{align*}
		and $(A_{x,a,c},C_{x,a,c})_t=(A_{z_{i,j},a_{i+1},c_{j+1}},C_{z_{i,j},a_{i+1},c_{j+1}})_{t-\tau^{\mc{E}}_{i,j}}\in\Pi^{\mc{A},\mc{C}}_{y_{i,j},a_{i+1},c_{j+1}}$ for $t\geq\tau^{\mc{E}}_{i,j}$. Finally, we have that $(A_{x,a,c},C_{x,a,c})=(A_{x,a_{i+1},c_{j+1}},C_{x,a_{i+1},c_{j+1}})\in\Pi^{\mc{A},\mc{C}}_{x,a_{i+1},c_{j+1}}$ for $(x,a,c)\in[z_{i,j},\infty)\times(a_{i+1},a_i)\times(c_j,c_{j+1})$.
	\end{itemize}
	The value function of the extended stationary strategy $\tilde\pi^{y,z}$ is defined as
	\begin{align*}
		J^{\tilde{\pi}^{y,z}}(x,a,c):=J(x;A_{x,a,c},C_{x,a,c}):[0,\infty)\times[a_m,a_1]\times[c_1,c_n]\to\mathbb{R}.
	\end{align*}
\end{myrem}

\subsection{Curve Strategies}

Consider the set
\begin{align*}
	\mc{B}:=\left\{\zeta\text{ s.t. }\zeta:[\ul{a},\ol{a}]\times[\ul{c},\ol{c})\to[0,\infty)\text{ is Riemann integrable and càdlàg with }\lim_{c\to\ol{c}^-}\zeta(a,c)<\infty\right\}.
\end{align*}
Write $\zeta:=(\zeta^{\mc{A}},\zeta^{\mc{C}})\in\mc{B}^2$. Define the $\zeta$-value function $W^{\zeta^{\mc{A}}}$ associated to two sets of curves given by
\begin{align*}
	\mc{R}^{\mc{A}}(\zeta):=\left\{(\zeta^{\mc{A}}(a),a,c):(a,c)\in[\ul{a},\ol{a}]\times[\ul c,\ol c]\right\}\subset[0,\infty)\times[\ul{a},\ol{a}]\times[\ul c, \ol c],
\end{align*}
and
\begin{align*}
	\mc{R}^{\mc{C}}(\zeta):=\left\{(\zeta^{\mc{C}}(a),a,c):(a,c)\in[\ul{a},\ol{a}]\times[\ul c,\ol c]\right\}\subset[0,\infty)\times[\ul{a},\ol{a}]\times[\ul c, \ol c].
\end{align*}
The curve $\mc{R}^{\mc{A}}(\zeta)$ splits the state space $[0,\infty)\times[\ul{a},\ol{a}]\times[\ul c,\ol c]$ into two connected regions: $\mc{NC}^{\mc{A}}(\zeta):=\left\{(x,a,c)\in[0,\infty)\times[\ul{a},\ol{a}]\times[\ul c,\ol c]:x<\zeta^{\mc{A}}(a,c)\right\}$ where the incoming claims is ceded to the reinsurer at a constant rate, and $\mc{CH}^{\mc{A}}(\zeta):=\left\{(x,a,c)\in[0,\infty)\times[\ul{a},\ol{a}]\times[\ul c,\ol c]:x\geq\zeta^{\mc{A}}(a,c)\right\}$ where the incoming claims ceded to the reinsurer increases. On the other hand, the curve $\mc{R}^{\mc{C}}(\zeta)$ splits the state space $[0,\infty)\times[\ul a,\ol a]\times[\ul{c},\ol{c}]$ into two connected regions: $\mc{NC}^{\mc{C}}(\zeta):=\left\{(x,a,c)\in[0,\infty)\times[\ul a,\ol a]\times[\ul{c},\ol{c}]:x<\zeta^{\mc{C}}(a,c)\right\}$ where dividends are paid at a constant rate, and $\mc{CH}^{\mc{C}}(\zeta):=\left\{(x,a,c)\in[0,\infty)\times[\ul a,\ol a]\times[\ul{c},\ol{c}]:x\geq\zeta^{\mc{C}}(a,c)\right\}$ where the dividend rate increases.


Consider the auxiliary functions $b^a_0,b^a_1,b^c_0,b^c_1:(0,\infty)\times[\ul{a},\ol{a}]\times[\ul{c},\ol{c}]\to\mathbb{R}$ defined as
\begin{equation}\label{b0 and b1 definition}
	\begin{aligned}
		b^a_0(x,a,c)&:=\frac{\frac{c}{q}e^{\theta_2(a,c)x}\frac{\partial}{\partial a}\theta_2(a,c)x}{e^{\theta_1(a,c)x}-e^{\theta_2(a,c)x}}\\
		b^a_1(x,a,c)&:=\frac{\left(\frac{\partial}{\partial a}\theta_2(a,c)e^{\theta_2(a,c)x}-\frac{\partial}{\partial a}\theta_1(a,c)e^{\theta_1(a,c)x}\right)x}{e^{\theta_1(a,c)x}-e^{\theta_2(a,c)x}}\\
		b^c_0(x,a,c)&:=\frac{-\frac{1}{q}\left(1-e^{\theta_2(a,c)x}\right)+\frac{c}{q}e^{\theta_2(a,c)x}\frac{\partial}{\partial c}\theta_2(a,c)x}{e^{\theta_1(a,c)x}-e^{\theta_2(a,c)x}}\\
		b^c_1(x,a,c)&:=\frac{\left(\frac{\partial}{\partial c}\theta_2(a,c)e^{\theta_2(a,c)x}-\frac{\partial}{\partial c}\theta_1(a,c)e^{\theta_1(a,c)x}\right)x}{e^{\theta_1(a,c)x}-e^{\theta_2(a,c)x}}.
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
The auxiliary functions are not defined at $x=0$, so we extend the definition as
\begin{align*}
	b^a_0(0,a,c)&=\lim_{x\to 0^+}b^a_0(x,a,c):=\frac{\frac{c}{q}\frac{\partial}{\partial a}\theta_2(a,c)}{\theta_1(a,c)-\theta_2(a,c)}\\
	b^a_1(0,a,c)&=\lim_{x\to 0^+}b^a_1(x,a,c):=\frac{\frac{\partial}{\partial a}\theta_2(a,c)-\frac{\partial}{\partial a}\theta_1(a,c)}{\theta_1(a,c)-\theta_2(a,c)}\\
	b^c_0(0,a,c)&=\lim_{x\to 0^+}b^c_0(x,a,c):=\frac{\frac{1}{q}\theta_2(a,c)+\frac{c}{q}\frac{\partial}{\partial a}\theta_2(a,c)}{\theta_1(a,c)-\theta_2(a,c)}\\
	b^c_1(0,a,c)&=\lim_{x\to 0^+}b^c_1(x,a,c):=\frac{\frac{\partial}{\partial c}\theta_2(a,c)-\frac{\partial}{\partial c}\theta_1(a,c)}{\theta_1(a,c)-\theta_2(a,c)}.
\end{align*}

The following result provides the candidate optimal strategies for the closed interval case. It also gives a condition that maximizes the expected payoff.

\begin{mypr}\label{definition H zeta}
	Given $\zeta\in\mc{B}^2$, the unique continuous function
	\begin{align*}
		H^{\zeta}:=\left\{(x,a,c)\in[0,\infty)\times[\ul{a},\ol{a}]\times[\ul{c},\ol{c}]:x<\zeta^{\mc{A}}(a,c)\wedge\zeta^{\mc{C}}(a,c)\right\}\to[0,\infty)
	\end{align*}
	which satisfies for any $(a,c)\in[\ul{a},\ol{a}]\times[\ul{c},\ol{c})$ that
	\begin{align*}
		\mc{L}^{a,c}(H^{\zeta})(x,a,c)=0\quad\mbox{for $x\in[0,\zeta^{\mc{A}}(a,c)\wedge\zeta^{\mc{C}}(a,c)]$}
	\end{align*}
	with boundary conditions $H^{\zeta}(0,a,c)=0$, $H^{\zeta}(x,\ul{a},\ol{c})=V^{\{\ul{a}\},\{\ol{c}\}}(x,\ul{a},\ol{c})$, $H^{\zeta}_a(\zeta^{\mc{A}}(a,c),a,c)=0$, and \newline$H^{\zeta}_c(\zeta^{\mc{C}}(a,c),a,c)=0$ at the points of continuity of $\zeta^{\mc{A}}$ and $\zeta^{\mc{C}}$ is given by
	\begin{align}\label{H zeta}
		H^{\zeta}(x,a,c)=\frac{c}{q}\left(1-e^{\theta_2(a,c)x}\right)+K^{\zeta}(a,c)\left(e^{\theta_1(a,c)x}-e^{\theta_2(a,c)x}\right),
	\end{align}
	where
	\small\begin{align}\label{H zeta solution a}
		K^{\zeta}(a,c)&=-\int_c^{\ol{c}}\exp\left(-\int_c^tb_1^c(\zeta^{\mc{C}}(a,s),a,s)ds\right)b_0^c(\zeta^{\mc{C}}(a,t),a,t)dt+M(a)\exp\left(\int_{\ul{c}}^cb_1^c(\zeta^{\mc{C}}(a,s),a,s)ds\right),
	\end{align}\normalsize
	if $\zeta^{\mc{A}}(a,c)\wedge\zeta^{\mc{C}}(a,c)=\zeta^{\mc{C}}(a,c)$ with $M(a)$ a function of $a$ that satisfies $M(\ul{a})=0$, and
	\small\begin{align}\label{H zeta solution c}
		K^{\zeta}(a,c)&=\int_{\ul{a}}^{a}\exp\left(-\int_a^tb_1^a(\zeta^{\mc{A}}(s,c),s,c)ds\right)b_0^a(\zeta^{\mc{A}}(t,c),t,c)dt+N(c)\exp\left(\int_{\ul{a}}^ab_1^a(\zeta^{\mc{A}}(s,c),s,c)ds\right),
	\end{align}\normalsize
	if $\zeta^{\mc{A}}(a,c)\wedge\zeta^{\mc{C}}(a,c)=\zeta^{\mc{A}}(a,c)$ with $N(c)$ a function of $c$ that satisfies $N(\ol{c})=0$. Moreover, $K^{\zeta}$ satisfies $K^{\zeta}(\ul{a},\ol{c})=0$, is differentiable in $a$ and $c$, and satisfies
	\begin{align}\label{H zeta partial a}
		\frac{\partial K^{\zeta}}{\partial a}(a,c)=b_0^a(\zeta^{\mc{A}}(a,c),a,c)+b_1^a(\zeta^{\mc{A}}(a,c),a,c)K^{\zeta}(a,c)
	\end{align}
	at the points where $\zeta^{\mc{A}}$ is continuous, and
	\begin{align}\label{H zeta partial c}
		\frac{\partial K^{\zeta}}{\partial c}(a,c)=b_0^c(\zeta^{\mc{C}}(a,c),a,c)+b_1^c(\zeta^{\mc{C}}(a,c),a,c)K^{\zeta}(a,c)
	\end{align}
	at the points where $\zeta^{\mc{C}}$ is continuous.
\end{mypr}
The above result is analogous to Proposition \ref{Recursive formula W}. In the previous subsection, we have introduced a method to determine the optimal threshold functions after maximizing $k^{y,z}(a_i,c_j)$ defined in Proposition \ref{Recursive formula W}. In Proposition \ref{definition H zeta}, we also maximize $K^{\zeta}(a,c)$, which can be done by solving the differential equations \eqref{H zeta partial a} and \eqref{H zeta partial c}. 

Given $\zeta\in\mc{B}^2$, define the $\zeta$-value function
\begin{equation}\label{zeta value function}
	\begin{aligned}
		W^{\zeta}(x,a,c):=
		\begin{cases}
			H^{\zeta}(x,a,c),&\mbox{if $(x,a,c)\in\mc{NC}^{\mc{A}}(\zeta)\cap \mc{NC}^{\mc{C}}(\zeta)$}\\
			H^{\zeta}(x,A(x,a,c),c),&\mbox{if $(x,a,c)\in\mc{CH}^{\mc A}(\zeta)\cap \mc{NC}^{\mc C}(\zeta)$}\\
			H^{\zeta}(x,a,C(x,a,c)),&\mbox{if $(x,a,c)\in\mc{CH}^{\mc C}(\zeta)\cap\mc{NC}^{\mc A}(\zeta)$}\\
			H^{\zeta}(x,A(x,a,c),C(x,a,c)),&\mbox{if $(x,a,c)\in\mc{CH}^{\mc A}(\zeta)\cap\mc{CH}^{\mc C}(\zeta)$},
		\end{cases}
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $H^{\zeta}$ is defined in Proposition \ref{definition H zeta},
\begin{align*}
	A(x,a,c)&:=\min\left\{h\in[\ul{a},a]:\zeta^{\mc{A}}(d,c)\leq x\mbox{ for $d\in (h,a]$}\right\}
\end{align*}
in the case that $x\geq \zeta^{\mc{A}}(a,c)$ and $(a,c)\in[\ul{a},\ol{a}]\times[\ul{c},\ol{c})$, and
\begin{align*}
	C(x,a,c)&:=\max\left\{h\in[c,\ol{c}]:\zeta^{\mc{C}}(a,d)\leq x\mbox{ for $d\in [c,h)$}\right\}
\end{align*}
in the case that $x\geq \zeta^{\mc{C}}(a,c)$ and $(a,c)\in[\ul{a},\ol{a}]\times[\ul{c},\ol{c})$.

The next result relates Remark \ref{remark on extended threshold strategy} and the threshold strategy introduced in the previous subsection. 
\begin{mypr}\label{prop on stationary value function}
	Given $y,z:\mc{A}\times\mc{C}\to[0,\infty]$ and the corresponding extended threshold strategy $\tilde\pi^{y,z}$ defined in Remark \ref{remark on extended threshold strategy}, consider the associated step functions $\zeta^{\mc{A}},\zeta^{\mc{C}}\in\mc{B}$ defined as
	\begin{align*}
		\zeta^{\mc{A}}(a,c):=\sum_{i=1}^{m-1}\left[\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}\tilde y(a_i,c_j)\cdot\mathds{1}_{(a_{i+1},a_i]}(a)\cdot\mathds{1}_{[c_j,c_{j+1})}(c)+\tilde y(a_i,c_j)\cdot\mathds{1}_{(a_{i+1},a_i]}(a)\cdot\mathds{1}_{\{\ol{c}\}}(c)\right]
	\end{align*}
	and
	\begin{align*}
		\zeta^{\mc{C}}(a,c):=\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}\left[\sum_{i=1}^{m-1}\tilde z(a_i,c_j)\cdot\mathds{1}_{(a_{i+1},a_i]}(a)\cdot\mathds{1}_{[c_j,c_{j+1})}(c)+\tilde z(a_i,c_j)\cdot\mathds{1}_{\{\ul{a}\}}(a)\cdot\mathds{1}_{[c_j,c_{j+1})}(c)\right],
	\end{align*}
	where
	\begin{align*}
		\tilde{y}(a_i,c_j)=
		\begin{cases}
			y(a_i,c_j)&\mbox{if $y(a_i,c_j)<\infty$}\\
			M&\mbox{if $y(a_i,c_j)=\infty$}
		\end{cases}\quad\mbox{and}\quad
		\tilde{z}(a_i,c_j)=
		\begin{cases}
			z(a_i,c_j)&\mbox{if $z(a_i,c_j)<\infty$}\\
			M&\mbox{if $z(a_i,c_j)=\infty$}
		\end{cases}
	\end{align*}
	for some large $M>0$. Then the stationary value function of the extended threshold strategy $\tilde\pi^{y,z}$ is given by
	\begin{align*}
		J^{\tilde\pi^{y,z}}(x,a,c)=W^{\zeta}(x,a,c).
	\end{align*}
\end{mypr}

The next result states a convergence result for the step functions defined in the previous proposition.

\begin{mypr}\label{prop on step functions}
	Given $\zeta^{\mc{A}},\zeta^{\mc{C}}\in\mc{B}$, there exist a sequence of left-continuous step functions $\zeta^{\mc{A}}_m:[\ul{a},\ol{a}]\times[\ul{c},\ol{c})\to[0,\infty)$ and a sequence of right-continuous step functions $\zeta^{\mc{C}}_n:[\ul{a},\ol{a}]\times[\ul{c},\ol{c})\to[0,\infty)$ such that $W^{\zeta^{\mc{A}}_m,\zeta^{\mc{C}}_n}(x,a,c)$ converges uniformly to $W^{\zeta}(x,a,c)$.
\end{mypr}


We now look for the maximum of $W^{\zeta}$ among $\zeta\in\mc{B}^2$. We will show that if there exists $\zeta_0:=(\zeta^{\mc{A}}_0,\zeta^{\mc{C}}_0)\in\mc{B}^2$ such that
\begin{equation}\label{K zeta_0}
	\begin{aligned}
		K^{\zeta_0}({a},\ul c)&=\max\left\{K^{\zeta}({a},\ul{c}):\zeta\in\mc{B}^2\right\},\\
		K^{\zeta_0}(\ol{a}, c)&=\max\left\{K^{\zeta}(\ol{a},{c}):\zeta\in\mc{B}^2\right\},
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
then $W^{\zeta_0}(x,a,c)\geq W^{\zeta}(x,a,c)$ for all $(x,a,c)\in[0,\infty)\times[\ul{a},\ol{a}]\times[\ul{c},\ol{c})$ and $\zeta\in\mc{B}^2$. This result follows from \eqref{H zeta} and the next claim, in which we show that the function $\zeta_0$ which maximizes \eqref{K zeta_0} also maximizes $K^{\zeta}(a,c)$ for any $(a,c)\in[\ul{a},\ol{a}]\times[\ul{c},\ol{c})$.

The following result states that $\max\left\{K^{\zeta}(a,c):\zeta\in\mc{B}^2_{a,c}\right\}$ exists.

\begin{mypr}\label{prop on K zeta 0}
	For a given $(a,c)\in[\ul{a},\ol{a}]\times[\ul{c},\ol{c})$, define
	\begin{align*}
		\mc{B}_{a,c}:=\left\{\zeta\text{ s.t. }\zeta:[\ul{a},a]\times[c,\ol{c})\to[0,\infty)\text{ is Riemann integrable and càdlàg with }\lim_{c\to\ol{c}^-}\zeta(c)<\infty\right\}.
	\end{align*}
	Suppose $\zeta_0:=(\zeta^{\mc{A}}_0,\zeta^{\mc{C}}_0)\in\mc{B}^2$ satisfies \eqref{K zeta_0}. Then, for any $(a,c)\in[\ul{a},\ol{a}]\times[\ul{c},\ol{c})$,
	\begin{align*}
		K^{\zeta_0}(a,c)=\max\left\{K^{\zeta}(a,c):\zeta\in\mc{B}^2_{a,c}\right\}.
	\end{align*}
\end{mypr}

The next lemma revolves around the properties of the function $\frac{\partial }{\partial x}b_1^c$ and its sign.

\begin{mylm}\label{nonpositive derivative}
	The function $\frac{\partial b^c_1}{\partial x}(x,a,c):(0,\infty)\times[\ul{a},\ol{a}]\times[\ul{c},\ol{c}]\to\mathbb{R}$ is infinitely continuously differentiable and $\frac{\partial b^c_1}{\partial x}(x,a,c)$ satisfies
	\begin{align*}
		\frac{\partial b^c_1}{\partial x}(x,a,c)<0\quad\mbox{for $x>0$}\quad\mbox{and}\quad\frac{\partial b^c_1}{\partial x}(0+,a,c)<0
	\end{align*}
	for any $(a,c)\in[\ul{a},\ol{a}]\times[\ul{c},\ol{c}]$.
\end{mylm}

The following result provides a formula for the function $K^{\zeta_0}(a,c)$.

\begin{mypr}\label{zeta implicit equations}
	Suppose the function $\zeta_0$ defined in \eqref{K zeta_0} exists. If $\zeta^{\mc{A}}(a,c)\wedge\zeta^{\mc{C}}(a,c)=\zeta^{\mc{C}}(a,c)$, then $K^{\zeta_0}(a,c)$ satisfies
	\begin{align}\label{implicit for zeta c}
		K^{\zeta_0}(a,c)=-\frac{\frac{\partial}{\partial x}b_0^{c}(\zeta_0^{\mc{C}}(a,c),a,c)}{\frac{\partial}{\partial x}b_1^{c}(\zeta_0^{\mc{C}}(a,c),a,c)}+M(a)\exp\left(\int_{\ul c}^cb_1^c(\zeta^{\mc{C}}(a,s),a,s)ds\right)
	\end{align}
	for all $(a,c)\in[\ul a, \ol a]\times[\ul c,\ol c)$. If $\zeta^{\mc{A}}(a,c)\wedge\zeta^{\mc{C}}(a,c)=\zeta^{\mc{A}}(a,c)$ and $\frac{\partial}{\partial x}b_1^a(\zeta_0^{\mc{A}}(a,c),a,c)\neq 0$, then $K^{\zeta_0}(a,c)$ satisfies
	\begin{align}\label{implicit for zeta a}
		K^{\zeta_0}(a,c)=-\frac{\frac{\partial}{\partial x}b_0^a(\zeta_0^{\mc{A}}(a,c),a,c)}{\frac{\partial}{\partial x}b_1^a(\zeta_0^{\mc{A}}(a,c),a,c)}+N(c)\exp\left(\int_{\ul a}^ab_1^a(\zeta^{\mc{A}}(s,c),s,c)ds\right).
	\end{align}
	Moreover, $K^{\zeta_0}(\ul a,\ol c)=0$ and $K^{\zeta_0}(a,c)>0$ for $(a,c)\in[\ul a, \ol a]\times[\ul c,\ol c)$.
\end{mypr}

We extend the definition of $\zeta_0$ to $[\ul a,\ol a]\times[\ul c,\ol c]$ as
\begin{align*}
	\zeta_0(a,\ol c):=(\zeta_0^{\mc{A}}(a,\ol c),\zeta_0^{\mc{C}}(a,\ol c)):=\left(\lim_{d\to\ol c^-}\zeta_0^{\mc{A}}(a,d),\lim_{d\to\ol c^-}\zeta_0^{\mc{C}}(a,d)\right).
\end{align*}
Since $K^{\zeta_0}(\ul a,\ol c)=0$, we have from Proposition \ref{zeta implicit equations}
\begin{align}\label{boundary condition}
	\frac{\partial }{\partial x}b_0^c(\zeta^{\mc{C}}(\ul a,\ol c),\ul a,\ol c)=\frac{\partial }{\partial x}b_0^a(\zeta^{\mc{A}}(\ul a,\ol c),\ul a,\ol c)=0.
\end{align}
Write
\begin{equation}\label{B^c_1}
	\begin{aligned}
		B^c_1(\zeta_0^{\mc{C}}(a,c),a,c)&:=\left[\frac{\frac{\partial b_1^{c}}{\partial x}\cdot\frac{\partial^2b_0^{c}}{\partial x^2}-\frac{\partial b_0^{c}}{\partial x}\cdot\frac{\partial^2 b_1^{c}}{\partial x^2}}{\left(\frac{\partial b_1^{c}}{\partial x}\right)^2}\right](\zeta_0^{\mc{C}}(a,c),a,c)\\
		&\quad-M(a)\exp\left(\int_{\ul c}^cb_1^c(\zeta^{\mc{C}}_0(a,s),a,s)ds\right)\frac{\partial}{\partial \zeta}\left(\int_{\ul c}^cb_1^c(\zeta^{\mc{C}}_0(a,s),a,s)ds\right),
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}\label{B^a_1}
	\begin{aligned}
		B^a_1(\zeta_0^{\mc{A}}(a,c),a,c)&:=\left[\frac{\frac{\partial b_1^{a}}{\partial x}\cdot\frac{\partial^2b_0^{a}}{\partial x^2}-\frac{\partial b_0^{a}}{\partial x}\cdot\frac{\partial^2 b_1^{a}}{\partial x^2}}{\left(\frac{\partial b_1^{a}}{\partial x}\right)^2}\right](\zeta_0^{\mc{A}}(a,c),a,c)\\
		&\quad-N(c)\exp\left(\int_{\ul a}^ab_1^a(\zeta^{\mc{A}}_0(s,c),s,c)ds\right)\frac{\partial}{\partial \zeta}\left(\int_{\ul a}^ab_1^a(\zeta^{\mc{A}}_0(s,c),s,c)ds\right),
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}\label{B^c_0}
	\begin{aligned}
		&B^c_0(\zeta_0^{\mc{C}}(a,c),a,c)\\
		&:=b_0^{c}(\zeta_0^{\mc{C}}(a,c),a,c)+b_1^{c}(\zeta_0^{\mc{C}}(a,c),a,c)\left[-\frac{\frac{\partial}{\partial x}b_0^{c}(\zeta_0^{\mc{C}}(a,c),a,c)}{\frac{\partial}{\partial x}b_1^{c}(\zeta_0^{\mc{C}}(a,c),a,c)}+M(a)\exp\left(\int_{\ul c}^cb_1^c(\zeta^{\mc{C}}(a,s),a,s)ds\right)\right]\\
		&\quad+\left[\frac{\frac{\partial b_1^{c}}{\partial x}\cdot\frac{\partial^2b_0^{c}}{\partial c\partial x}-\frac{\partial b_0^{c}}{\partial x}\cdot\frac{\partial^2 b_1^{c}}{\partial c\partial x}}{\left(\frac{\partial b_1^{c}}{\partial x}\right)^2}\right](\zeta_0^{\mc{C}}(a,c),a,c)-M(a)\exp\left(\int_{\ul c}^cb_1^c(\zeta^{\mc{C}}(a,s),a,s)ds\right)b_1^c(\zeta_0^{\mc{C}}(a,c),a,c),
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}\label{B^a_0}
	\begin{aligned}
		&B^a_0(\zeta_0^{\mc{A}}(a,c),a,c)\\
		&:=b_0^{a}(\zeta_0^{\mc{A}}(a,c),a,c)+b_1^{a}(\zeta_0^{\mc{A}}(a,c),a,c)\left[-\frac{\frac{\partial}{\partial x}b_0^{a}(\zeta_0^{\mc{A}}(a,c),a,c)}{\frac{\partial}{\partial x}b_1^{a}(\zeta_0^{\mc{A}}(a,c),a,c)}+N(c)\exp\left(\int_{\ul a}^ab_1^a(\zeta^{\mc{A}}(s,c),s,c)ds\right)\right]\\
		&\quad+\left[\frac{\frac{\partial b_1^{a}}{\partial x}\cdot\frac{\partial^2b_0^{a}}{\partial a\partial x}-\frac{\partial b_0^{a}}{\partial x}\cdot\frac{\partial^2 b_1^{a}}{\partial a\partial x}}{\left(\frac{\partial b_1^{a}}{\partial x}\right)^2}\right](\zeta_0^{\mc{A}}(a,c),a,c)-N(c)\exp\left(\int_{\ul a}^ab_1^a(\zeta^{\mc{A}}(s,c),s,c)ds\right)b_1^a(\zeta_0^{\mc{A}}(a,c),a,c).
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

The next result states that the functions $\zeta_0^{\mc{A}}$ and $\zeta_0^{\mc{A}}$ are solutions of differential equations.

\begin{myth}\label{theorem on diff equations}
	Suppose the assumptions in Proposition \ref{zeta implicit equations} hold. If $\zeta_0$ defined in \eqref{K zeta_0} is continuous and satisfies
	\small\begin{equation}\label{assumption 5.20}
		\begin{aligned}
			B^c_1(\zeta_0^{\mc{C}}(a,c),a,c)\neq 0\quad\mbox{and}\quad B^a_1(\zeta_0^{\mc{A}}(a,c),a,c)\neq 0
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation}\normalsize
	for $(a,c)\in[\ul a, \ol a]\times[\ul c,\ol c]$, then $\zeta_0^{\mc{C}}$ is infinitely differentiable and it is the unique solution of 
	\begin{align}\label{5.19}
		\frac{\partial}{\partial c}\zeta_0^{\mc{C}}(a,c)=\frac{-B^c_0(\zeta_0^{\mc{C}}(a,c),a,c)}{B^c_1(\zeta_0^{\mc{C}}(a,c),a,c)}
	\end{align}
	with boundary condition
	\begin{align}\label{5.19 boundary condition}
		\frac{\partial }{\partial x}b_0^c(\zeta^{\mc{C}}(\ul a,\ol c),\ul a,\ol c)=0,
	\end{align}
	and $\zeta_0^{\mc{A}}$ is infinitely differentiable and it is the unique solution of 
	\begin{align}\label{5.19.5}
		\frac{\partial}{\partial a}\zeta_0^{\mc{A}}(a,c)=\frac{-B^a_0(\zeta_0^{\mc{A}}(a,c),a,c)}{B^a_1(\zeta_0^{\mc{A}}(a,c),a,c)}
	\end{align}
	with boundary condition
	\begin{align}\label{5.19.5 boundary condition}
		\frac{\partial }{\partial x}b_0^a(\zeta^{\mc{A}}(\ul a, \ol c),\ul a,\ol c)=0.
	\end{align}
\end{myth}

