\section{Conclusion}\label{section:conc}
In this paper, we have extended the existing literature on optimal dividend payout problems with ratcheting by considering the additional irreversible reinsurance constraint. This leads to a multi-dimensional stochastic control problem, where the company must optimize its dividend payout strategy subject to both a ratcheting dividend constraint and an irreversible reinsurance constraint. Through a dynamic programming approach, we have derived the HJB equations for both the finite set case and the closed interval case, and established the convergence of the optimal value functions. For the finite set case, we have presented a method to determine the appropriate threshold levels for the dividend and reinsurance variables. For the closed interval case, we have discussed the curve strategies and obtained an $\epsilon$-optimal strategy under specific conditions. A possible direction of future research is to consider jump-diffusion processes on top of the Brownian motion process to model the reserve level. Drawdown constraints, which allow the levels to go down at a certain percentage of the current level, can also be incorporated on both the dividend and reinsurance levels.

