\begin{abstract}
In this preliminary note we prove that the theory of valued fields equipped with the action of a given finite group has a model companion.
\end{abstract} \maketitle

\section{Introduction}
In this preliminary note we prove that the theory of valued fields equipped with an action of a given finite group $G$ (we call fields \textbf{valued $G$-fields}, see Definition \ref{def: gvf}) has a model companion. This extends one of the results of \cite{GTCF}, where the same was achieved for pure fields, without any valuation around.

Our main result (Theorem \ref{thm: main ake}) states that existentially closed valued $G$-fields admit a characterization in the spirit of Ax-Kochen-Ershov. From this the existence of a model companion follows (see Theorem \ref{thm: mc}).

\subsection*{Conventions} Throughout this note we work inside a big algebraically closed field $\Omega$ equipped with a nontrivial valuation $v$. We also fix a nontrivial \textbf{finite} group $G$. Some of the results are valid also for infinite $G$, but most use finiteness in a crucial way.

Whenever we speak about existentially closed substructures, we work in some fixed language, which should be clear from the context. For valued field the language we opt for is the one-sorted language $\lngval = \mathcal{L}_{\operatorname{rng}}\cup \left\{ \mathcal{O} \right\}$ consisting of the language of rings $\mathcal{L}_{\operatorname{rng}}=\left\{ +,-,\cdot, 0, 1\right\}$ together with an unary predicate $\mathcal{O}$ of the valuation ring. For ordered abelian groups we work in the language $\mathcal{L}_{\operatorname{oag}}=\left\{ +,-,0,\le\right\}$ (in particular, existentially closed ordered abelian groups are precisely the divisible ones).

\subsection*{Acknowledgement} We would like to thank Piotr Kowalski and Franz-Viktor Kuhlmann for numerous valuable discussions.

\section{Preliminaries}

\subsection{\texorpdfstring{$G$}{G}-fields}
\begin{definition}
A \textbf{$G$-field} is a field $K$ equipped with an action of $G$ by field automorphisms. A $G$-field is called \textbf{strict}, if the action of $G$ is faithful. We call the fixed field $K^G$ the \textbf{field of constants} of $K$.
\end{definition}

We will always consider $G$-fields as structures in the language $\mathcal{L}_G=\mathcal{L}_{\operatorname{rng}}\cup\left\{ \sigma_g\colon g\in G \right\}$, where $\sigma_g$ are unary function symbols with an obvious intented intepretation. Sometimes it will be useful to explicitely name the automorphisms yielding the $G$-action on $K$. In such a situation we will write e. g. that ``$\left( K,\sigma\right)$ is a $G$-field'', where $\sigma = \left( \sigma_g\colon g\in G \right)$ is tuple in which $\sigma_g\colon K\to K$ denotes the action of the element $g\in G$ on $K$.

The following easy fact will be used throughout this note.
\begin{lemma}\label{lemma: G compositum}
Let $K$ be a strict $G$-field and let $C'$ be an extension of $C=K^G$ linearly disjoint from $K$ over $C$. Then the action of $G$ has a unique extension to $KC'$ for which the field of constants is $C'$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Uniqueness is evident, so let us prove existence. By linear disjointness we have that the compositum $KC'$ is isomorphic (as a $C$-algebra) to the tensor product $K\otimes_C C'$. The group $G$ naturally acts on this tensor product and hence on $KC'$; explicitely, we have
$$\widetilde{\s}_g\left( \sum_{i=1}^n x_i\otimes c_i \right) = \sum_{i=1}^n \s_g\left( x_i\right)\otimes c_i,$$
for $g\in G$, $x_1,\dotsc, x_n\in K$, $c_1,\dotsc,c_n\in C'$. It is easy to check that the field of constants of this action is precisely $C'$.
\end{proof}
\begin{lemma}\label{lemma: perfect constants}
Let $L$ be a $G$-field of characteristic $p>0$ and assume that every element of $L^G$ has a $p$th root in $L$. Then $L^G$ and $L$ are perfect.   
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Let $a\in L^G$ and take $b\in L$ such that $b^p = a$. Then, for any $g\in G$ we have that 
$$\sigma_g\left( b \right) ^p = \sigma_g\left( b^p \right) = \sigma_g\left( a \right) = a = b^p,$$
thus $\sigma_g\left( b \right) = b$. Therefore, $b\in L^G$ and hence $L^G$ is perfect. Since $L^G\subseteq L$ is algebraic, the field $L$ is also perfect.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lemma: ec red to c pure}
Let $K\subseteq K'$ be an extension of strict $G$-fields with fields of constants $C$ and $C'$ respectively. Then $K$ is existentially closed in $K'$ as a $G$-field if and only if $C$ is existentially closed in $C'$ as a pure field.  
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The ``only if'' part is immediate, so let us focus on the ``if'' part. The point is that the $G$-field $K$ is quantifier-free definable (with parameters) in the pure field $C$. To see this note that $K\cong C^{\left|G\right|}$ as $C$-vector spaces and the multiplication on $K$ is coded under this isomorphism by a set of $\left| G \right|$ matrixes with coefficients in $C$. Moreover, the formulas yielding the intepretation of $K$ in $C$ yield also an intepretation of $K'$ in $C'$, hence existential closedness passes from $C\subseteq C'$ to $K\subseteq K'$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Valued fields}

Let $\Kv$ be a valued field. We denote the value group of $\Kv$ by $vK$, the residue field by $Kv$ and the valuation ring by $\mathcal{O}_K$.

Recall that for a finite extension $C\subseteq K$ of valued fields we have the so-called \textbf{fundamental inequality}
$$\left[ K : C \right] \ge \left( vK: vC \right) \cdot [ Kv: Cv ]$$
A finite extension $C\subseteq K$ of valued fields is called \textbf{defectless} if the above inequality is in fact an equality. On the other extreme are the \textbf{immediate extensions}, that is extensions $C\subseteq C'$ for which $vC=vC'$ and $Cv=C'v$. A valued field $C$ is is called \textbf{algebraically maximal} if it has no proper algebraic immediate extension.
\begin{fact}[Lemma 2.4 in \cite{Kuhlmann}]\label{fact: immediate and defectless are ld}
Let $C$ be a valued field and $C\subseteq C'$ be an immediate
extension. If $C\subseteq K$ is a finite defectless extension admitting a unique extension of the valuation, then the same holds for $C'\subseteq KC'$ and $K$ is linearly disjoint from $C'$ over $C$.
\end{fact}
Crucial for our purposes is the notion of tame valued fields, introduced in \cite{tame}. 
\begin{definition}
A valued field $\Kv$ is called \textbf{tame} if it is algebraically maximal, $Kv$ is perfect and $vK$ is $p$-divisible, where $p$ is the characteristic exponent of $Kv$.
\end{definition}
The above is not the original definition of tame fields, but rather an equivalent one, which is part of the content of Theorem 3.2 in \cite{tame}. Tame fields are important because they form in a sense the largest class of valued fields for which one can hope for an Ax-Kochen-Ershov principle:
\begin{fact}[Theorem 1.4 in \cite{tame}]\label{fact: ake for tame}
Assume $\Kv$ is a tame field and $\Kv\subseteq \left( L, v \right)$ is an extension of valued fields. If $Kv$ is existentially closed in $Lv$ (as a pure field) and $vK$ is existentially closed in $vL$ (as an ordered abelian group), then $K$ is existentially closed in $L$ (as a valued field).
\end{fact}

\section{Valued \texorpdfstring{$G$}{G}-fields}
\subsection{Generalities}
\begin{definition}\label{def: gvf}
A \textbf{valued $G$-field} is a valued field $\left( K, v \right)$ together with an action of $G$ by valued field automorphisms, i. e. field automorphisms preserving the valuation ring $\mathcal{O}_K$. A valued $G$-field is called \textbf{residue-strict}, if the action of $G$ on $Kv$ is faithful (in particular, $K$ is strict as a $G$-field).
\end{definition}

We will consider valued $G$-fields as structures in the language $\lng = \mathcal{L}_G\cup \left\{ \mathcal{O} \right\}$ 

\begin{remark}
Let $\Kvs$ be a valued $G$-field. The action of $G$ on $K$ induces an action on the residue field $Kv$ by field automorphisms and on the value group $vK$ by order-preserving group automorphisms. The action of $G$ on $vK$ is by force trivial, due to the finiteness of $G$. Indeed, assume $g\in G$ has order $n$ and let $\gamma\in vK$ be such that $\s_g\left( \gamma \right) \neq \gamma$, say $\s_g\left( \gamma \right) > \gamma$. Since $\s_g$ is order-preserving we have that
$$\gamma < \s_g\left( \gamma \right) < \s_g^2\left( \gamma \right)<\dotsc < \s_g^n\left( \gamma \right)=\gamma,$$
which is a contradiction.
\end{remark}
We will denote by $\left( Kv, \overline{\s}\right)$ the $G$-field structure on the residue field of $K$.
\begin{lemma}\label{lemma: unique}
If $\Kvs$ is a valued $G$-field with field of constants $C$, then valuation $v|_C$ on $C$ extends uniquely to a valuation on $K$.    
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
By passing to a quotient of $G$ we may assume that $\Kvs$ is strict. Since the extension $C\subseteq K$ is Galois, any two extensions of $v|_C$ to $\operatorname{Gal}\left( K/C \right)$-conjugate, in particular any extension is conjugate to $v$. But since $\Kvs$ is a valued $G$-field, the valuation $v$ is invariant under the action of $G\cong \operatorname{Gal}\left(K/C\right)$, hence $v$ is the unique extension of $v|_C$ to $K$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lemma: ext to residue action faithful}
Every valued $G$-field $\Kvs$ admits a valued $G$-field extension $L$ which is residue-strict.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Consider the function field $L = K\left( X_g\colon g\in G\right)$ with its natural $G$-action. By basic valuation theory (see e. g. \cite[Corollary 2.2.2]{engler}), there is a unique extension $w$ of $v$ to $L$ such that for all $g\in G$:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $w\left( X_g \right) = 0$,
    \item $\operatorname{res}\left( X_g \right)$ is transcendental over $Kv$.
\end{enumerate}
It is easy to check that the action of $G$ on $\left( L, w \right)$ turns $L$ into a valued $G$-field extension of $K$ and that the residue action of $G$ on $L$ is faithful.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lemma: vK=vC, k^G=c}
Let $\Kvs$ be a residue-strict valued $G$-field. Let $C$ be the field of constants of $K$. Then the extension $C\subseteq K$ is defectless, $\left( Kv \right)^G = Cv$ and $vC = vK$. 
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
By the fundamental inequality for extensions of valued fields we have
$$\left[ K : C \right] \ge \left( vK: vC \right) \cdot [ Kv: Cv ].$$
Certainly $Cv \subseteq \left( Kv \right)^G$, thus $\left[ Kv: Cv \right] \ge [ Kv: \left( Kv \right)^G ]$. Since $G$ acts faithfully on $Kv$ it does so also on $K$, thus $\left[ K: C \right] = \left[ Kv: \left( Kv \right)^G \right] = | G |
$. Thus
$$| G | \ge \left( vK: vC \right) \cdot  \left[ Kv: Cv \right]  = \left( vK: vC \right) \cdot | G | \ge | G|,$$
hence all the inequalities are equalities, in particular
$$\left[ Kv: Cv \right]  = \left[ Kv: \left( Kv \right)^G \right]\quad\mbox{ and }\quad\left( vK: vC \right) = 1, $$
thus $\left( Kv \right)^G = Cv$, $vC = vK$ and the extensions $C\subseteq K$ is defectless.
\end{proof}

The results below is a valuative counterpart of Lemma \ref{lemma: G compositum}.
\begin{lemma}\label{lemma: GVF compositum}
Let $K$ be a strict valued $G$-field and let $C'$ be an valued field extension of $C=K^G$ which is linearly disjoint from $K$ over $C$. Assume that the valuation on $C'$ extends uniquely to a valuation on $KC'$. Then the action of $G$ has a unique extension to $KC'$ for which the field of constants is $C'$.    
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma \ref{lemma: G compositum} we get a unique $G$-field structure on $KC'$. Since the valuation on $C'$ extends uniquely to a valuation on $w$ on $KC'$, this valuation $w$ has to be $\operatorname{Gal}\left( KC'/C'\right)$-invariant. But $\operatorname{Gal}\left( KC'/C'\right)\cong \operatorname{Gal}\left( K/C\right) \cong G$ by linear disjointness, therefore $\left( KC'\right)$ with the induced action of $G$ is a valued $G$-field extension of $K$ with the desired properties.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lemma: henselian}
Every valued $G$-field $(K,v,\s)$ admits an extension $(K',v',\s')$, such that $(K'^G,v')$ is henselian (so in particular, $(K',v')$ is henselian too). 
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Let $C^h$ be the henselianization of the field of constants $C=K^G$ of $K$. The extension $C\subseteq C^h$ is immediate and by Lemma \ref{lemma: vK=vC, k^G=c} the extension $C\subseteq K$ is defectless, thus by Fact \ref{fact: immediate and defectless are ld} we have that $K$ and $C^h$ are linearly disjoint over $C$, hence by Lemma \ref{lemma: GVF compositum} the compositum $KC^h$ has the structure of a valued $G$-field extension of $K$ with fields of constants $C^h$. Hence $K'=KC^h$ has all the desired properties.
\end{proof}
The following is an analogue of Lemma \ref{lemma: ec red to c pure} in the presence of a valuation.
\begin{lemma}\label{lemma: ec red to c}
Let $K\subseteq K'$ be an extension of strict valued $G$-fields with fields of constants $C$ and $C'$ respectively. Then $K$ is an existentially closed in $L$ as a valued $G$-field if and only if $C$ is existentially closed in $C'$ as a valued field.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The ``only if'' part is straightforward, so let us focus on the reverse direction. For the ``if'' part we will argue as in the proof of Lemma \ref{lemma: ec red to c pure}. We already know that $K$ as a $G$-field is quantifier-free definable in $C$. Since the valuation on $C$ extends uniquely to $K$ by Lemma \ref{lemma: unique}, we get that the integral closure of $\mathcal{O}_C$ in $K$ is exactly $\mathcal{O}_K$, which gives an existential definition of the valuation on $K$ in the structure $\left( C,v \right)$. So we get an existentially-definable intepretation of the valued $G$-field $K$ in the valued field $C$ and the same formulas yield and interpretation of $K'$ in $C'$. Thus as in the proof of Lemma \ref{lemma: unique} existential closedness passes from $C\subseteq C'$ to $K\subseteq K'$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Existentially closed valued \texorpdfstring{$G$}{G}-fields} Throughout this subsection we assume that $\left( K, v,\s\right)$ is an existentially closed valued $G$-field.

\begin{lemma}\label{lemma: residue action faithful}
$\Kvs$ is residue-strict.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
For a fixed element $g\in G$ the property $\overline{\s}_g\neq \operatorname{id}_{Kv}$ is expressible by an existential $\lng$-sentence. Thus, since $\Kvs$ is existentially closed, it is enough to construct a valued $G$-field extension of $K$ on which the residue action in faithful, which was done in Lemma \ref{lemma: ext to residue action faithful}.
\end{proof}
Immediately from Lemma \ref{lemma: vK=vC, k^G=c} and Lemma \ref{lemma: residue action faithful} we get the following.
\begin{corollary}\label{cor: vK=vC, k^G=c}
The extension $C\subseteq K$ is defectless, $\left( Kv \right)^G = Cv$ and $vC = vK$. 
\end{corollary}

\begin{lemma}\label{lemma: qth powers}
Let $q$ be a prime number. Then every element of $C$ (resp. $Cv$) is has a $q$th root in $K$ (resp. $Kv$).
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
It is enough to prove the statement for $C$ and $K$. Assume $a\in C$ is not a $q$th power in $K$ and let $b$ be any $q$th root of $a$ inside $\Omega$. It is known (and easy to check) that the polynomial $X^q-a$ is irreducible over $C$, thus the extension $C\subseteq C\left( b \right)$ has degree $q$. Consider the field $L= C\left( b\right)\cap K$. Since $C\subseteq L\subseteq C\left( b \right)$ and $\left[ C\left( b \right) : C \right] = q$ is prime we have that either $L=C$ or $L=C\left(b\right)$. In the latter case $b\in L\subseteq K$, so $a$ is a $q$th power in $K$, contrary to the assumption. Thus $L= C$ and since $C\subseteq K$ is a Galois extension we have that $K$ and $C\left( b \right)$ are linearly disjoint over $C$. Thus by Lemma \ref{lemma: GVF compositum} the compositum $K.C(b)=K(b)$ becomes a valued $G$-field extension of $K$. But $K\subseteq K(b)$ is a proper algebraic extension, which contradicts the fact that $K$ is existentially closed as a valued $G$-field.
\end{proof}

Immediately from Lemma \ref{lemma: perfect constants} and Lemma \ref{lemma: qth powers} we get the following.
\begin{corollary}\label{cor: perfect}
The fields $K, C, Kv, Cv$ are perfect.
\end{corollary}

\begin{lemma}\label{lemma: divisible}
The group $vC=vK$ is divisible.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Pick any $a\in C$ and any prime number $q$. By Lemma \ref{lemma: qth powers} we have that there is some $b\in K$ such that $b^q = a$ and hence $v\left( a \right) =qv\left( b \right)\in q\cdot vK$, but by Corollary \ref{cor: vK=vC, k^G=c} we have $vK = vC$, so $v\left( a \right)$ is $q$-divisible in $vC$. Hence $vC$ is a divisible group.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lemma: tame}
The valued fields $\Cv$ and $\Kv$ are tame.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
By \cite[Lemma 2.13]{tame} an algebraic extension of a tame fields is again a tame field, thus it is enough to show that $\Cv$ is tame.
By Corollary \ref{cor: perfect} and Lemma \ref{lemma: divisible} the only thing left to prove is that $\Cv$ is algebraically maximal. Let $\Cv\subseteq \left( C', v\right)$ be an algebraic immediate extension. We aim to show that $C'=C$. By Fact \ref{fact: immediate and defectless are ld} and Corollary \ref{cor: vK=vC, k^G=c} we have that $K$ and $C'$ are linearly disjoint over $C$, so by Lemma \ref{lemma: GVF compositum} and Fact \ref{fact: immediate and defectless are ld} again the field $KC'$ becomes naturally a valued $G$-field extension of $K$. Since $K$ is existentially closed as a valued $G$-field and $KC'$ is algebraic over $K$ we have that $KC'=K$. Thus $C'=C$ as desired.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lemma: kv ec}
The residue field $Kv$ is existentially closed as an $G$-field.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Let $Kv\subseteq k$ be a $G$-field extension. Pick valued field extensions $K\subseteq K'$ and $C\subseteq C'\subseteq K'$ such that $k=K'v$, $k^G=C'v$ and $vK'=vK$. The by Lemma \ref{lemma: G compositum} we get that $K'$ has the structure of a valued $G$-field extension of $K$ and it follows that $Kv$ is existentially closed as a $G$-field in $k=K'v$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}
The field $K$ is not PAC. In particular, the $G$-field $\left( K,\s \right)$ is not existentially closed.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Assume $K$ is PAC. By the Frey–Prestel theorem (see \cite[Corollary 11.5.5]{fields}) we have that the henselianization of $\left( K, v \right)$ is the separable closure of $K$ and the residue field $Kv$ is separably closed. But $G$ acts faithfully on $Kv$, so by the Artin-Schreirer theorem we have that $G = C_2$ is the cyclic group of order two, $Kv$ is algebraically closed of characteristic zero and the fixed field $Cv$ is real closed. But by \cite[Example 3.5]{GTCF} an existentially closed $C_2$-field cannot be algebraically closed. The ``In particular'' part follows, since existentially closed $G$-fields are PAC by \cite[Theorem 3.29]{GTCF}.
\end{proof}

\section{The model companion}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm: main ake}
Let $(K, v, \s)$ be a valued $G$-field, where $G$ is a finite nontrivial group, with field of constants $C$. Then $(K, v, \s)$ is existentially closed if and only if
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $C$ is tame,
    \item $vK$ is existentially closed as an ordered abelian group,
    \item $Kv$ is existentially closed as a $G$-field.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The ``only if'' part follows directly from Lemmas \ref{lemma: divisible}, \ref{lemma: tame} and \ref{lemma: kv ec}. For the other direction, let us assume that $K$ satisfies $(1)$, $(2)$ and $(3)$ and let $K\subseteq K'$ be an extension of valued $G$-fields. We want to show that $K$ is existentially closed in $K'$ as a valued $G$-field. Denote by $C'$ the field of constants of $K'$. By Lemma \ref{lemma: ec red to c} it is enough to check that $C$ is existentially closed in $C'$ as a valued field. For this we will use Fact \ref{fact: ake for tame}.

Note that by $(3)$ the $G$-field $Kv$ is strict (so a fortiori $Lv$ is also strict), hence by Lemma \ref{lemma: vK=vC, k^G=c} we get that $vC=vK$, therefore by $(2)$ we have that $vC$ is existentially closed in $vC'$ (as an ordered abelian group). On the other hand, by Lemma \ref{lemma: ec red to c pure} and $(3)$ we have that $\left( Kv \right)^G$ is existentially closed in $\left( K'v \right)^G$ (as a pure field). But again by Lemma \ref{lemma: vK=vC, k^G=c} we have that $\left( Kv \right)^G=Cv$ and $\left( K'v \right)^G=C'v$, thus $Cv$ is existentially closed in $C'v$. Hence by Fact \ref{fact: ake for tame} we get that $C$ is existentially closed in $C'$ as a valued field, as desired.
\end{proof}

By Subsection 7.1 of \cite{tame} we have that the class of tame fields is elementary. Let $\gtcvf$ be the $\lng$-theory expressing the following properties:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $\Cv$ is tame,
    \item $\left( Kv, \overline{\sigma}\right)$ is a model of $G$-$\operatorname{TCF}$.
    \item $vK$ is divisible.
\end{enumerate}
Using this definition, Theorem \ref{thm: main ake} translates into the following.
\begin{theorem}\label{thm: mc}
The theory $G$-$\operatorname{TCVF}$ is the model companion of the theory of valued $G$-fields in the language $\lng$.
\end{theorem}

