\section{Beating a stochastic benchmark}\label{sec:bench}
We would also like to mention that the general setting we discussed here may be specified to a classical benchmark problem where we have only one agent who tries to beat a (stochastic) benchmark with at least a certain probability. We explain this with a simple specific example: Suppose we have a Black Scholes market with one riskless bond with zero interest and one stock with price process
\begin{align*}
\mathrm{d}S_t=&S_t\Big(\mu\,
\mathrm{d}t+\sigma\,\mathrm{d}W_t\Big),
\end{align*}
where $S_0=1$, $\sigma> 0$ and $\mu \in\R$ are given parameters with $\sigma^2 > \mu$. W.l.o.g.\ we assume that $S_0=1.$
Thus we have 
\begin{align*}
S_t=&\exp\Big(\Big(\mu-\frac12 \sigma^2\Big)t +\sigma W_t\Big),\\
Z_t=& \exp\Big(-\frac12 \frac{\mu^2}{\sigma^2}t -\frac{\mu}{\sigma}  W_t\Big)
\end{align*}
where $(Z_t)$ is again the state price density. We consider the problem
$$(P_B) \left\{ \begin{array}{l}
\EE \ln (X) \to \max\\
\PP(X \ge  \beta S_T)\ge \alpha,\\
\EE[Z_TX]=x_0,\\
X \mbox{ is } \mathcal{F}-\mbox{measurable.}\\
\end{array}\right.$$
Thus, we maximize the logarithmic utility of terminal wealth under the constraint that we beat at least with probability $\alpha$ the benchmark $\beta S_T.$ The initial wealth is $x_0.$ We can use Lemma~\ref{lem:bound2} to derive an explicit solution. First, the optimal set where the constraint will be satisfied is given by 
\begin{align}
    M_{\lambda_\alpha} := \Big\{ \beta S_T \le \frac{\lambda_\alpha}{Z_T}\Big\} \mbox{ where } \lambda_\alpha \mbox{ is such that } \PP(M_{\lambda_\alpha})=\alpha.
\end{align}
In our application this set can be characterized further. Note that 
\begin{align}
    S_TZ_T = (Z_T)^{1-\frac{\sigma^2}{\mu}} \exp\Big(\frac12 (\mu-\sigma^2)T\Big).
\end{align}
Thus, when we define
\begin{align}
    \kappa(\lambda) := \Big(\frac{\lambda}{\beta}\Big)^{\frac{\mu}{\mu-\sigma^2}}\exp\Big(-\frac{1}{2}\mu T \Big), %\Big[ \frac{\lambda}{\beta} \exp\big(-\frac12 (\mu-\sigma^2)T\big) \Big]^{\frac{\mu}{\mu-\sigma^2}}
\end{align}
we can express $ M_{\lambda_\alpha}$ as $ M_{\lambda_\alpha} =\{Z_T \ge  \kappa(\lambda_\alpha) \}.$ Thus, in total we obtain with  Lemma \ref{lem:bound2} that the optimal terminal wealth is $X^* =x_0/Z_T$ if $x_0\ge \lambda_\alpha$. In the case $x_0< \lambda_\alpha$, we obtain
$$ X^* =  \left\{ \begin{array}{cl}
\lambda/Z_T, & \mbox{ if } Z_T \le \kappa(\lambda_\alpha) \mbox{ or } Z_T > \kappa(\lambda)\\
\beta S_T,  & \mbox{ if } Z_T \in ( \kappa(\lambda_\alpha),  \kappa(\lambda)  ]
\end{array}\right.$$
where $\lambda > \lambda_\alpha$ is such that $\EE[X^*Z_T]=x_0.$ This problem is related to a number of similar questions which have been treated in the literature before such as maximizing the probability of beating a benchmark or minimizing shortfall \citep[see e.g.][]{browne1999reaching,korn2014portfolio, follmer2016stochastic}.

