\noindent \textbf{Why video?} The video/movie domain, characterized by inherently rich multimodal information—combining visual, audio, and textual elements—represents an ideal yet challenging environment for testing multimodal RAG-based recommender systems. Video recommender systems must not only handle complex and sparse metadata but also effectively fuse multimodal signals extracted from diverse representations such as movie trailers, audio tracks, and textual plot synopses. However, the research community currently lacks comprehensive, openly accessible datasets specifically designed to evaluate multimodal RAG approaches for within realistic and demanding video recommendation scenarios.

\textbf{Gap in Existing Datasets.} Despite notable advances in multimodal recommendation, current datasets often fall short for rigorous retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) (see Table~\ref{tbl:sample_table}). Key shortcomings include:  
(i) \textit{Minimal support for RAG}—most datasets (\textit{e.g.,} MMTF-14K~\cite{mmtf14k}, MicroLens~\cite{microlens}) focus on side-information-based collaborative filtering, overlooking external multimodal retrieval for generation.  
(ii) \textit{Overly simplistic fusion}—datasets like Ducho~\cite{ducho2} or MMRec~\cite{mmrec} emphasize basic fusion (\textit{e.g.,} parallel concatenation) instead of alignment (\textit{e.g.,} CCA) or robust retrieval.  
(iii) \textit{Sparse metadata handling}—many neglect the common reality of incomplete item descriptions in domains like fashion or travel.  
(iv) \textit{Inadequate cold-start coverage}—few explore early-stage recommendations when textual data is limited, hindering understanding of multimodal signals.

\noindent
\textbf{\RAGBench\footnote{\url{https://anonymous.4open.science/r/RAG-VisualRec-2866/}} at a glance.}  
To close these gaps, we present \RAGBench, the first \emph{open, auditable, multimodal benchmark and toolkit} tailored to \textit{multimodal} RAG for video recommendation.  Rather than proposing yet another algorithm, \RAGBench \, aims to deliver the missing research infrastructure:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.1em]
    \item \textbf{Dataset layer.} 9,724 MovieLens titles aligned with 3,751 trailers, each enriched by eight low‑level audiovisual descriptors and GPT‑4o‑generated plot synopses with chain‑of‑thought rationales. % Every artefact is timestamped and SHA‑256‑hashed for verifiable reproducibility. 
    \item \textbf{Toolkit layer.} A typed, open‑source library offering early‑ and mid‑fusion operators (concat, PCA, CCA),  textual augmentation, strong \ac{CF} baselines, and a plug‑and‑play RAG loop with optional LLM re‑ranking.
    \item \textbf{Benchmark harness.} Declarative configurations and pipeline codes regenerate \emph{all} metrics—12 accuracy and 5 beyond‑accuracy indicators (coverage, novelty, long‑tail share, cold‑start lift, diversity)—with a single command, aligned with ACM reproducibility guidelines. In this paper, due to space constraints, we report results on a shorter set of metrics: NDCG, recall, novelty, TailFrac, and catalog coverage.
\end{itemize}

