\section{Evidence for the Vanishing of $\mu$} The quantity $\mu$ appearing in (\ref{eq:widemoment2}) can be viewed as a horizontal analogue of the classical $\mu$-invariant (see \cite[Chapter 12]{Washington}). In this section we give evidence to support the conjectural vanishing of $\mu$ postulated in Remark \ref{rem:Kurihara}. Our main evidence toward this conjecture is given by Corollary \ref{cor:kolynonvan}, which shows that $\mu = 0$ under certain assumptions.

\subsection{Kato--Kolyvagin Derivatives}\label{sec:firstdigit}
Consider a sequence of finite abelian groups $(G_n)_{n\in \N}$ such that each $G_n$ admits $\Z/p$ as a quotient. Assume furthermore that the first $r$ groups $G_1,\ldots, G_r$ are cyclic with $G_i=\langle g_i\rangle$ and $h_i=|G_i|$. For the rest of this section, let $R$ be the ring of integers of a finite extension of $\Q_p$. 

For an integer $r\geq 0$, we define the \emph{$r^\mathrm{th}$ Kato--Kolyvagin derivative} to be the element
\begin{align}\label{eq:Dr}
 D^r:=\sum_{n_1=1}^{h_1}\cdots \sum_{n_r=1}^{h_r} \left(\prod_{i=1}^r n_i\right)[(g_1^{n_1},\ldots, g_r^{n_r})] \in R[G_1\times \cdots \times G_r].  
 \end{align}
These correspond to Kolyvagin derivatives considered in \cite{WZhang} in the setting of elliptic curves over $\mathbb{Q}$ considered over imaginary quadratic fields. When $r=0$ this is understood to be the element $D^0=[1]\in R[\langle 1\rangle]$. When $r = 1$ we will write $D^r = D$ for brevity. Now given an element of the group algebra $\nu\in R[G_1\times \cdots \times G_r]$ we get, by the duality $\mathrm{Hom}_R(R[G_1 \times \cdots \times G_r],R) \cong R[G_1 \times \cdots \times G_r]$, an associated function which we denote by
 $$\varphi_\nu: G_1\times \cdots \times G_r \rightarrow R. $$
 We define the \emph{$r$-th Kato--Kolyvagin derivative of} $\nu$ to be
 $$D^r\nu:=D^r(\varphi_\nu)=\nu(\varphi_{D^r})\in R.$$
For simplicity of exposition, we will henceforth only consider the case $r = 1$, although many of the results in the sequel admit generalizations to general $r$.


