$$\nu(\chi) = f_{\nu,1}(\zeta-1) = h(\zeta-1) + f_{\nu,1}(0) = h(\zeta-1) + \nu(1).$$
If both $v_p(\nu(\chi)) > \frac{1}{p-1}$ and $v_p(\nu(1)) > \frac{1}{p-1}$ then the previous two displayed equalities yield a contradiction. Thus the existence of a $\chi_1 : G_1 \rightarrow \C_p^{\times}$ of order dividing $p$ and satisfying (\ref{eq:chir}) follows.


%$v_p(\nu(\chi)) = \frac{1}{p-1}$. Since all $\chi : G \rightarrow \C_p$ of exact order $p$ are given by $\chi(g_1) = \zeta$ for some $\zeta \in \mu_p^\ast$, this implies (\ref{eq:chir}). Now note that 
%$$f_{\eta,1}(\zeta-1)$$



