\subsection{Results on Topic Modeling}
Topic modeling is a statistical method used to uncover hidden themes (topics) within a large collection of documents. It identifies groups of words that frequently appear together, allowing for the discovery of underlying topics without prior labeling, useful for summarizing and understanding large text datasets. For our application, topic modeling helps us determine whether the responses to the questions are relevant to the topic of statistical methods. If topics are unrelated to statistical methods, or unrelated to the questions being asked, then we can conclude that ChatGPT might be at worst, hallucinating, or, at the least, providing responses that are irrelevant to the question being asked. The seeds of topic modeling were planted mid-way through the last century \cite{ballester2022}. Since that time, other methods such as non-negative matrix factorization (NMF) \cite{leeseung}, latent Dirichlet allocation (LDA) \cite{blei2003}, and Word2Vec \cite{ballester2022} have been developed. For our investigation, we have chosen LDA due to its relative simplicity and the ready availability of R code. 

For our application, a ``corpus'' refers to the text answers to the graduate exam questions for one version of the generative AI platforms examined. Each corpus in our application contains 16 documents. We perform LDA on each corpus separately and examine the results to determine whether the same topics are discovered within each corpus. Because we are unsure about the number of topics in our corpus, we use unsupervised LDA with $k=5$ as a starting point. $k=5$ was used because each corpus is small, consisting of only 16 documents. The choice of $k$ has been investigated in several papers, for example, \cite{ballester2022}, \cite{meaney2019}, and \cite{hecking2018}. Most investigations of the choice of $k$ on the robustness of LDA have used corpora with hundreds or thousands of documents \cite{hecking2018}. In general, the larger the value of $k$, the more granular the results, and the more likely that nonsensical topics will be ``discovered'' by the algorithm. We began with  $k = 5$, but discovered that our documents tended to map to only 3 topics; therefore, we settled with $k=3$. A small value of $k$ is appropriate for small corpora. Table \ref{tab:topics} shows the topics and their associated terms for the three generative AI models.

