\section*{Plain Language Summary}

The Earth is continuously bombarded by charged particles originating from the sun, other objects in our galaxy, and from distant galaxies further out in the cosmos. These particles, and those produced by their collisions with the atmosphere, can become trapped in the earth's magnetic field, and form two regions surrounding the earth known as the Van Allen radiation belts. These regions can support a type of electromagnetic wave known as chorus waves, named for their resemblance to birds chirping at dawn when their radio frequencies are played as audio. Interactions between chorus waves and electrons can strongly amplify the waves and accelerate electrons to high energies, influencing space weather and posing dangers to important satellites and probes transiting the region. Recent efforts to understand the mechanism behind chorus wave amplification have drawn upon parallels to free-electron lasers, laboratory devices that generate intense laser beams with tunable frequencies. This approach is known as the free-electron laser model of chorus amplification. While previous work focused only on the initial stages of wave amplification, this study explores the later stages, revealing crucial facts about the long-term behavior of the system and new agreements between the model and observational data from satellites. This study also proposes that when there are multiple chorus waves, each with different frequencies, their behavior can be understood using one of the most well-studied nonlinear equations in physics - the Ginzburg-Landau equation. One of the interesting predictions of this equation is the existence of solitary wave behavior. This phenomenon, first noticed in the context of water waves propagating in a canal, is characterized by isolated waves propagating for surprising distances without changing their shape or speed. These findings offer new perspectives on chorus waves, improve our understanding of space weather in the Van Allen radiation belts, and could inform strategies to protect space-based technologies. 

