We proceed as follows. For every value of the parameters $\gamma$ and $\beta$, we compute the expected dissipation $\bar D$ for a symmetric and a symmetry-broken configuration
\begin{align}
    \text{symmetric:} \; & k_1 = k_2 = \cdots = k_{2N} = (2N)^{-\frac{1}{\gamma}} \kappa \nonumber \\ 
    \text{symmetry-broken:} \; & k_{2N} = 0.\label{eq:symm-symm-broken-capacities}
\end{align}
The computation can be carried out partly analytically as outlined in appendix \ref{sec:dissipation-as}. Furthermore, we use the numerical scheme described in Sec.~\ref{sec:numerics} with different initial guesses to validate the semi-analytic results. We use (i) the symmetric and symmetry-broken configurations to check whether these state are local minima and (ii) random initial guesses to exclude that a different global minimum exists.

The results of our analysis are shown in  (Fig.~\ref{fig:phase_diagram_ring}). We plot the minimum edge capacity $\min_{e \in \EE} k_e$ to distinguish between the symmetry-broken phase with $\min_{e \in \EE} k_e = 0$ and the symmetric phase with $\min_{e \in \EE} k_e> 0$. 
For a fixed value of the fluctuation parameter $\beta$, we find a transition from a symmetry-broken phase to a symmetric phase when the scaling exponent $\gamma$ increases. The critical value satisfies $\gamma_c \le 1$, where equality holds only for $\beta = 0$. That is, the phase transition generally occurs for $\gamma_c < 1$ in a system with non-vanishing fluctuations. The transition is always discontinuous as discussed for the elementary examples in Sec.~\ref{sec:elementary-cases}.

A remarkable behavior is observed when we monitor the optimal network as a function of the fluctuation parameter $\beta$. For certain values of $\gamma$ we find a reentrant phase transition from the symmetry-broken phase to the symmetric phase and back to the symmetry-broken phase. Hence, more fluctuations do not always favor more connectivity.

\begin{figure}[tb]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{N2DsDaloglog.pdf}
    \caption{
    Scaling behavior of the expected dissipation in the symmetry-broken state $\bar D_a$ and the symmetric state $\bar D_s$ with the fluctuation strength $\beta$. Results are shown for $N=2$ and $\gamma=0.53$. 
    }
    \label{fig:DsDaScaling}
\end{figure}

The reentrant behavior can be deduced from the scaling behavior of the expected dissipation in the symmetry-broken state $\bar D_a$ and the symmetric state $\bar D_s$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:DsDaScaling}). 
For large values of $\beta$ we find the scaling
\begin{align}
    \bar D_s \sim \beta^{1.61},
    \quad \mbox{and} \quad  
    \bar D_a \sim \beta^{1.40}.
\end{align}
That is the expected dissipation $\bar D_s$ grows faster as $\bar D_a$ such that the symmetry-broken network is optimal.
For banishing fluctuations, the expected dissipation $\bar D_s$ levels off at a value of
\begin{align}
    \bar D_s(\beta=0)  = 6.86, 
    \quad \mbox{and} \quad  
    \bar D_a (\beta = 0) = 4.44.
\end{align}
such that the symmetry-broken network is also optimal in this parameter region. Only for intermediate values of $\beta$ we find $\bar D_a > \bar D_s$ such that the optimal network is symmetric. 

A qualitative explanation is provided by the structure of the optimal networks sketched in Fig.~
\ref{fig:phase_diagram_ring}. We observe that optimal symmetry-broken networks for small and large $\beta$ differ considerably. 
For small $\beta$, sources and sinks balance locally. That is, every source can supply one adjacent sink up too small residual fluctuations. Hence, the optimal network contains $N$ strong edges linking one source and one sink each.
For large $\beta$, fluctuations between the sources become dominant. Global balancing between the source nodes necessitates a extended backbone of strong edges.
For intermediate values of $\beta$, symmetric networks are favorable even for small values of $\gamma$ because they enable both local and global balancing.


\subsection{Scaling with system size}


\begin{figure}[tb]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{scaling-figure.pdf}
    \caption{Scaling with system size $N$ of the minimal value $\gamma_c$ for which we observe the phase transition between symmetry-broken and symmetric states (top), and the corresponding symmetrical capacity values $k_c$ (bottom). We fit the theoretical values from the top panel to $\gamma_c = c N^{-\alpha}$ using nonlinear least squares. In the bottom panel, we keep the constants from the previous fit.}
    \label{fig:mingammac}
\end{figure}

We now analyze how the critical behavior scales with system size. We consider the smallest value of $\gamma$ for which the transition from the symmetry-broken to symmetric state occurs. This is the cusp point in the the phase diagrams in Figure \ref{fig:phase_diagram_ring}, i.e. the smallest $\gamma$ for which $\min_{e \in \EE} k_e > 0$. We can find $\gamma_c$ theoretically, since the average dissipation for the symmetric and symmetry-broken states, $\bar D_s$ and $\bar D_a$ can be obtained semi-analytically, as described in Appendix \ref{sec:dissipation-as}. Then we can numerically determine the smallest $\gamma$ value for which the curves $\bar D_s(\beta)$ and $\bar D_a(\beta)$ cross. Repeating this procedure for a range of $N \in [2, 9]$ values reveals a power law dependence of the critical value on the system size
\begin{align*}
    \gamma_c \sim c N^{-\alpha}
\end{align*}
as shown in the top panel of Fig.~\ref{fig:mingammac}. We apply the the nonlinear least squares method to obtain the scaling exponent $\alpha$ and constant factor $c$,
\begin{align*}
\alpha &= 1.092 \pm 0.004 \\
c &= 0.938 \pm 0.004.
\end{align*}











\begin{figure*}[tb]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=.45\linewidth]{renewables_fluctuating_generation.pdf}
    \includegraphics[width=.45\linewidth]{renewables_mink_zeta.pdf}
