\subsection{Correlation with IGDS9-SF and Game Time}
SCRs and AUC were tested using the ranked Spearman correlation test, and results show that none of the parameters presents a statistically significant correlation when tested with the IGDS9-SF scores (\textit{SCRs}, \textit{p} = .88, \textit{r} = -.05; \textit{AUC}: \textit{p} = .42, \textit{r} = -.27) or the weekly hours spent gaming (\textit{SCRs}, \textit{p} = .87, \textit{r} = .06; \textit{AUC}: \textit{p} = .99, \textit{r} = .00). To test the hyposensitivity hypothesis based on~\cite{b24} results, the same test was executed between the SCRs ranges and both the IGDS9-SF scores and the weekly hours spent gaming. When testing this correlation, we tested the SCR amplitude ranges across all events (\textit{IGDS9-SF}: \textit{p} = .20, \textit{r} = -.41; \textit{Playtime}: \textit{p} = .75, \textit{r} = -.11), across gP events (\textit{IGDS9-SF}: \textit{p} = .10, \textit{r} = -.52; \textit{Playtime}: \textit{p} = .51, \textit{r} = -.22), and across lB events (\textit{IGDS9-SF}: \textit{p} = .99, \textit{r} = .00; \textit{Playtime}: \textit{p} = .55, \textit{r} = -.20). The results show that none of the tested ranges presents a statistically significant correlation with the IGDS9-SF scores or the weekly hours spent gaming. 
